window.PSAT_DATA = [{"id": "reading-writing-module-1", "label": "Module 1", "exams": [[{"id": "RW1-M1-001", "prompt": "In 2021, a group of students started a small garden behind their school. At first, only a few vegetables grew well, but by the next spring, the students had learned which plants needed the most sun and how often each bed should be watered. Their project became more successful because they were willing to revise their methods.\nAs used in the text, “revise” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "record", "B": "change", "C": "announce", "D": "defend"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-002", "prompt": "Many people think of libraries mainly as places to borrow books. However, public libraries often provide much more. Some offer language classes, homework help, free computer access, and community events. These services can make libraries important centers of learning and connection.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Public libraries provide many services beyond lending books.", "B": "Public libraries should replace schools as learning centers.", "C": "Most people visit libraries only when they need computers.", "D": "Libraries are less useful today than they were in the past."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe collection of old maps <u>show</u> how the city changed over time.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "shows", "C": "have shown", "D": "are showing"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-004", "prompt": "Mia wanted to learn the cello, but she did not own an instrument. ______, her music teacher arranged for her to borrow one from the school.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "For example", "B": "Instead", "C": "Fortunately", "D": "In contrast"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching the history of the bicycle, a student has taken these notes:\n• Early bicycles were often difficult to balance.\n• In the 1880s, the “safety bicycle” became popular.\n• The safety bicycle had two wheels of similar size.\n• This design made bicycles easier for many people to ride.\nThe student wants to explain why the safety bicycle was important. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "The safety bicycle became popular in the 1880s and had two wheels.", "B": "Early bicycles were difficult to balance, but the safety bicycle’s two similar-sized wheels made riding easier for many people.", "C": "The history of the bicycle includes many different designs, including some from the 1880s.", "D": "Many people rode bicycles after the safety bicycle became popular."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-006", "prompt": "Jalen had practiced his speech all week. When he stepped onto the stage, his hands shook slightly, but he looked at the audience and began in a clear voice. By the end, he was smiling.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Jalen?", "choices": {"A": "He forgot most of his speech.", "B": "He was nervous at first but became more confident.", "C": "He did not care about doing well.", "D": "He had given many speeches before."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nMy aunt <u>who lives in Denver</u> sent me a postcard from the mountains.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "aunt, who lives in Denver,", "C": "aunt who lives, in Denver,", "D": "aunt, who lives in Denver"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-008", "prompt": "A student recorded the number of pages she read during four days.\nDay: Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday\nPages read: 18 | 24 | 20 | 30\nAccording to the table, on which day did the student read the greatest number of pages?", "choices": {"A": "Monday", "B": "Tuesday", "C": "Wednesday", "D": "Thursday"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-009", "prompt": "Sea otters often float on their backs while eating. They may use rocks to crack open hard shells, making them one of the few mammals known to use tools. This behavior helps sea otters eat foods that would otherwise be difficult to open.\nWhat is the main purpose of the underlined sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "To explain why sea otters sleep while floating", "B": "To show how sea otters solve a problem when eating", "C": "To compare sea otters with other ocean animals", "D": "To describe where sea otters usually find rocks"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-010", "prompt": "The art teacher asked students to look closely at a painting and describe what they noticed. Some students focused on the bright colors, while others observed the careful brushstrokes. The teacher explained that careful observation can help viewers ______ details they might otherwise miss.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "invent", "B": "appreciate", "C": "hide", "D": "remove"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe storm ended before sunset <u>the players returned to the field.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "sunset, the players returned to the field.", "C": "sunset, and the players returned to the field.", "D": "sunset the players, returned to the field."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nWhen the baby robins opened their beaks, the mother bird dropped worms into <u>its</u> mouths.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "his", "C": "their", "D": "our"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-013", "prompt": "Some deserts are extremely hot during the day. ______, temperatures in those same deserts can drop sharply at night.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "However", "B": "Therefore", "C": "Similarly", "D": "For instance"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-014", "prompt": "In the 1800s, many newspapers were printed using machines that had to be operated by hand. Later, faster printing presses allowed newspapers to be produced more quickly and in larger numbers. As a result, more people could read news about events happening far from their own towns.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Faster printing presses helped newspapers reach more readers.", "B": "Most newspapers in the 1800s reported only local events.", "C": "Printing newspapers by hand was easier than using machines.", "D": "People in the 1800s were not interested in news from far away."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching the planet Mars, a student has taken these notes:\n• Mars is often called the Red Planet.\n• Its reddish color comes from iron minerals in its soil.\n• Mars has the tallest known volcano in the solar system, Olympus Mons.\n• Scientists study Mars to learn more about planets besides Earth.\nThe student wants to explain why Mars looks red. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Mars is called the Red Planet because iron minerals in its soil give it a reddish color.", "B": "Scientists study Mars because it has a volcano called Olympus Mons.", "C": "Mars is one of several planets that scientists study besides Earth.", "D": "Olympus Mons is the tallest known volcano in the solar system."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Running through the park,</u> the fountain looked beautiful to Elena.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Running through the park, Elena thought the fountain looked beautiful.", "B": "The fountain running through the park looked beautiful to Elena.", "C": "Elena looked beautiful to the fountain while running through the park.", "D": "The park was running while Elena looked at the beautiful fountain."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-017", "prompt": "Alicia wrote a poem about a city street after a rainstorm. She described puddles reflecting traffic lights, umbrellas moving like bright dots, and the smell of wet pavement. These details help readers imagine the scene clearly.\nWhat is the main function of the details about puddles, umbrellas, and wet pavement?", "choices": {"A": "They explain why Alicia dislikes rainstorms.", "B": "They show that Alicia’s poem is mainly about traffic safety.", "C": "They create a vivid picture of the street after the storm.", "D": "They prove that the city has many transportation problems."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-018", "prompt": "To prepare for the science fair, Nora built a model bridge from wooden craft sticks. After placing small weights on the bridge, she noticed that one side bent more than the other. This result helped her identify a weak part of the design.\nAs used in the text, “identify” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "find", "B": "ignore", "C": "decorate", "D": "imagine"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nOur hiking club packed three important items<u> water bottles, trail maps, and flashlights.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "items: water bottles, trail maps, and flashlights.", "C": "items water bottles: trail maps, and flashlights.", "D": "items, water bottles: trail maps, and flashlights."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-020", "prompt": "A study of school lunches found that students chose fruit more often when the fruit was placed near the beginning of the lunch line. The fruit itself did not change, but its location did. The researchers concluded that small changes in arrangement can influence students’ choices.\nWhich choice best describes what the study suggests?", "choices": {"A": "Students never choose fruit unless teachers require it.", "B": "The order in which food is displayed can affect what students select.", "C": "Fruit should be removed from school lunch lines.", "D": "Students choose food only based on how it tastes."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about sleep. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Getting enough sleep helps students focus during the school day.\n2. Many students stay up late finishing homework or using electronic devices.\n3. Schools and families can support better habits by encouraging regular bedtimes.\n4. As a result, these students may feel tired in class the next morning.\nTo make the paragraph most logical, where should sentence 4 be placed?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nLast year, the robotics team <u>builds</u> a machine that could sort plastic blocks by color.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "build", "C": "built", "D": "has build"}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-023", "prompt": "A class measured the growth of a bean plant.\nWeek: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nHeight: 4 cm | 7 cm | 10 cm | 13 cm\nWhich statement is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "The plant grew the same amount each week.", "B": "The plant stopped growing after week 2.", "C": "The plant was tallest in week 1.", "D": "The plant grew more between weeks 1 and 2 than between weeks 3 and 4."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-024", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe school newspaper published an article about the food drive, and the article was helpful.\nWhich revision makes the sentence more specific?", "choices": {"A": "The school newspaper published an article about the food drive, and it was an article.", "B": "The school newspaper published a helpful article that listed food-drive dates, drop-off locations, and needed items.", "C": "The school newspaper published something about the food drive, and many people read it.", "D": "The school newspaper published an article about the food drive, which was nice."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe camp counselor taught us how to pitch a tent, how to build a safe campfire, and <u>reading a compass.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "how to read a compass.", "C": "a compass was read.", "D": "compass reading."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-026", "prompt": "Many birds migrate, or travel long distances, during certain seasons. Some migrate to find food, while others travel to places where they can raise their young. Scientists often track migrating birds to learn more about their routes and habits.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Scientists know everything about why birds migrate.", "B": "Birds migrate for different reasons, and scientists study their movements.", "C": "All birds migrate to the same place every year.", "D": "Birds migrate only when the weather becomes hot."}}, {"id": "RW1-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching glass recycling, a student has taken these notes:\n• Glass can be melted and made into new bottles and jars.\n• Recycling glass can reduce the need for new raw materials.\n• Some recycling centers sort glass by color.\n• Clear, green, and brown glass are often separated before processing.\nThe student wants to describe one environmental benefit of glass recycling. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Some recycling centers sort glass into clear, green, and brown groups.", "B": "Glass recycling can reduce the need for new raw materials because old glass can be melted into new bottles and jars.", "C": "Bottles and jars are usually made from glass that can be sorted by color.", "D": "Clear, green, and brown glass are often separated before processing."}}], [{"id": "RW2-M1-001", "prompt": "During the class trip, students were allowed to handle a replica of an ancient clay cup, but the original cup stayed behind glass because it was too fragile to touch.\nAs used in the text, “fragile” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "expensive", "B": "easily broken", "C": "carefully painted", "D": "difficult to find"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-002", "prompt": "Honeybees can share information about food sources with other bees. When a bee finds flowers with nectar, it may return to the hive and move in a pattern called a waggle dance. The movement can help other bees locate the flowers.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Honeybees use a special movement to help other bees find food.", "B": "Honeybees make honey only after they visit many flowers.", "C": "Flowers with nectar are usually far away from beehives.", "D": "Bees dance because they cannot fly inside a hive."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe principal displayed the <u>students</u> artwork in the front hallway.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "student’s", "C": "students’", "D": "students’s"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-004", "prompt": "Lena began practicing piano scales every afternoon. ______, her younger brother worked on a drawing at the kitchen table.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Meanwhile", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For example", "D": "Instead"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching the history of kites, a student has taken these notes:\n• Kites were flown in China more than 2,000 years ago.\n• Early kites were made from materials such as silk and bamboo.\n• Some early kites were used to measure wind or send signals.\n• Today, many kites are flown for sport or recreation.\nThe student wants to show that kites have had different purposes over time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Kites were flown in China more than 2,000 years ago and were made from silk and bamboo.", "B": "Early kites could be used to measure wind or send signals, while many kites today are flown for sport or recreation.", "C": "Kites are often made from different materials, including silk, bamboo, and other supplies.", "D": "People in China flew kites long before many people used them for recreation."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-006", "prompt": "At the beginning of the soccer season, Mateo rarely passed the ball. After his coach reminded the team that good plays often involve several players, Mateo began looking for open teammates. Soon, the team scored more often.\nWhich choice best describes what Mateo learned?", "choices": {"A": "Teamwork can improve a team’s success.", "B": "Coaches should let players ignore advice.", "C": "Passing the ball always prevents a team from scoring.", "D": "Soccer is easier when only one player controls the ball."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nMaya packed her lunch, <u>she forgot her water bottle.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "lunch she forgot her water bottle.", "C": "lunch, but she forgot her water bottle.", "D": "lunch but, she forgot her water bottle."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-008", "prompt": "A school club counted how many reusable bags it sold during one week.\nDay: Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Friday\nBags sold: 9 | 14 | 11 | 18\nAccording to the table, how many more bags were sold on Friday than on Monday?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "7", "C": "9", "D": "18"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-009", "prompt": "In a biography of astronaut Mae Jemison, the author explains that Jemison studied both engineering and medicine before traveling to space. This detail shows that Jemison’s path to becoming an astronaut included knowledge from more than one field.\nWhat is the main purpose of the detail about engineering and medicine?", "choices": {"A": "To show that Jemison’s background included different areas of study", "B": "To prove that all astronauts must become doctors before going to space", "C": "To explain why Jemison stopped being interested in science", "D": "To compare Jemison’s space mission with later missions"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-010", "prompt": "The new student did not know anyone in class, so Aaron invited him to join a lunch table and explained the rules of a classroom game. Aaron’s actions were ______ because they helped the new student feel included.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "generous", "B": "careless", "C": "hidden", "D": "ordinary"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nBecause the bus arrived early, <u>we had time to review our notes.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "Because the bus arrived early we had time, to review our notes.", "C": "Because, the bus arrived early we had time to review our notes.", "D": "Because the bus arrived early; we had time to review our notes."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-012", "prompt": "In the poem, the speaker describes a candle in a dark window as “a tiny sun.” This comparison suggests that the candle seems bright and comforting even though it is small.\nWhich choice best explains the effect of the comparison?", "choices": {"A": "It makes the candle seem powerful in the darkness.", "B": "It shows that the candle is too hot to touch.", "C": "It proves that the window is open.", "D": "It explains how candles are made."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-013", "prompt": "Some frogs can survive in very cold places by becoming inactive during winter. ______, certain frogs can partly freeze and then become active again when temperatures rise.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "For instance", "B": "Nevertheless", "C": "On the other hand", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-014", "prompt": "In the late 1800s, mail-order catalogs allowed people in rural areas to buy goods without traveling long distances to stores. Customers could look through printed pages, choose items, and receive their orders by mail. These catalogs made shopping more convenient for many families far from cities.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Mail-order catalogs helped rural customers shop more easily.", "B": "Stores in cities stopped selling goods in the late 1800s.", "C": "Most rural families refused to buy items by mail.", "D": "Catalogs were used mainly to teach people how to read."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching wind energy, a student has taken these notes:\n• Wind turbines use moving air to turn blades.\n• The turning blades help generate electricity.\n• Wind energy does not require burning fuel.\n• Some wind farms are located on open plains or offshore.\nThe student wants to explain how wind turbines produce electricity. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Some wind farms are located on open plains or offshore.", "B": "Wind energy does not require burning fuel, and some wind farms are offshore.", "C": "Wind turbines generate electricity when moving air turns their blades.", "D": "Wind turbines are one type of machine used in open areas."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe guide showed us three rooms in the historic house: the kitchen, the parlor, and <u>where people slept.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "the bedroom.", "C": "sleeping happened.", "D": "a room for sleep."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a neighborhood book exchange. The sentences are shown below.\n1. A neighborhood book exchange lets people take a book and leave a book for others.\n2. The small wooden box is usually placed where many neighbors can reach it.\n3. This simple system helps books continue to be read instead of sitting unused on shelves.\n4. Some people decorate the boxes with bright paint or signs.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to keep the focus on how the book exchange works before adding an extra detail?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-018", "prompt": "Ari looked at the first page of the novel and saw a long list of unfamiliar names. He almost closed the book, but then he noticed a map at the front. Using the map, he began to understand where the characters lived, and the story became easier to follow.\nWhich choice best describes Ari’s experience?", "choices": {"A": "A feature in the book helped him understand the story.", "B": "He decided that maps should not be included in novels.", "C": "He finished the entire novel without reading any names.", "D": "The first page made the story impossible to understand."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe bakery opened at six in the morning<u> however,</u> the first customers arrived at five forty-five.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "morning, however", "C": "morning; however,", "D": "morning however"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-020", "prompt": "A class compared how long three ice cubes took to melt in different places.\nLocation: Windowsill | Desk | Shaded shelf\nTime to melt: 22 min | 34 min | 41 min\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "The ice cube on the shaded shelf melted most slowly.", "B": "The ice cube on the windowsill melted most slowly.", "C": "All three ice cubes melted in the same amount of time.", "D": "The ice cube on the desk melted faster than the one on the windowsill."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe class presentation about volcanoes was interesting.\nWhich revision provides the most specific description of why the presentation was interesting?", "choices": {"A": "The class presentation about volcanoes was very interesting to many people.", "B": "The class presentation about volcanoes included a model eruption, photos of lava flows, and a clear explanation of how pressure builds underground.", "C": "The class presentation was about volcanoes, which are a topic that can be interesting.", "D": "The class presentation about volcanoes happened during science class and was interesting."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nEach of the dancers <u>wear</u> a blue scarf during the final scene.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "wears", "C": "have worn", "D": "were wearing"}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-023", "prompt": "In 1912, Juliette Gordon Low founded an organization that encouraged girls to explore nature, learn practical skills, and serve their communities. The organization, now known as Girl Scouts of the USA, grew from a small group into a national program.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Juliette Gordon Low helped create an organization that gave girls opportunities to learn and serve.", "B": "Girl Scouts of the USA was founded only to teach girls how to camp.", "C": "Juliette Gordon Low started a group that remained small for many years.", "D": "Community service became popular only after 1912."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching the artist Alma Thomas, a student has taken these notes:\n• Alma Thomas was an American painter.\n• She often used bright colors and repeated shapes.\n• Her paintings can suggest gardens, music, or space.\n• In 1972, she became the first Black woman to have a solo exhibition at the Whitney Museum of American Art.\nThe student wants to describe Thomas’s painting style. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Alma Thomas became the first Black woman with a solo exhibition at the Whitney Museum of American Art.", "B": "Alma Thomas was an American painter whose works often used bright colors and repeated shapes.", "C": "Alma Thomas painted works that were shown in a museum in 1972.", "D": "Alma Thomas’s paintings are connected to gardens, music, and museums."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nAfter the final bell rang, <u>the hallway filled with students rushing to buses.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "the hallway, filled with students rushing to buses.", "C": "the hallway filled, with students rushing to buses.", "D": "the hallway; filled with students rushing to buses."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-026", "prompt": "In the Arctic, some foxes have white fur in winter and brown fur in summer. The seasonal color change helps them blend with snow during colder months and with rocks and plants during warmer months.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains how the foxes’ fur color helps them in different seasons.", "B": "It argues that Arctic foxes should live only in snowy places.", "C": "It compares Arctic foxes with animals that cannot change color.", "D": "It shows that winter in the Arctic is always warmer than summer."}}, {"id": "RW2-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching farmers markets, a student has taken these notes:\n• Farmers markets are places where local growers sell food directly to customers.\n• Some markets sell fruits, vegetables, bread, and flowers.\n• Buying local food can help support nearby farms.\n• Many markets are held outdoors once or twice a week.\nThe student wants to explain one reason people may choose to shop at farmers markets. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Farmers markets may sell fruits, vegetables, bread, and flowers outdoors.", "B": "People may shop at farmers markets because buying local food can help support nearby farms.", "C": "Farmers markets are held once or twice a week in many places.", "D": "Local growers sometimes sell food directly to customers at outdoor markets."}}], [{"id": "RW3-M1-001", "prompt": "During a long charity walk, the organizers asked participants to move at a steady pace so that the group would stay together and arrive at each checkpoint on schedule.\nAs used in the text, “steady” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "expensive", "B": "even", "C": "silent", "D": "unusual"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-002", "prompt": "Octopuses can change the color and texture of their skin. This ability helps them hide from predators, surprise prey, and communicate with other octopuses. Scientists continue to study how octopuses control these quick changes.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Octopuses use changes in their skin for several important purposes.", "B": "Octopuses are the only animals that can hide from predators.", "C": "Scientists already understand everything about octopus behavior.", "D": "Octopuses change color mainly because ocean water is cold."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe coach praised the <u>team captains</u> decision to begin practice early.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "team captain’s", "C": "team captains’s", "D": "team captain"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-004", "prompt": "The students first sorted the donated clothing by size. ______, they folded each item and placed it in a labeled box.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Afterward", "B": "Nevertheless", "C": "For example", "D": "In contrast"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching ancient aqueducts, a student has taken these notes:\n• Aqueducts are structures that carry water from one place to another.\n• Some ancient Roman aqueducts used a slight downward slope.\n• The slope helped water move without pumps.\n• Aqueducts supplied water to cities, baths, and farms.\nThe student wants to explain how some ancient aqueducts moved water. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Some ancient Roman aqueducts used a slight downward slope that helped water move without pumps.", "B": "Aqueducts supplied water to cities, baths, and farms in ancient times.", "C": "Aqueducts are structures that can carry water from one place to another.", "D": "Ancient Roman aqueducts were one type of structure used by cities."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-006", "prompt": "Sofia opened the envelope and found the art contest results. She read the page twice, then ran to the kitchen holding it above her head. “Mom!” she called. “You have to see this!”\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Sofia?", "choices": {"A": "She is excited about the contest results.", "B": "She is trying to hide the results from her family.", "C": "She did not understand the page she read.", "D": "She wishes she had not entered the contest."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nI planned to finish my science poster on Thursday <u>I ran out of markers.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "Thursday, but I ran out of markers.", "C": "Thursday but, I ran out of markers.", "D": "Thursday, I ran out of markers."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-008", "prompt": "A school recycling team recorded the number of aluminum cans collected during four weeks.\nWeek: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nCans collected: 35 | 42 | 38 | 50\nAccording to the table, how many more cans were collected in week 4 than in week 1?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "12", "C": "15", "D": "50"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-009", "prompt": "A student writing about paleontology explains that fossils are not always complete skeletons. Footprints, eggshells, and even fossilized droppings can help scientists understand how ancient animals lived.\nWhat is the main purpose of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "To show that several kinds of fossils can provide information", "B": "To argue that skeletons are not useful to scientists", "C": "To explain why all fossils are easy to find", "D": "To describe how ancient animals built nests"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-010", "prompt": "The park’s new trail signs include large arrows, simple maps, and distances to nearby landmarks. These signs help visitors ______ the park more easily.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "decorate", "B": "navigate", "C": "ignore", "D": "replace"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nWhen the fire alarm sounded, the students walked calmly to the exit, and <u>wait</u> outside with their teacher.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "waits", "C": "waited", "D": "waiting"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe cookbook contains recipes for soups, breads, and desserts, <u>it also includes tips for storing leftovers.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "desserts; it also includes tips for storing leftovers.", "C": "desserts it also includes tips for storing leftovers.", "D": "desserts, it also, includes tips for storing leftovers."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-013", "prompt": "Some seeds are spread by wind, floating away from the parent plant on light, fluffy parts. ______, other seeds are spread by animals that carry them on fur or eat fruit and leave seeds behind.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Similarly", "B": "In contrast", "C": "Therefore", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-014", "prompt": "Before electric refrigerators became common, many families used iceboxes to keep food cool. Delivery workers brought large blocks of ice to homes, and the ice slowly melted inside the box. Although iceboxes required regular deliveries, they helped food stay fresh for longer than it would at room temperature.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Iceboxes helped families keep food cool before electric refrigerators became common.", "B": "Electric refrigerators required large blocks of ice to work properly.", "C": "Delivery workers stopped visiting homes after iceboxes became popular.", "D": "Food stored at room temperature always stayed fresh for many weeks."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching the moon, a student has taken these notes:\n• The moon does not make its own light.\n• Sunlight reflects off the moon’s surface.\n• The moon appears to change shape during the month.\n• These changing shapes are called phases.\nThe student wants to explain why the moon looks bright in the night sky. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "The moon appears to change shape during the month, and these shapes are called phases.", "B": "The moon looks bright because sunlight reflects off its surface.", "C": "The moon is visible at night during some parts of the month.", "D": "The moon has phases even though it does not change its actual shape."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe school garden includes three kinds of herbs<u> basil, mint, and rosemary.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "herbs: basil, mint, and rosemary.", "C": "herbs basil: mint, and rosemary.", "D": "herbs, basil: mint, and rosemary."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a class podcast. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Each episode of the class podcast features a different student interview.\n2. Before recording, students write questions and practice speaking clearly.\n3. After recording, they edit the audio and add a short introduction.\n4. The podcast is available on the school website.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to describe the preparation process before explaining where listeners can find the podcast?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-018", "prompt": "At the beginning of the school year, the drama club had only six members. After the club performed a short scene during morning announcements, more students came to the next meeting. The performance helped the club attract new members.\nAs used in the text, “attract” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "pull in", "B": "count", "C": "frighten", "D": "train"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Covered in colorful stickers,</u> Jordan carried the water bottle to soccer practice.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Jordan carried the water bottle covered in colorful stickers to soccer practice.", "B": "Covered in colorful stickers, soccer practice was where Jordan carried the water bottle.", "C": "Jordan, covered in colorful stickers, carried the water bottle to soccer practice.", "D": "The colorful stickers carried Jordan’s water bottle to soccer practice."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-020", "prompt": "A class recorded the number of minutes four students spent reading on Saturday.\nStudent: Ava | Ben | Chloe | Diego\nMinutes read: 25 | 40 | 35 | 30\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Ben read for the longest amount of time.", "B": "Ava read longer than Chloe.", "C": "Diego and Ben read for the same amount of time.", "D": "Chloe read for fewer minutes than Ava."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe new playground is fun.\nWhich revision gives the most specific reason the playground is fun?", "choices": {"A": "The new playground is fun because many students think it is fun.", "B": "The new playground has a climbing wall, a rope bridge, and a long slide that curves around a tower.", "C": "The new playground is located behind the school near the field.", "D": "The new playground opened recently and is used by students."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nNeither the blue folder nor the red notebook <u>were</u> on the desk after class.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "are", "C": "was", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-023", "prompt": "In some coastal towns, volunteers mark storm drains with small signs that say, “Drains to River” or “Drains to Bay.” The signs remind people that litter dropped near a drain may travel directly into local waterways. This reminder can encourage people to keep streets cleaner.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Storm drain signs can help people understand how street litter may affect waterways.", "B": "Coastal towns should remove all storm drains from their streets.", "C": "Volunteers mark storm drains because signs are difficult to read.", "D": "Rivers and bays are always located far away from streets."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching the poet Paul Laurence Dunbar, a student has taken these notes:\n• Paul Laurence Dunbar was an American poet and writer.\n• He published poetry, essays, short stories, and novels.\n• His poems appeared in both standard English and dialect.\n• His work helped him become one of the first nationally known Black writers in the United States.\nThe student wants to emphasize the variety of Dunbar’s writing. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Paul Laurence Dunbar wrote poetry, essays, short stories, and novels.", "B": "Paul Laurence Dunbar became nationally known in the United States.", "C": "Paul Laurence Dunbar was an American poet whose poems appeared in dialect.", "D": "Paul Laurence Dunbar’s work helped him become well known."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nMy brother enjoys sketching buildings, designing simple machines, and <u>to solve puzzles.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "solving puzzles.", "C": "puzzles are solved.", "D": "the solving of puzzles."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-026", "prompt": "A scientist studying lakes explains that clear water does not always mean healthy water. Some pollutants are invisible, and some harmful changes can be detected only by testing water samples. For this reason, scientists often use tools and laboratory tests to study lake conditions.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains why appearances alone may not show whether water is healthy.", "B": "It argues that all clear water is unsafe for people and animals.", "C": "It describes the exact tools used to collect lake water.", "D": "It shows that scientists can study lakes without testing samples."}}, {"id": "RW3-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching after-school tutoring programs, a student has taken these notes:\n• Some tutoring programs pair older students with younger students.\n• Tutors can help students practice reading or math skills.\n• Younger students may feel more comfortable asking questions in a small group.\n• Tutoring programs often meet once or twice a week.\nThe student wants to explain one possible benefit of tutoring programs for younger students. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Some tutoring programs meet once or twice a week and include older students.", "B": "Tutoring programs may help younger students practice reading or math while giving them a comfortable place to ask questions.", "C": "Older students sometimes work as tutors in after-school programs.", "D": "Tutoring programs can be organized in many schools after regular classes."}}], [{"id": "RW4-M1-001", "prompt": "The student’s directions were brief: meet by the front doors, bring a pencil, and wear comfortable shoes.\nAs used in the text, “brief” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "short", "B": "confusing", "C": "colorful", "D": "loud"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-002", "prompt": "Bats do not need bright light to find insects at night. Many bats make high sounds that bounce off nearby objects and return as echoes. By listening to the echoes, the bats can tell where insects and obstacles are.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some bats use echoes to find their way and locate food at night.", "B": "Bats are unable to fly unless the sky is bright.", "C": "Insects make echoes to protect themselves from bats.", "D": "All nighttime animals use the same method to find food."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nA stack of clean towels <u>sit</u> beside the swimming pool.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "sits", "C": "have sat", "D": "are sitting"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-004", "prompt": "The art club sketched designs for the school banner. ______, the members voted on which design to paint.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Then", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching sundials, a student has taken these notes:\n• Sundials are tools that use the sun to show time.\n• A raised part of the sundial casts a shadow.\n• As the sun appears to move across the sky, the shadow changes position.\n• The position of the shadow can show the time of day.\nThe student wants to explain how a sundial works. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Sundials are tools that have been used to show time during the day.", "B": "A sundial shows time because the sun makes a raised part cast a shadow that changes position.", "C": "The sun appears to move across the sky, especially during the day.", "D": "Some tools use shadows, while other tools use numbers or hands."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-006", "prompt": "When the class fish tank began to leak, Devon quickly placed a bucket under the dripping corner. He then found the teacher and calmly explained what had happened.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Devon?", "choices": {"A": "He responded to the problem in a careful way.", "B": "He caused the leak by moving the fish tank.", "C": "He ignored the problem until the next day.", "D": "He was afraid to tell the teacher about the leak."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nNora’s notebook was full of ideas<u> sketches of costumes, lists of props, and possible titles.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "ideas: sketches of costumes, lists of props, and possible titles.", "C": "ideas sketches: of costumes, lists of props, and possible titles.", "D": "ideas, sketches of costumes: lists of props, and possible titles."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-008", "prompt": "A student recorded the number of laps she ran during four practice days.\nDay: Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday | Friday\nLaps: 6 | 8 | 7 | 10\nAccording to the table, on which day did the student run 8 laps?", "choices": {"A": "Tuesday", "B": "Wednesday", "C": "Thursday", "D": "Friday"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-009", "prompt": "In a paragraph about pottery, a student explains that clay must be shaped before it is fired in a kiln. Once the clay is fired, it becomes hard and cannot easily be reshaped. This detail helps readers understand why potters plan their designs carefully.\nWhat is the main purpose of the detail about fired clay?", "choices": {"A": "To explain why potters must shape clay carefully before it hardens", "B": "To show that clay is always soft after it leaves a kiln", "C": "To argue that pottery should never be decorated", "D": "To describe where clay is usually found outdoors"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-010", "prompt": "At the end of the school concert, the audience stood and clapped for several minutes. The long applause showed that the performance had been ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "appreciated", "B": "delayed", "C": "hidden", "D": "replaced"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nBefore leaving for the museum, the students checked their backpacks and <u>line up</u> by the classroom door.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "lining up", "C": "lined up", "D": "has lined up"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe tiny seeds were carried by the breeze, <u>they landed near the fence.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "breeze they landed near the fence.", "C": "breeze, and they landed near the fence.", "D": "breeze, they, landed near the fence."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-013", "prompt": "Some students prefer to study in silence. ______, others remember information better when soft music plays in the background.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "On the other hand", "B": "As a result", "C": "For instance", "D": "In addition"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-014", "prompt": "Early cameras were large and difficult to carry. Over time, camera designs became smaller and easier to use. These changes made it possible for more people to take photographs in everyday places.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Smaller, easier-to-use cameras helped more people take photographs.", "B": "Early cameras were always used underwater.", "C": "People stopped taking photographs when cameras became smaller.", "D": "Camera designs have never changed."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching hummingbirds, a student has taken these notes:\n• Hummingbirds can beat their wings very quickly.\n• Their fast wingbeats help them hover in place.\n• Hummingbirds often feed on nectar from flowers.\n• Some hummingbirds migrate long distances.\nThe student wants to explain how hummingbirds are able to stay in one place in the air. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Hummingbirds often feed on nectar from flowers and may migrate long distances.", "B": "Hummingbirds can hover in place because their wings beat very quickly.", "C": "Some hummingbirds travel long distances during certain seasons.", "D": "Nectar is one food source for hummingbirds that visit flowers."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Carrying a tray of snacks,</u> the doorbell rang suddenly.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Carrying a tray of snacks, Elena heard the doorbell ring suddenly.", "B": "Carrying a tray of snacks, the doorbell rang suddenly for Elena.", "C": "The doorbell, carrying a tray of snacks, rang suddenly.", "D": "The tray of snacks carried the doorbell suddenly."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a school lost-and-found. The sentences are shown below.\n1. The school lost-and-found gives students a place to look for missing items.\n2. Students often find jackets, lunch boxes, or notebooks there.\n3. Items that are not claimed after several months may be donated.\n4. The lost-and-found is located beside the main office.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to explain where the lost-and-found is before describing what students find there?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-018", "prompt": "The theater director asked the actors to project their voices so people in the back row could hear every line.\nAs used in the text, “project” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "speak loudly and clearly", "B": "guess about the future", "C": "draw a careful picture", "D": "keep completely quiet"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe librarian recommended a mystery novel, a book of poems, and <u>reading a travel guide.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "a travel guide.", "C": "to travel in a guide.", "D": "travel guide reading."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-020", "prompt": "A class recorded the number of leaves on three small plants.\nPlant: A | B | C\nNumber of leaves: 12 | 15 | 9\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Plant B had the most leaves.", "B": "Plant C had more leaves than Plant A.", "C": "All three plants had the same number of leaves.", "D": "Plant A had fewer leaves than Plant C."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe school assembly was useful.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the assembly was useful?", "choices": {"A": "The school assembly was useful because it was held in the gym.", "B": "The school assembly was useful because students learned how to report bullying, find a counselor, and ask for help.", "C": "The school assembly was useful, and many students sat with their classes.", "D": "The school assembly was something that students attended during the day."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nEvery chair in the auditorium <u>have</u> a number on the back.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "has", "C": "were having", "D": "are having"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-023", "prompt": "Many sea stars can regrow a lost arm. This ability helps them recover from injuries caused by predators or rough waves. Some species can even grow a new body from part of an arm if enough of the center remains attached.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some sea stars can regrow body parts after injury.", "B": "Sea stars are unable to survive in rough water.", "C": "Predators never injure sea stars.", "D": "All sea stars grow new bodies from a single arm."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching the inventor Garrett Morgan, a student has taken these notes:\n• Garrett Morgan was an American inventor and business owner.\n• He patented a traffic signal in 1923.\n• His traffic signal included a warning position between “stop” and “go.”\n• Morgan also developed a safety hood that helped protect people from smoke.\nThe student wants to describe one feature of Morgan’s traffic signal. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Garrett Morgan was both an inventor and a business owner.", "B": "Morgan patented a traffic signal in 1923 that included a warning position between “stop” and “go.”", "C": "Morgan developed a safety hood that helped protect people from smoke.", "D": "Garrett Morgan created more than one invention during his career."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe <u>childrens</u> drawings were taped to the classroom wall.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "childrens’", "C": "children’s", "D": "child’s"}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-026", "prompt": "A student wrote a review of a video game. The review explains that the game’s instructions are easy to follow, the levels become harder slowly, and the puzzles require planning. These details support the student’s opinion that the game is challenging but fair.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the details about instructions, levels, and puzzles?", "choices": {"A": "They support the reviewer’s opinion about the game.", "B": "They prove that all video games are too easy.", "C": "They explain why the reviewer stopped playing the game.", "D": "They show that the game has no instructions."}}, {"id": "RW4-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching bike lanes, a student has taken these notes:\n• Bike lanes are marked areas of streets for bicycle riders.\n• Some bike lanes are separated from car traffic by posts or curbs.\n• Separated bike lanes can help riders feel safer.\n• Bike lanes may encourage more people to ride bicycles.\nThe student wants to explain one reason separated bike lanes can be helpful. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Bike lanes are marked areas of streets used by bicycle riders.", "B": "Some bike lanes are separated from car traffic by posts or curbs, which can help riders feel safer.", "C": "Bike lanes may encourage more people to ride bicycles in some places.", "D": "Bicycle riders sometimes use streets that have marked bike lanes."}}], [{"id": "RW5-M1-001", "prompt": "The travel toothbrush folded into a compact case, so it took up very little space in Anika’s backpack.\nAs used in the text, “compact” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "small", "B": "heavy", "C": "bright", "D": "expensive"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-002", "prompt": "Beavers build dams from sticks, mud, and stones. These dams slow the movement of water and can create ponds where many plants, insects, fish, and birds live. Because of this, beavers can change an area in ways that help other species.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Beaver dams can create habitats that support many living things.", "B": "Beavers build dams only when birds need places to live.", "C": "Sticks and stones are the only materials found near ponds.", "D": "Beavers avoid changing the areas where they live."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe group of singers <u>were</u> waiting beside the stage.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "have been", "D": "are being"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-004", "prompt": "The students cleaned the paintbrushes and closed the jars of paint. ______, they wiped the tables and stacked the chairs.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "After that", "B": "Nevertheless", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching snowshoes, a student has taken these notes:\n• Snowshoes are worn over boots.\n• They have a wide frame.\n• The wide frame spreads a person’s weight over a larger area.\n• This design helps people walk on deep snow without sinking as much.\nThe student wants to explain why snowshoes are useful. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Snowshoes are worn over boots and may have different kinds of frames.", "B": "Snowshoes help people walk on deep snow because their wide frames spread body weight over a larger area.", "C": "Snowshoes are useful in winter, especially when people wear boots.", "D": "Some people use snowshoes when they travel across snowy places."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-006", "prompt": "After borrowing her neighbor’s umbrella, Elise noticed that one rib was bent. She dried the umbrella, gently fixed the rib, and returned it with a note thanking her neighbor.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Elise?", "choices": {"A": "She treated the borrowed umbrella responsibly.", "B": "She wanted to keep the umbrella for herself.", "C": "She blamed her neighbor for the bent rib.", "D": "She forgot to return the umbrella."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe city’s oldest bridge <u>built in 1898</u> still carries pedestrians across the river.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "bridge, built in 1898,", "C": "bridge built, in 1898,", "D": "bridge, built in 1898"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-008", "prompt": "A class measured rainfall during four days.\nDay: Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday\nRainfall: 2 cm | 5 cm | 3 cm | 1 cm\nAccording to the table, which day had the most rainfall?", "choices": {"A": "Monday", "B": "Tuesday", "C": "Wednesday", "D": "Thursday"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-009", "prompt": "A sculptor creates artwork from pieces of old metal, broken tools, and discarded machine parts. In an interview, she says that using these materials lets her turn objects people no longer want into something worth noticing.\nWhat is the main purpose of the detail about old metal, broken tools, and discarded machine parts?", "choices": {"A": "To show what kinds of materials the sculptor gives new value to", "B": "To prove that the sculptor refuses to use any tools", "C": "To explain why metal objects should never be recycled", "D": "To describe the exact size of each sculpture"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-010", "prompt": "The school’s website now includes a calendar, lunch menus, and bus schedules. These updates make important information more ______ to families.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "available", "B": "distant", "C": "invisible", "D": "crowded"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe bakery sells muffins in the morning <u>the cinnamon rolls usually sell out first.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "morning, the cinnamon rolls usually sell out first.", "C": "morning, and the cinnamon rolls usually sell out first.", "D": "morning the cinnamon rolls, usually sell out first."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nLast summer, our family <u>visits</u> a cave where the guide pointed out underground streams.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "visit", "C": "visited", "D": "will visit"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-013", "prompt": "The hallway lights are controlled by motion sensors. ______, the lights turn on automatically when someone walks by.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Therefore", "B": "However", "C": "Similarly", "D": "Instead"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-014", "prompt": "For many years, lighthouses helped sailors travel safely near coastlines. Their bright lights warned ships about rocks, shallow water, or the location of a harbor. Even after newer navigation tools were invented, many lighthouses remained important landmarks.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Lighthouses helped guide sailors and warn them about coastal dangers.", "B": "Lighthouses were built only after modern navigation tools were invented.", "C": "Sailors used lighthouses mainly to learn how to build harbors.", "D": "Bright lights are useful only in places far from water."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching monarch butterflies, a student has taken these notes:\n• Monarch butterflies have orange and black wings.\n• Many monarchs migrate long distances.\n• Some travel from Canada or the United States to central Mexico.\n• Their migration can take several generations to complete.\nThe student wants to describe the long distance of monarch migration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Monarch butterflies have orange and black wings and migrate at different times.", "B": "Some monarch butterflies travel from Canada or the United States to central Mexico during migration.", "C": "Monarch butterflies are insects with colorful wings.", "D": "A butterfly’s migration may happen during more than one season."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Resting on the windowsill,</u> Liam noticed the small glass bird.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Liam noticed the small glass bird resting on the windowsill.", "B": "Resting on the windowsill, Liam noticed the small glass bird.", "C": "Liam, resting on the windowsill, noticed the small glass bird.", "D": "The windowsill noticed Liam resting on the small glass bird."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-017", "prompt": "In a review of a school cafeteria, a student writes that the salad bar has fresh lettuce, crisp carrots, sliced cucumbers, and several dressings. These details help explain why the student thinks the salad bar offers good choices.\nWhat is the main function of the details about lettuce, carrots, cucumbers, and dressings?", "choices": {"A": "They support the student’s opinion about the salad bar.", "B": "They show that the cafeteria serves only salads.", "C": "They explain why students should not eat vegetables.", "D": "They prove that the cafeteria has no other food options."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-018", "prompt": "To assist younger readers, the book includes a glossary that defines difficult words at the end of each chapter.\nAs used in the text, “assist” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "help", "B": "test", "C": "confuse", "D": "select"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe poster showed three rules for using the computer lab<u> save your work, keep food away from keyboards, and log out when finished.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "lab: save your work, keep food away from keyboards, and log out when finished.", "C": "lab save your work: keep food away from keyboards, and log out when finished.", "D": "lab, save your work: keep food away from keyboards, and log out when finished."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-020", "prompt": "A student timed how long it took four paper airplanes to cross the classroom.\nPlane: A | B | C | D\nTime: 4 seconds | 7 seconds | 5 seconds | 6 seconds\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Plane B took the longest time to cross the classroom.", "B": "Plane A took longer than Plane D.", "C": "Plane C and Plane D took the same amount of time.", "D": "Plane D took less time than Plane A."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe field trip was educational.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the field trip was educational?", "choices": {"A": "The field trip was educational because it was on a weekday.", "B": "The field trip was educational because students identified animal tracks, compared leaf shapes, and recorded stream observations.", "C": "The field trip was educational, and the students rode a bus to get there.", "D": "The field trip was something that many students attended with their teachers."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nEach of the glass jars <u>are</u> labeled with the name of a spice.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "is", "C": "were", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-023", "prompt": "A music teacher explains that a metronome makes a steady clicking sound. Musicians use the clicks to practice keeping a regular beat. This tool can help a performer play at the correct speed without rushing or slowing down.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A metronome helps musicians practice keeping a steady beat.", "B": "A metronome is a type of musical instrument used in concerts.", "C": "Musicians should never change the speed of a song.", "D": "Clicking sounds are useful only for beginning drummers."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching author Beverly Cleary, a student has taken these notes:\n• Beverly Cleary wrote books for children.\n• Her characters often faced everyday problems at home or school.\n• Ramona Quimby is one of her well-known characters.\n• Cleary’s books are known for humor and realistic details.\nThe student wants to describe a common feature of Cleary’s books. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Beverly Cleary wrote children’s books that often used humor and realistic details about everyday problems.", "B": "Ramona Quimby is one of the characters created by Beverly Cleary.", "C": "Beverly Cleary wrote books for children and became a well-known author.", "D": "Some books for children include characters who attend school."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe nurse reminded students to wash their hands, to cover coughs, and <u>keeping water bottles clean.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "to keep water bottles clean.", "C": "water bottles should be kept clean.", "D": "keeping clean water bottles."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-026", "prompt": "Many maps use symbols instead of drawing every object exactly as it appears. A small tent shape may show a campsite, while a dotted line may show a trail. A legend explains what the symbols mean.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the examples of the tent shape and dotted line?", "choices": {"A": "They show how map symbols can stand for real places or features.", "B": "They prove that maps should not include legends.", "C": "They explain why campsites are always far from trails.", "D": "They show that symbols make maps impossible to read."}}, {"id": "RW5-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching community tool libraries, a student has taken these notes:\n• Tool libraries allow people to borrow tools instead of buying them.\n• Members may borrow items such as drills, rakes, and ladders.\n• Borrowing tools can save money for people who need a tool only once or twice.\n• Tool libraries often require members to return items on time.\nThe student wants to explain one benefit of tool libraries. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Tool libraries may lend drills, rakes, and ladders to members.", "B": "Tool libraries can save people money by letting them borrow tools they need only once or twice.", "C": "Tool libraries often have rules about when borrowed items must be returned.", "D": "Some people use tools such as drills, rakes, and ladders for projects."}}], [{"id": "RW6-M1-001", "prompt": "The first step in the experiment was simple: place the bean seeds on a damp paper towel and leave them near a sunny window.\nAs used in the text, “simple” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "easy", "B": "strange", "C": "hidden", "D": "noisy"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-002", "prompt": "Mangrove trees grow along some warm coastlines. Their tangled roots can slow waves, hold soil in place, and provide shelter for young fish and crabs. These trees are important parts of many coastal ecosystems.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Mangrove trees help protect coastlines and support animal life.", "B": "Mangrove trees grow only in cold mountain regions.", "C": "Young fish and crabs avoid living near tree roots.", "D": "Waves make it impossible for trees to grow near water."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe <u>womens</u> basketball team practiced in the gym before school.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "woman’s", "C": "women’s", "D": "womens’"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-004", "prompt": "The students mixed flour, salt, and water to make dough. ______, the teacher heated the oven.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "At the same time", "B": "For this reason", "C": "Nevertheless", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching periscopes, a student has taken these notes:\n• A periscope is a tool that lets someone see over or around an object.\n• Simple periscopes can use two mirrors.\n• The mirrors are placed at angles inside a tube.\n• Light bounces from one mirror to the other before reaching the viewer’s eye.\nThe student wants to explain how a simple periscope works. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "A periscope is a tool that can be made in more than one way.", "B": "Simple periscopes use angled mirrors inside a tube to bounce light to the viewer’s eye.", "C": "A person can use a periscope to see over or around an object.", "D": "Some tubes contain mirrors that are placed inside them."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-006", "prompt": "Tessa saw a small tear in the library book’s cover. Instead of placing the book back on the shelf, she carried it to the front desk and told the librarian what she had noticed.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Tessa?", "choices": {"A": "She wanted to help take care of the library book.", "B": "She planned to damage the book further.", "C": "She believed the book should be hidden from the librarian.", "D": "She had already read every book in the library."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nMy cousin Diego <u>who lives in Phoenix</u> taught me how to make tamales.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "Diego, who lives in Phoenix,", "C": "Diego who lives, in Phoenix,", "D": "Diego, who lives in Phoenix"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-008", "prompt": "A student recorded how many minutes she practiced violin during four days.\nDay: Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday\nMinutes practiced: 15 | 25 | 20 | 30\nAccording to the table, how many more minutes did the student practice on Thursday than on Monday?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "10", "C": "15", "D": "30"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-009", "prompt": "A paragraph about stage design explains that lighting can change how a scene feels. A soft blue light may make a stage seem calm, while a sharp red light may make the same stage seem tense.\nWhat is the main purpose of the examples of blue and red lighting?", "choices": {"A": "To show how lighting can affect the mood of a scene", "B": "To explain why stage actors must wear blue costumes", "C": "To prove that red lights are brighter than all other lights", "D": "To describe how theater seats are arranged"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-010", "prompt": "The new classroom shelves have labels for novels, dictionaries, art supplies, and science materials. The labels help students ______ what they need quickly.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "locate", "B": "damage", "C": "forget", "D": "purchase"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nMina found her library card in her backpack <u>she used it to borrow two books.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "backpack, she used it to borrow two books.", "C": "backpack, and she used it to borrow two books.", "D": "backpack she used, it to borrow two books."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nYesterday, the debate team <u>meet</u> with their coach to practice opening statements.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "meets", "C": "met", "D": "will meet"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-013", "prompt": "The museum display includes a real astronaut glove. ______, it includes a video showing how astronauts move their fingers while wearing the glove.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "In addition", "B": "However", "C": "Instead", "D": "For this reason"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-014", "prompt": "Before backpacks became common, many students carried books with a strap or held them in their arms. Backpacks allowed students to carry books, lunches, and supplies while keeping their hands free. This made moving between home and school easier for many students.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Backpacks made it easier for students to carry school materials.", "B": "Students stopped carrying books after backpacks became common.", "C": "Backpacks were designed mainly to replace school lunches.", "D": "Carrying books by hand was always faster than using a backpack."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching dragonflies, a student has taken these notes:\n• Dragonflies have two pairs of wings.\n• They can fly forward, backward, and sideways.\n• Dragonflies often hunt small flying insects.\n• Their large eyes help them notice movement.\nThe student wants to describe a dragonfly’s flying ability. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Dragonflies have large eyes that help them notice movement.", "B": "Dragonflies can fly in several directions, including forward, backward, and sideways.", "C": "Dragonflies often hunt insects that fly through the air.", "D": "Dragonflies are insects with two pairs of wings and large eyes."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Waiting by the bus stop,</u> the rain began to fall on Marcus.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Waiting by the bus stop, Marcus felt the rain begin to fall.", "B": "Waiting by the bus stop, the rain began to fall on Marcus.", "C": "The rain, waiting by the bus stop, began to fall on Marcus.", "D": "Marcus began to fall while the rain waited by the bus stop."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about classroom plants. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Classroom plants can make a room feel more pleasant.\n2. Students may take turns watering the plants and checking the soil.\n3. Caring for plants can also help students practice responsibility.\n4. Some classroom plants grow well near a sunny window.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to mention where plants may be kept before explaining student responsibilities?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-018", "prompt": "The art contest rules require each drawing to be original, so students must create their own designs instead of copying pictures from books or websites.\nAs used in the text, “original” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "created independently", "B": "folded carefully", "C": "shown in public", "D": "difficult to erase"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe teacher brought three supplies for the activity<u> index cards, colored pencils, and glue sticks.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "activity: index cards, colored pencils, and glue sticks.", "C": "activity index cards: colored pencils, and glue sticks.", "D": "activity, index cards: colored pencils, and glue sticks."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-020", "prompt": "A class counted the number of shells found during a beach cleanup.\nGroup: A | B | C | D\nShells found: 11 | 16 | 13 | 9\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Group B found the most shells.", "B": "Group D found more shells than Group A.", "C": "Groups A and C found the same number of shells.", "D": "Group C found fewer shells than Group D."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe science demonstration was exciting.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the demonstration was exciting?", "choices": {"A": "The science demonstration was exciting because it happened after lunch.", "B": "The science demonstration was exciting because bubbles rose from a test tube, a balloon expanded, and the liquid changed color.", "C": "The science demonstration was exciting, and the teacher stood near the table.", "D": "The science demonstration was something the class watched during science."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nOne of the posters <u>show</u> the steps for recycling paper.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "shows", "C": "are showing", "D": "have shown"}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-023", "prompt": "Some rabbits thump their back feet on the ground. The sound can warn other rabbits nearby that danger may be present. This behavior helps the group respond quickly to possible threats.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Rabbits may use foot thumping to warn one another about danger.", "B": "Rabbits thump their feet only when they are looking for food.", "C": "Back feet prevent rabbits from hearing sounds nearby.", "D": "All rabbits live in groups that never face threats."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching the artist Faith Ringgold, a student has taken these notes:\n• Faith Ringgold is an American artist and writer.\n• She is known for story quilts.\n• Story quilts combine images, fabric, and written text.\n• Ringgold’s works often tell stories about family, history, and identity.\nThe student wants to explain what story quilts are. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Faith Ringgold is an American artist and writer known for story quilts.", "B": "Story quilts combine images, fabric, and written text to tell stories.", "C": "Faith Ringgold’s works often explore family, history, and identity.", "D": "Some artists create works that are connected to personal memories."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe guide asked visitors to stay on the path, to keep voices low, and <u>watching for trail signs.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "to watch for trail signs.", "C": "trail signs were watched.", "D": "watching trail signs carefully."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-026", "prompt": "A paragraph about sound explains that thick curtains can reduce echoes in a room. The curtains absorb some sound instead of letting it bounce repeatedly off hard walls. This is why theaters often include soft materials on walls or seats.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains how thick curtains help reduce echoes.", "B": "It argues that curtains should always be made of hard materials.", "C": "It describes why theaters never need seats.", "D": "It shows that echoes are useful in every room."}}, {"id": "RW6-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching neighborhood newsletters, a student has taken these notes:\n• Neighborhood newsletters share local information.\n• They may include event dates, safety reminders, and announcements.\n• Some newsletters are printed, while others are sent by email.\n• Newsletters can help residents learn about activities near them.\nThe student wants to explain one benefit of neighborhood newsletters. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Neighborhood newsletters may be printed or sent by email.", "B": "Neighborhood newsletters can help residents learn about local events, reminders, and announcements.", "C": "Some newsletters include information about more than one topic.", "D": "Printed newsletters and email newsletters are two common types."}}], [{"id": "RW7-M1-001", "prompt": "The portable speaker was small enough to fit in Kai’s jacket pocket, so he could carry it to the picnic easily.\nAs used in the text, “portable” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "easy to carry", "B": "difficult to hear", "C": "made of metal", "D": "newly repaired"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-002", "prompt": "Cactus plants can survive in dry places partly because of their special stems. Many cactuses store water in thick stems, and their waxy surfaces help keep that water from evaporating quickly. These features allow cactuses to live where rainfall is limited.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Cactuses have features that help them survive in dry environments.", "B": "Cactuses need more rainfall than most other plants.", "C": "Waxy surfaces cause cactus stems to lose water quickly.", "D": "Cactuses grow only in places where no other plants live."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nA pair of muddy boots <u>were</u> left outside the classroom door.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "have been", "D": "are being"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-004", "prompt": "The school printer stopped working just before the flyers were due. ______, the office assistant sent the announcement by email instead.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "As a result", "B": "For example", "C": "In contrast", "D": "Meanwhile"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching rice terraces, a student has taken these notes:\n• Rice terraces are flat fields built into hillsides.\n• The fields are arranged like steps.\n• The step-like design helps slow water as it moves downhill.\n• Slower water is easier for farmers to control.\nThe student wants to explain one purpose of the step-like design of rice terraces. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Rice terraces are flat fields that farmers build into hillsides.", "B": "Rice terraces are arranged like steps, which helps slow downhill water so farmers can control it.", "C": "Some hillsides have flat fields where rice can grow in rows.", "D": "Farmers use water in fields where rice is grown."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-006", "prompt": "Nadia had never ridden the city bus alone before. She checked the route number twice, kept her transfer ticket in her hand, and counted the stops until she reached the library.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Nadia?", "choices": {"A": "She was careful while taking an unfamiliar trip.", "B": "She forgot where she was going.", "C": "She rode the bus every day without thinking about it.", "D": "She decided not to go to the library."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nAfter the final rehearsal <u>the choir director reminded everyone to arrive early.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "After the final rehearsal, the choir director reminded everyone to arrive early.", "C": "After, the final rehearsal the choir director reminded everyone to arrive early.", "D": "After the final rehearsal the choir director, reminded everyone to arrive early."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-008", "prompt": "A class counted how many pencils were collected for a donation drive.\nGrade: 6 | 7 | 8 | 9\nPencils collected: 48 | 55 | 62 | 51\nAccording to the table, which grade collected the greatest number of pencils?", "choices": {"A": "Grade 6", "B": "Grade 7", "C": "Grade 8", "D": "Grade 9"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-009", "prompt": "A paragraph about comic strips explains that artists often use speech bubbles to show what characters say. Some artists also change the size or shape of the letters to show whether a character is whispering, shouting, or speaking nervously.\nWhat is the main purpose of the detail about letter size and shape?", "choices": {"A": "To show how comic artists can suggest the way a character speaks", "B": "To explain why comic strips should not include words", "C": "To prove that all comic strips use the same drawing style", "D": "To describe how paper for comic strips is made"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-010", "prompt": "The school nurse placed a poster near the sink reminding students to wash their hands for at least twenty seconds. The poster was meant to ______ healthy habits.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "encourage", "B": "delay", "C": "hide", "D": "borrow"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe wind knocked over the empty trash can <u>it rolled across the playground.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "trash can, it rolled across the playground.", "C": "trash can, and it rolled across the playground.", "D": "trash can it, rolled across the playground."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe two painters cleaned the brushes and put <u>it</u> back in the cabinet.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "them", "C": "he", "D": "its"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-013", "prompt": "Some birds build nests high in trees. ______, burrowing owls nest underground in tunnels made by other animals.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "In contrast", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For this reason", "D": "In addition"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-014", "prompt": "In the 1800s, some city streets were lit by gas lamps. Workers had to light the lamps each evening and turn them off in the morning. Later, electric streetlights made it easier to keep streets bright after dark.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Electric streetlights made nighttime street lighting easier than gas lamps did.", "B": "Gas lamps could turn themselves on each evening.", "C": "City streets in the 1800s were never lit after dark.", "D": "Workers used electric streetlights before gas lamps were invented."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching glaciers, a student has taken these notes:\n• Glaciers are large masses of ice.\n• They form where snow builds up over many years.\n• The weight of the snow presses lower layers into ice.\n• Some glaciers move slowly downhill.\nThe student wants to explain how glaciers form. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Glaciers are large masses of ice, and some move slowly downhill.", "B": "Glaciers form when snow builds up for many years and its weight presses lower layers into ice.", "C": "Some ice can be found in cold places where snow falls.", "D": "Glaciers can move across land very slowly."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Covered with tiny beads,</u> Priya admired the handmade bracelet.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Priya admired the handmade bracelet covered with tiny beads.", "B": "Covered with tiny beads, Priya admired the handmade bracelet.", "C": "Priya, covered with tiny beads, admired the handmade bracelet.", "D": "The tiny beads admired Priya’s handmade bracelet."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a classroom job chart. The sentences are shown below.\n1. A classroom job chart shows which students are responsible for different tasks.\n2. Jobs may include passing out papers, feeding the class pet, or wiping the board.\n3. The chart is updated each week so students can try different responsibilities.\n4. The chart hangs near the classroom door.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to identify where the chart is before explaining what it lists?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-018", "prompt": "The author revised the ending of the story because the first version was unclear and left readers unsure about what happened to the main character.\nAs used in the text, “unclear” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "hard to understand", "B": "full of color", "C": "written quickly", "D": "easy to remember"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe <u>mans</u> coat was hanging on the back of the chair.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "man’s", "C": "mans’", "D": "mens"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-020", "prompt": "A student recorded the number of pages in four short stories.\nStory: A | B | C | D\nPages: 14 | 21 | 18 | 16\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Story B has the most pages.", "B": "Story A has more pages than Story C.", "C": "Story D and Story B have the same number of pages.", "D": "Story C has fewer pages than Story A."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe classroom display was helpful.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the display was helpful?", "choices": {"A": "The classroom display was helpful because it was on the wall.", "B": "The classroom display was helpful because it showed the homework steps, due dates, and examples of completed work.", "C": "The classroom display was helpful, and students looked at it sometimes.", "D": "The classroom display was something the teacher made for the class."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe captain of the two teams <u>are</u> meeting with the referee.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "is", "C": "were", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-023", "prompt": "Some ants leave scent trails as they move. Other ants can follow these trails to reach food or return to the nest. The scent trails help the ants share information without using sound.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some ants use scent trails to guide other ants.", "B": "Ants cannot find food unless humans help them.", "C": "Scent trails prevent ants from returning to the nest.", "D": "All insects communicate in exactly the same way."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching the librarian Pura Belpré, a student has taken these notes:\n• Pura Belpré was a storyteller, writer, and librarian.\n• She worked in New York City public libraries.\n• She shared Puerto Rican folktales with children.\n• An award for Latino children’s literature is named in her honor.\nThe student wants to describe Belpré’s work with stories. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Pura Belpré worked in public libraries in New York City.", "B": "Pura Belpré shared Puerto Rican folktales with children through her work as a storyteller, writer, and librarian.", "C": "A children’s literature award is named in honor of Pura Belpré.", "D": "Public libraries sometimes hold storytelling programs for children."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe camp schedule includes swimming in the morning, hiking after lunch, and <u>to play games</u> in the evening.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "playing games", "C": "games are played", "D": "to be playing games"}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-026", "prompt": "A paragraph about school gardens explains that earthworms can improve soil. As earthworms move through the ground, they create small tunnels that let air and water reach plant roots more easily.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains how earthworms can help soil support plants.", "B": "It argues that earthworms should be removed from all gardens.", "C": "It describes why plant roots avoid air and water.", "D": "It shows that earthworms live only above the ground."}}, {"id": "RW7-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching community language classes, a student has taken these notes:\n• Community language classes may teach English, Spanish, Mandarin, or other languages.\n• Classes often include speaking practice.\n• Learning another language can help neighbors communicate with more people.\n• Some classes are held at libraries or community centers.\nThe student wants to explain one benefit of community language classes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Community language classes may take place at libraries or community centers.", "B": "Community language classes can help neighbors communicate with more people by teaching languages and offering speaking practice.", "C": "Some language classes teach English, Spanish, Mandarin, or other languages.", "D": "Classes held in community centers may meet in the evening."}}], [{"id": "RW8-M1-001", "prompt": "The librarian chose a durable cover for the new dictionary because it would be used by many students every day.\nAs used in the text, “durable” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "long-lasting", "B": "brightly colored", "C": "easy to fold", "D": "recently printed"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-002", "prompt": "Tide pools are small pools of seawater that remain on rocky shores after the tide goes out. Animals living in tide pools must handle changing conditions, such as warmer water, less space, and waves returning at high tide. These pools may look simple, but they are home to organisms with special survival skills.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Tide pools contain animals adapted to changing conditions.", "B": "Tide pools are always empty after the tide goes out.", "C": "Animals in tide pools never face difficult conditions.", "D": "Rocky shores are found only in warm places."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe basket of apples <u>were</u> placed on the kitchen counter.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-004", "prompt": "The gym floor had just been cleaned. ______, the coach asked students to leave their muddy shoes outside.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "For that reason", "B": "In contrast", "C": "For example", "D": "Meanwhile"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching paper cranes, a student has taken these notes:\n• Paper cranes are made through origami, the art of paper folding.\n• A single square sheet of paper is often used.\n• The paper is folded into a bird shape without cutting.\n• Origami can be used to create animals, boxes, and other forms.\nThe student wants to explain how a paper crane is made. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Paper cranes are one type of animal shape made through origami.", "B": "A paper crane is made by folding a single square sheet of paper into a bird shape without cutting it.", "C": "Origami can be used to make animals, boxes, and many other forms.", "D": "Many people use paper when they create folded objects."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-006", "prompt": "Owen wanted to join the chess club, but he worried that everyone else would be more experienced. At the first meeting, an older student explained the rules patiently and invited Owen to play a practice game. Owen came back the next week.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Owen?", "choices": {"A": "He felt more comfortable after receiving help.", "B": "He decided never to play chess again.", "C": "He already knew every rule before the meeting.", "D": "He disliked the older student’s explanation."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nBefore the visitors entered the greenhouse <u>the guide explained how to stay on the path.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "Before the visitors entered the greenhouse, the guide explained how to stay on the path.", "C": "Before, the visitors entered the greenhouse the guide explained how to stay on the path.", "D": "Before the visitors entered the greenhouse the guide, explained how to stay on the path."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-008", "prompt": "A student recorded the number of stickers sold by the art club during four lunch periods.\nLunch period: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nStickers sold: 12 | 19 | 15 | 22\nAccording to the table, how many more stickers were sold during lunch period 4 than during lunch period 1?", "choices": {"A": "7", "B": "10", "C": "12", "D": "22"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-009", "prompt": "A paragraph about bird nests explains that not all nests are built in trees. Some birds nest on cliffs, on the ground, or inside holes in tree trunks. These examples help show that birds can use different places to protect their eggs.\nWhat is the main purpose of the examples of cliffs, ground nests, and holes in tree trunks?", "choices": {"A": "To show that birds may build nests in many kinds of places", "B": "To prove that birds never use trees for nests", "C": "To explain why all bird eggs are the same size", "D": "To describe how cliffs are formed"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-010", "prompt": "The museum guide spoke slowly and pointed to important details in each painting. Her explanation made the paintings easier to ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "understand", "B": "hide", "C": "carry", "D": "repair"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe students measured the table, cut the paper, and <u>they taped</u> the banner to the wall.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "taping", "C": "taped", "D": "to tape"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe science magazine arrived in the mail <u>my sister read the article about volcanoes first.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "mail, my sister read the article about volcanoes first.", "C": "mail, and my sister read the article about volcanoes first.", "D": "mail my sister, read the article about volcanoes first."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-013", "prompt": "Some students learn best by reading instructions. ______, others understand a task more easily after watching a demonstration.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "In contrast", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For instance", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-014", "prompt": "For centuries, people used hand fans to create a cooling breeze. Fans could also be decorated with painted designs or folded so they were easy to carry. In some cultures, fans became both useful tools and works of art.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Hand fans have served both practical and artistic purposes.", "B": "Hand fans were useful only before people learned to paint.", "C": "All decorated objects must be folded to work correctly.", "D": "People used hand fans mainly to make rooms darker."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching sea glass, a student has taken these notes:\n• Sea glass begins as broken pieces of glass.\n• Waves and sand smooth the sharp edges over time.\n• Sea glass is often found on beaches.\n• Pieces may appear green, brown, white, or blue.\nThe student wants to explain why sea glass feels smooth. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Sea glass may be found on beaches in several different colors.", "B": "Sea glass feels smooth because waves and sand wear down the sharp edges of broken glass over time.", "C": "Broken pieces of glass can sometimes become green, brown, white, or blue.", "D": "Many beaches contain objects that have been moved by waves."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Painted bright yellow,</u> Marisol noticed the bicycle leaning against the fence.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Marisol noticed the bicycle painted bright yellow leaning against the fence.", "B": "Painted bright yellow, Marisol noticed the bicycle leaning against the fence.", "C": "Marisol, painted bright yellow, noticed the bicycle leaning against the fence.", "D": "The fence noticed Marisol’s bicycle painted bright yellow."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a class suggestion box. The sentences are shown below.\n1. A class suggestion box gives students a way to share ideas with the teacher.\n2. Students can write suggestions on slips of paper and place them inside the box.\n3. The teacher reviews the suggestions at the end of each week.\n4. The box sits on a shelf near the classroom calendar.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to tell where the box is before explaining how students use it?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-018", "prompt": "The instructions said to attach the two pieces of cardboard securely so they would not come apart during the test.\nAs used in the text, “securely” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "firmly", "B": "secretly", "C": "quickly", "D": "politely"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe <u>girls</u> soccer uniforms were stacked on the bench before the game.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "girl’s", "C": "girls’", "D": "girls’s"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-020", "prompt": "A class recorded the number of questions answered correctly during a review game.\nTeam: Red | Blue | Green | Yellow\nCorrect answers: 14 | 17 | 12 | 19\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Yellow had the most correct answers.", "B": "Green had more correct answers than Blue.", "C": "Red and Yellow had the same number of correct answers.", "D": "Blue had fewer correct answers than Green."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe new library corner is comfortable.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the library corner is comfortable?", "choices": {"A": "The new library corner is comfortable because it is part of the classroom.", "B": "The new library corner has soft cushions, a small rug, and a lamp for quiet reading.", "C": "The new library corner is comfortable, and students sometimes sit there.", "D": "The new library corner was arranged after the teacher moved a table."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nNeither the pencil case nor the notebook <u>are</u> in my backpack.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "is", "C": "were", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-023", "prompt": "Some spiders build webs to catch insects. The sticky threads can trap small flying insects, and the spider can sense movement in the web. This helps the spider know when prey has been caught.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some spiders use webs to catch and detect prey.", "B": "Spiders build webs mainly to decorate trees.", "C": "Sticky threads prevent spiders from sensing movement.", "D": "All flying insects can escape spider webs easily."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching author Laurence Yep, a student has taken these notes:\n• Laurence Yep is an American writer.\n• He has written novels, picture books, and plays.\n• Some of his books explore Chinese American history and family stories.\n• Dragonwings is one of his well-known novels.\nThe student wants to emphasize the variety of Yep’s writing. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Laurence Yep has written novels, picture books, and plays.", "B": "Dragonwings is a well-known novel by Laurence Yep.", "C": "Some of Laurence Yep’s books explore Chinese American history.", "D": "Laurence Yep is an American writer who has written about families."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe club members promised to collect canned food, to sort donations, and <u>delivering boxes</u> to the shelter.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "to deliver boxes", "C": "boxes were delivered", "D": "delivering the boxes"}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-026", "prompt": "A paragraph about rain barrels explains that they collect water from rooftops during storms. The stored water can later be used for gardens, which may reduce the amount of tap water needed for watering plants.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains a practical benefit of collecting rainwater.", "B": "It argues that gardens should never use stored water.", "C": "It describes why rooftops cannot collect rain.", "D": "It shows that rain barrels are used only indoors."}}, {"id": "RW8-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching local history walking tours, a student has taken these notes:\n• Walking tours guide people through important places in a town.\n• Tours may include old homes, public buildings, or historic streets.\n• Guides often tell stories about people who lived in the area.\n• Walking tours can help residents and visitors learn local history.\nThe student wants to explain one benefit of local history walking tours. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Walking tours may include old homes, public buildings, or historic streets.", "B": "Local history walking tours can help residents and visitors learn about a town’s past through important places and stories.", "C": "Some tour guides tell stories about people who once lived in the area.", "D": "Walking tours are usually taken by people who are able to travel through a town."}}], [{"id": "RW9-M1-001", "prompt": "The hiking boots were made with sturdy soles, so they could handle rocky trails without wearing out quickly.\nAs used in the text, “sturdy” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "strong", "B": "colorful", "C": "wet", "D": "narrow"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-002", "prompt": "Kelp forests grow in cool ocean water. Their tall, leaflike blades create shelter for fish, sea urchins, crabs, and other animals. Like forests on land, kelp forests provide living space for many species.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Kelp forests provide habitat for many ocean animals.", "B": "Kelp forests grow only where no animals live.", "C": "Sea urchins build kelp forests in cool water.", "D": "Forests on land and kelp forests are made of the same plants."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe tray of blueberry muffins <u>were</u> cooling on the kitchen table.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-004", "prompt": "The students rinsed the paint cups and hung the wet brushes to dry. ______, they swept the floor around the art tables.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Afterward", "B": "However", "C": "For example", "D": "In contrast"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching weather vanes, a student has taken these notes:\n• A weather vane is usually placed on a roof or tower.\n• It can spin when the wind blows.\n• The arrow points in the direction the wind is coming from.\n• Weather vanes help people observe wind direction.\nThe student wants to explain what a weather vane does. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "A weather vane is often placed on a roof or tower where wind can reach it.", "B": "A weather vane spins in the wind and points toward the direction the wind is coming from.", "C": "Some towers and roofs have weather vanes attached to them.", "D": "People have used weather tools to study the outdoors for many years."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-006", "prompt": "Eli had practiced the trumpet part at home, but he still worried about playing it alone in front of the band. When his turn came, he took a deep breath, counted the beats carefully, and played the notes without stopping.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Eli?", "choices": {"A": "He managed his nervousness and completed the part.", "B": "He forgot to bring his trumpet to practice.", "C": "He refused to play when his turn came.", "D": "He had never practiced the music before."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nBefore the bake sale began <u>the volunteers arranged cookies on trays.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "Before the bake sale began, the volunteers arranged cookies on trays.", "C": "Before, the bake sale began the volunteers arranged cookies on trays.", "D": "Before the bake sale began the volunteers, arranged cookies on trays."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-008", "prompt": "A student recorded how many bookmarks the library club gave away during four days.\nDay: Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday\nBookmarks given away: 18 | 26 | 21 | 30\nAccording to the table, how many more bookmarks were given away on Thursday than on Wednesday?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "9", "C": "12", "D": "30"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-009", "prompt": "A paragraph about calligraphy explains that artists often vary the thickness of their lines. Thin lines can make letters look delicate, while thick lines can make letters seem bold. These choices help calligraphers create different effects with writing.\nWhat is the main purpose of the examples of thin and thick lines?", "choices": {"A": "To show how calligraphers can create different visual effects", "B": "To explain why calligraphy should not include letters", "C": "To prove that thick lines are always easier to read than thin lines", "D": "To describe the kind of paper used in calligraphy"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-010", "prompt": "The science teacher placed the microscope slides in a padded box so the glass would not crack during the move. The box was meant to ______ the slides.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "protect", "B": "count", "C": "erase", "D": "invite"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe class finished the group project <u>everyone turned in the final report.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "project, everyone turned in the final report.", "C": "project, and everyone turned in the final report.", "D": "project everyone, turned in the final report."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nLast week, the gardening club <u>plant</u> tulip bulbs near the front entrance.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "plants", "C": "planted", "D": "will plant"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-013", "prompt": "Some students prefer to outline an essay before writing. ______, others begin with a rough paragraph and organize their ideas later.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "By contrast", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For this reason", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-014", "prompt": "Before telephones became common in homes, people often sent short written messages by telegraph. Telegraph operators used electrical signals to send words over long distances. This technology helped people share urgent news more quickly than sending letters by mail.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Telegraphs allowed people to send messages quickly over long distances.", "B": "Telegraphs were used mainly to deliver packages by mail.", "C": "Telephones were invented before people needed long-distance messages.", "D": "Telegraph operators avoided using electrical signals."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching pulleys, a student has taken these notes:\n• A pulley is a wheel with a rope or cable around it.\n• Pulling the rope can help lift an object.\n• Some pulleys make heavy objects easier to raise.\n• Pulleys are used in flagpoles, cranes, and elevators.\nThe student wants to explain how a pulley can help lift something. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Pulleys can be found in flagpoles, cranes, and elevators.", "B": "A pulley uses a rope or cable around a wheel to help lift an object.", "C": "Some machines have wheels, ropes, or cables as parts.", "D": "Heavy objects are sometimes raised in buildings or on construction sites."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Shaped like a tiny house,</u> Gabriel placed the bird feeder on a post.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Gabriel placed the bird feeder shaped like a tiny house on a post.", "B": "Shaped like a tiny house, Gabriel placed the bird feeder on a post.", "C": "Gabriel, shaped like a tiny house, placed the bird feeder on a post.", "D": "The post placed Gabriel on a bird feeder shaped like a tiny house."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a school supply station. The sentences are shown below.\n1. A school supply station gives students access to extra materials.\n2. Students may borrow pencils, rulers, or notebook paper from the station.\n3. The station is checked each Friday so missing supplies can be replaced.\n4. The station is a small cart near the counselor’s office.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to identify the station’s location before explaining what students may borrow?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-018", "prompt": "The coach asked the swimmers to monitor their times during practice by checking the clock after each lap.\nAs used in the text, “monitor” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "watch", "B": "forget", "C": "decorate", "D": "fold"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe checklist included three items for the camping trip<u> sunscreen, extra socks, and a flashlight.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "trip: sunscreen, extra socks, and a flashlight.", "C": "trip sunscreen: extra socks, and a flashlight.", "D": "trip, sunscreen: extra socks, and a flashlight."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-020", "prompt": "A class recorded the number of votes for four field trip choices.\nChoice: Aquarium | Theater | Planetarium | Nature Center\nVotes: 14 | 19 | 23 | 16\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "The planetarium received the most votes.", "B": "The aquarium received more votes than the theater.", "C": "The theater and nature center received the same number of votes.", "D": "The nature center received fewer votes than the aquarium."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe after-school workshop was useful.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the workshop was useful?", "choices": {"A": "The after-school workshop was useful because it happened in the library.", "B": "The after-school workshop was useful because students practiced taking notes, organizing folders, and planning homework time.", "C": "The after-school workshop was useful, and several students attended it.", "D": "The after-school workshop was something that happened after classes ended."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe row of lockers near the gym <u>are</u> painted green.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "is", "C": "were", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-023", "prompt": "Squirrels sometimes bury acorns and other nuts in the ground. Later, they may return to dig up the stored food. If a squirrel forgets some buried nuts, those nuts may sprout into new trees.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Squirrels can store food, and forgotten nuts may grow into trees.", "B": "Squirrels bury nuts because they never eat them later.", "C": "Acorns can sprout only when humans plant them.", "D": "New trees prevent squirrels from finding food."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching athlete Wilma Rudolph, a student has taken these notes:\n• Wilma Rudolph was an American sprinter.\n• As a child, she overcame serious illness and difficulty walking.\n• She later won three gold medals at the 1960 Olympic Games.\n• Rudolph became a symbol of determination for many people.\nThe student wants to explain why Rudolph inspired others. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Wilma Rudolph was an American sprinter who competed in the Olympic Games.", "B": "Rudolph inspired others because she overcame serious childhood illness and later won three Olympic gold medals.", "C": "The 1960 Olympic Games included many track and field events.", "D": "Wilma Rudolph had difficulty walking when she was a child."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe students agreed to gather leaves, to label each sample, and <u>placing them</u> in a notebook.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "to place them", "C": "placed them", "D": "samples were placed"}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-026", "prompt": "A paragraph about murals explains that artists sometimes paint a light sketch on the wall before adding final colors. The sketch helps them plan where figures, shapes, and words should go.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains why artists may sketch before painting a mural.", "B": "It argues that murals should never include final colors.", "C": "It describes why artists avoid planning large paintings.", "D": "It shows that sketches are always painted after murals are finished."}}, {"id": "RW9-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching community repair cafés, a student has taken these notes:\n• Repair cafés are events where people bring broken household items.\n• Volunteers may help repair lamps, clothing, toys, or small appliances.\n• Repair cafés can keep usable items from being thrown away.\n• Some events also teach visitors basic repair skills.\nThe student wants to explain one benefit of repair cafés. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Repair cafés are events where people bring broken household items.", "B": "Repair cafés can help keep usable items out of the trash and teach visitors basic repair skills.", "C": "Volunteers at repair cafés may work with lamps, clothing, toys, or small appliances.", "D": "Some household items are easier to repair than others."}}], [{"id": "RW10-M1-001", "prompt": "The ceramic bowl was placed on the top shelf because it was valuable and needed to be handled with care.\nAs used in the text, “valuable” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "worth a lot", "B": "easy to carry", "C": "recently washed", "D": "full of food"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-002", "prompt": "Mosses are small plants that often grow in damp places. Unlike many plants, they do not have deep roots. Instead, they absorb water through their surfaces, which helps them survive on rocks, logs, and shaded ground.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Mosses have features that help them live in damp places.", "B": "Mosses grow only in dry deserts.", "C": "Mosses have the deepest roots of all plants.", "D": "Mosses cannot grow on rocks or logs."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe box of colored chalk <u>were</u> stored near the blackboard.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-004", "prompt": "The students measured the classroom windows. ______, they cut paper decorations to fit the glass.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Then", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching hot-air balloons, a student has taken these notes:\n• Hot-air balloons have a large fabric envelope.\n• Air inside the envelope is heated.\n• Warm air is lighter than cooler air around it.\n• This difference helps the balloon rise.\nThe student wants to explain why a hot-air balloon rises. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Hot-air balloons have large fabric envelopes that can come in many colors.", "B": "A hot-air balloon rises because heated air inside its envelope is lighter than the cooler air around it.", "C": "Some balloons are used for travel, while others are used for decoration.", "D": "Air can be found both inside and outside a hot-air balloon."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-006", "prompt": "Maya saw that the classroom recycling bin was overflowing. She flattened several cardboard boxes, placed them neatly beside the bin, and wrote a note asking classmates not to stack more items on top.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Maya?", "choices": {"A": "She tried to solve the problem in an organized way.", "B": "She wanted classmates to stop recycling forever.", "C": "She planned to hide the recycling bin.", "D": "She did not notice that the bin was full."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nAfter the morning announcements <u>students returned to their group projects.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "After the morning announcements, students returned to their group projects.", "C": "After, the morning announcements students returned to their group projects.", "D": "After the morning announcements students, returned to their group projects."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-008", "prompt": "A student recorded the number of visitors to a classroom exhibit during four hours.\nHour: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nVisitors: 13 | 20 | 18 | 25\nAccording to the table, during which hour did the exhibit have 20 visitors?", "choices": {"A": "Hour 1", "B": "Hour 2", "C": "Hour 3", "D": "Hour 4"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-009", "prompt": "A paragraph about puppetry explains that puppeteers use more than voices to make characters seem alive. Small movements of a puppet’s head, hands, or shoulders can show surprise, worry, or excitement.\nWhat is the main purpose of the details about a puppet’s movements?", "choices": {"A": "To show how puppeteers can express emotions through movement", "B": "To explain why puppets should never be used in performances", "C": "To prove that puppeteers do not need practice", "D": "To describe the materials used to build a stage"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-010", "prompt": "The student read the directions twice before starting the project. Reading carefully helped her ______ making mistakes.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "avoid", "B": "collect", "C": "decorate", "D": "announce"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe bus stopped at the corner <u>several students climbed aboard.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "corner, several students climbed aboard.", "C": "corner, and several students climbed aboard.", "D": "corner several students, climbed aboard."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nTomorrow, the class <u>visited</u> the local history museum.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "visits", "C": "visit", "D": "will visit"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-013", "prompt": "Some students like to solve math problems on paper. ______, others prefer using a whiteboard so they can erase and change their work easily.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "On the other hand", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For this reason", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-014", "prompt": "Before email, many people wrote letters to communicate with friends or family who lived far away. Letters could take days or weeks to arrive, but they allowed people to share news, questions, and personal thoughts across long distances.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Letters helped people communicate across long distances before email.", "B": "Letters always arrived faster than modern messages.", "C": "People wrote letters only to ask for directions.", "D": "Email was used for many centuries before letters."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching barn owls, a student has taken these notes:\n• Barn owls often hunt at night.\n• They have excellent hearing.\n• Their soft feathers help them fly quietly.\n• Quiet flight can make it easier for them to approach prey.\nThe student wants to explain how barn owls can hunt quietly. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Barn owls often hunt at night and use their hearing.", "B": "Barn owls can approach prey quietly because their soft feathers help them fly with little sound.", "C": "Some owls are active at night and search for food.", "D": "Excellent hearing is useful for animals that hunt after dark."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Filled with fresh lemonade,</u> Jamal carried the pitcher to the picnic table.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Jamal carried the pitcher filled with fresh lemonade to the picnic table.", "B": "Filled with fresh lemonade, Jamal carried the pitcher to the picnic table.", "C": "Jamal, filled with fresh lemonade, carried the pitcher to the picnic table.", "D": "The picnic table carried Jamal’s pitcher filled with fresh lemonade."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a school equipment checkout system. The sentences are shown below.\n1. A school equipment checkout system helps teachers borrow items for classroom activities.\n2. Teachers can sign out calculators, clipboards, or measuring tapes.\n3. The equipment is returned to the storage room at the end of the day.\n4. The checkout sheet is kept in a binder near the storage-room door.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to identify where the checkout sheet is before explaining what teachers can borrow?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-018", "prompt": "The chef asked the students to combine the ingredients in a large bowl by stirring the flour, eggs, and milk together.\nAs used in the text, “combine” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "mix", "B": "count", "C": "freeze", "D": "deliver"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe sign listed three pool rules<u> walk on the deck, shower before swimming, and listen to lifeguards.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "rules: walk on the deck, shower before swimming, and listen to lifeguards.", "C": "rules walk: on the deck, shower before swimming, and listen to lifeguards.", "D": "rules, walk on the deck: shower before swimming, and listen to lifeguards."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-020", "prompt": "A class recorded the number of marbles in four jars.\nJar: A | B | C | D\nMarbles: 32 | 27 | 35 | 30\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Jar C has the most marbles.", "B": "Jar B has more marbles than Jar A.", "C": "Jar D and Jar C have the same number of marbles.", "D": "Jar A has fewer marbles than Jar B."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe safety poster was clear.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the poster was clear?", "choices": {"A": "The safety poster was clear because it was hanging in the hallway.", "B": "The safety poster was clear because it used large headings, simple pictures, and short steps for emergencies.", "C": "The safety poster was clear, and many students walked past it.", "D": "The safety poster was something the office displayed near the door."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nNeither the blue marker nor the black pen <u>were</u> inside the pencil pouch.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-023", "prompt": "Some dolphins hunt by working together. One dolphin may herd fish into a tight group while others wait nearby. This cooperation can make it easier for the dolphins to catch food.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some dolphins cooperate when hunting for food.", "B": "Dolphins never hunt near other dolphins.", "C": "Fish form groups only when humans feed them.", "D": "Dolphins cannot catch food in the ocean."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching writer Gary Soto, a student has taken these notes:\n• Gary Soto is an American poet and author.\n• He has written poems, essays, and books for young readers.\n• Many of his works describe family, childhood, and Mexican American life.\n• His writing often uses clear details from everyday experience.\nThe student wants to describe common subjects in Soto’s writing. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Gary Soto has written poems, essays, and books for young readers.", "B": "Gary Soto’s writing often describes family, childhood, Mexican American life, and everyday experiences.", "C": "Gary Soto is an American poet and author who has written many kinds of texts.", "D": "Some writers create works for both adults and young readers."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe museum rules ask visitors to silence phones, to stay behind barriers, and <u>speaking quietly</u> in exhibit rooms.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "to speak quietly", "C": "quietly speaking", "D": "visitors speak quietly"}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-026", "prompt": "A paragraph about bicycle helmets explains that foam inside the helmet can absorb some of the force from a fall. By reducing the force that reaches the head, the foam can lower the chance of injury.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains how helmet foam can help protect a rider.", "B": "It argues that helmets should never contain foam.", "C": "It describes why bicycles cannot be used safely.", "D": "It shows that foam increases the force of a fall."}}, {"id": "RW10-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching neighborhood block parties, a student has taken these notes:\n• Block parties are social events held on neighborhood streets or shared spaces.\n• Neighbors may bring food, games, or music.\n• Block parties can help residents meet one another.\n• Some block parties require permission from local officials.\nThe student wants to explain one benefit of block parties. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Block parties may include food, games, or music in a shared space.", "B": "Block parties can help neighbors meet one another during social events.", "C": "Some block parties are held on streets and require official permission.", "D": "Residents may attend events that happen in their neighborhoods."}}], [{"id": "RW11-M1-001", "prompt": "The campsite was remote, so the hikers had to drive on a dirt road for several miles before they reached it.\nAs used in the text, “remote” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "far away", "B": "recently built", "C": "easy to repair", "D": "crowded"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-002", "prompt": "Salamanders are amphibians that often live in moist places. Many salamanders breathe partly through their skin, so they need damp surroundings to keep their skin from drying out. This is one reason they are commonly found under logs, near streams, or in wet forests.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Salamanders often need damp places because of how they breathe.", "B": "Salamanders live only in hot deserts.", "C": "Logs and streams prevent salamanders from breathing.", "D": "All amphibians spend their entire lives underwater."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe stack of clean plates <u>were</u> placed beside the sink.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-004", "prompt": "The students finished interviewing their classmates. ______, they began organizing the responses into categories.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching water clocks, a student has taken these notes:\n• Water clocks were used to measure time before modern clocks.\n• Some water clocks had a container with a small opening.\n• Water flowed out of the opening at a steady rate.\n• Marks on the container could show how much time had passed.\nThe student wants to explain how some water clocks measured time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Water clocks were used before many modern clocks were invented.", "B": "Some water clocks measured time by letting water flow through a small opening at a steady rate past marks on a container.", "C": "A container can hold water and may have marks on its side.", "D": "People used different kinds of clocks to keep track of time."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-006", "prompt": "Luca had never used a sewing machine before. He read the directions carefully, asked his teacher where to place the fabric, and practiced on a scrap piece before starting his project.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Luca?", "choices": {"A": "He prepared carefully before using an unfamiliar machine.", "B": "He refused to read the directions.", "C": "He had already mastered every sewing skill.", "D": "He wanted to finish the project without help."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nDuring the school tour <u>the guide pointed out the music room and the cafeteria.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "During the school tour, the guide pointed out the music room and the cafeteria.", "C": "During, the school tour the guide pointed out the music room and the cafeteria.", "D": "During the school tour the guide, pointed out the music room and the cafeteria."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-008", "prompt": "A student recorded the number of tickets sold for a class raffle during four days.\nDay: Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday\nTickets sold: 24 | 31 | 28 | 36\nAccording to the table, how many more tickets were sold on Thursday than on Monday?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "8", "C": "12", "D": "36"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-009", "prompt": "A paragraph about shadow puppets explains that performers move flat figures between a light and a screen. When a figure is moved closer to the light, its shadow appears larger on the screen. This effect lets performers change how characters look during a scene.\nWhat is the main purpose of the detail about moving a figure closer to the light?", "choices": {"A": "To explain how shadow puppet performers can change a puppet’s appearance", "B": "To prove that shadow puppets cannot be used in stories", "C": "To describe why screens should be removed from performances", "D": "To show that puppets must always be made from glass"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-010", "prompt": "The directions for the board game were written in numbered steps with pictures beside each step. The pictures helped make the rules more ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "confusing", "B": "understandable", "C": "private", "D": "expensive"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe class planted flower seeds in paper cups <u>they placed the cups near the window.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "cups, they placed the cups near the window.", "C": "cups, and they placed the cups near the window.", "D": "cups they, placed the cups near the window."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nNext month, the drama club <u>performed</u> a short comedy for the school.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "performs", "C": "perform", "D": "will perform"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-013", "prompt": "Some students remember vocabulary words by making flashcards. ______, others draw small pictures beside each word to help them remember its meaning.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Similarly", "B": "Therefore", "C": "In conclusion", "D": "However"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-014", "prompt": "Before modern weather apps, many people listened to radio forecasts to learn about storms, temperatures, and changing conditions. Radio reports helped listeners plan travel, outdoor work, and school events.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Radio forecasts helped people plan for weather conditions before modern weather apps.", "B": "Weather apps were used long before radio was invented.", "C": "Radio reports made storms happen more often.", "D": "People listened to weather reports only to hear music."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching bamboo, a student has taken these notes:\n• Bamboo is a type of grass.\n• Some bamboo grows very quickly.\n• Bamboo stems can be strong and flexible.\n• People use bamboo to make furniture, flooring, baskets, and tools.\nThe student wants to describe why bamboo is useful as a material. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Bamboo is a type of grass, and some kinds grow quickly.", "B": "Bamboo is useful because its stems can be strong and flexible enough for furniture, flooring, baskets, and tools.", "C": "People have used plants for many purposes throughout history.", "D": "Some bamboo grows quickly in places where grasses can grow."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Wrapped in silver paper,</u> Harper placed the gift on the table.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Harper placed the gift wrapped in silver paper on the table.", "B": "Wrapped in silver paper, Harper placed the gift on the table.", "C": "Harper, wrapped in silver paper, placed the gift on the table.", "D": "The table placed Harper’s gift wrapped in silver paper."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a classroom calendar. The sentences are shown below.\n1. A classroom calendar helps students keep track of important dates.\n2. The calendar shows test days, project deadlines, and field trips.\n3. Students check the calendar at the start of each week.\n4. The calendar hangs beside the classroom door.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to identify the calendar’s location before explaining what it shows?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-018", "prompt": "The coach asked the runners to pause at the halfway mark so she could check their form before they continued.\nAs used in the text, “pause” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "stop briefly", "B": "run faster", "C": "turn around", "D": "speak loudly"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe <u>teachers</u> lounge was repainted during spring break.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "teacher’s", "C": "teachers’", "D": "teachers’s"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-020", "prompt": "A class recorded how many seeds sprouted in four trays.\nTray: A | B | C | D\nSeeds sprouted: 17 | 22 | 19 | 15\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Tray B had the most seeds sprout.", "B": "Tray D had more seeds sprout than Tray A.", "C": "Tray C and Tray B had the same number of seeds sprout.", "D": "Tray A had fewer seeds sprout than Tray D."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe school map was useful.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the school map was useful?", "choices": {"A": "The school map was useful because it was printed on paper.", "B": "The school map was useful because it labeled entrances, stairways, restrooms, and the main office.", "C": "The school map was useful, and students looked at it during the day.", "D": "The school map was something that appeared on a bulletin board."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nEither the green folder or the purple binder <u>are</u> on the back table.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "is", "C": "were", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-023", "prompt": "Many turtles have hard shells that help protect their bodies. When threatened, some turtles can pull their heads and legs partly inside the shell. This defense can make it harder for predators to harm them.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Turtle shells can help protect turtles from danger.", "B": "Turtles use shells mainly to catch fish.", "C": "Predators protect turtles by hiding inside shells.", "D": "All turtles leave their shells when they are threatened."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching author Mildred D. Taylor, a student has taken these notes:\n• Mildred D. Taylor is an American author.\n• Her novels often focus on the Logan family.\n• Her books explore family, courage, and injustice.\n• Roll of Thunder, Hear My Cry won the Newbery Medal.\nThe student wants to describe common themes in Taylor’s books. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Mildred D. Taylor is an American author whose novels often focus on one family.", "B": "Taylor’s books often explore family, courage, and injustice.", "C": "Roll of Thunder, Hear My Cry won the Newbery Medal.", "D": "The Logan family appears in several novels by Mildred D. Taylor."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe library rules ask students to return books on time, to use quiet voices, and <u>keeping food outside</u> the reading room.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "to keep food outside", "C": "food should stay outside", "D": "keeping outside food"}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-026", "prompt": "A paragraph about backpacks explains that padded shoulder straps can spread weight more evenly. This design may make a heavy backpack feel more comfortable to carry.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains a benefit of padded shoulder straps.", "B": "It argues that backpacks should never have straps.", "C": "It describes why students should carry all books by hand.", "D": "It shows that padding makes backpacks impossible to use."}}, {"id": "RW11-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching neighborhood tree-planting days, a student has taken these notes:\n• Tree-planting days bring residents together to plant young trees.\n• Volunteers may dig holes, water trees, or add mulch.\n• New trees can provide shade as they grow.\n• Events may teach residents how to care for trees.\nThe student wants to explain one benefit of neighborhood tree-planting days. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Tree-planting days may include digging holes, watering trees, and adding mulch.", "B": "Neighborhood tree-planting days can bring residents together and teach them how to care for trees that may later provide shade.", "C": "Young trees need water and mulch after they are planted.", "D": "Some neighborhood events take place outdoors with volunteers."}}], [{"id": "RW12-M1-001", "prompt": "The wall clock was accurate, so students could trust it to show the correct time before the bell rang.\nAs used in the text, “accurate” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "correct", "B": "heavy", "C": "silent", "D": "decorated"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-002", "prompt": "A river delta forms where a river drops sand and soil near the place it enters a larger body of water. Over time, the collected material can build new land. Deltas often become rich habitats for fish, birds, and plants.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "River deltas can form new land and support many living things.", "B": "River deltas are always found at the tops of mountains.", "C": "Rivers stop moving when they reach a delta.", "D": "Fish and birds prevent soil from collecting near rivers."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe <u>drivers</u> route changed because of road repairs.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "driver’s", "C": "drivers’", "D": "drivers’s"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-004", "prompt": "The class finished reading the short article. ______, students answered three questions about the author’s main point.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "After that", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-005", "prompt": "While researching compass roses, a student has taken these notes:\n• A compass rose is a symbol found on many maps.\n• It shows directions such as north, south, east, and west.\n• Some compass roses also show northeast, northwest, southeast, and southwest.\n• Compass roses help map readers understand direction.\nThe student wants to explain the purpose of a compass rose. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "A compass rose is a symbol that shows directions and helps people read maps.", "B": "Some maps include many kinds of symbols and labels.", "C": "North, south, east, and west are examples of directions.", "D": "Compass roses may be simple or include several direction labels."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-006", "prompt": "Rina had never entered a spelling bee before. She studied the word list each evening, asked her brother to quiz her, and practiced saying each word clearly before spelling it.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Rina?", "choices": {"A": "She prepared seriously for a new challenge.", "B": "She refused to study the word list.", "C": "She already knew every word before practicing.", "D": "She decided not to enter the spelling bee."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nBefore the first guest arrived <u>the students placed name tags on the table.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "Before the first guest arrived, the students placed name tags on the table.", "C": "Before, the first guest arrived the students placed name tags on the table.", "D": "Before the first guest arrived the students, placed name tags on the table."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-008", "prompt": "A student recorded the number of minutes four groups spent cleaning their assigned areas.\nGroup: A | B | C | D\nMinutes: 18 | 24 | 21 | 16\nAccording to the table, which group spent 24 minutes cleaning?", "choices": {"A": "Group A", "B": "Group B", "C": "Group C", "D": "Group D"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-009", "prompt": "A paragraph about animation explains that artists create the feeling of movement by showing a series of slightly different images very quickly. For example, a character’s hand may be drawn in a new position in each image.\nWhat is the main purpose of the example about the character’s hand?", "choices": {"A": "To show how small changes between images can create movement", "B": "To explain why animated characters cannot have hands", "C": "To prove that all animation is drawn on paper", "D": "To describe how artists choose music for films"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-010", "prompt": "The math teacher gave students a sample problem before the quiz. The example was meant to ______ how to use the new formula.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "demonstrate", "B": "hide", "C": "delay", "D": "borrow"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe team practiced for an hour <u>the coach gave everyone feedback.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "hour, the coach gave everyone feedback.", "C": "hour, and the coach gave everyone feedback.", "D": "hour the coach, gave everyone feedback."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-012", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nYesterday, the school band <u>plays</u> three songs at the assembly.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "play", "C": "played", "D": "will play"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-013", "prompt": "Some students choose books by reading the back cover. ______, others ask friends or teachers for recommendations.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Similarly", "B": "Therefore", "C": "In conclusion", "D": "Instead"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-014", "prompt": "Long ago, people used sundried bricks to build homes in dry regions. The bricks were made from mud and straw and then left in the sun to harden. This building material was useful because it could be made from materials found nearby.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Sundried bricks were a useful building material in some dry regions.", "B": "Sundried bricks were made from metal and glass.", "C": "People used sundried bricks only after modern machines were invented.", "D": "Mud and straw cannot be used to make building materials."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-015", "prompt": "While researching fireflies, a student has taken these notes:\n• Fireflies are insects.\n• Many fireflies produce light in their bodies.\n• Fireflies may use flashes of light to find mates.\n• Different firefly species can have different flashing patterns.\nThe student wants to explain one reason fireflies flash. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Fireflies are insects, and different species may live in different places.", "B": "Many fireflies produce flashes of light that can help them find mates.", "C": "Some insects are active at night and can be difficult to see.", "D": "Firefly species may be identified by scientists who study insects."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\n<u>Stacked in neat rows,</u> Noah placed the chairs against the wall.\nWhich choice best corrects the misplaced modifier in the sentence?", "choices": {"A": "Noah placed the chairs stacked in neat rows against the wall.", "B": "Stacked in neat rows, Noah placed the chairs against the wall.", "C": "Noah, stacked in neat rows, placed the chairs against the wall.", "D": "The wall placed Noah’s chairs stacked in neat rows."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-017", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about a class reading log. The sentences are shown below.\n1. A class reading log helps students keep track of what they read.\n2. Students write down each book’s title, author, and date completed.\n3. The teacher reviews the log every Friday.\n4. The log is kept in a folder on the teacher’s desk.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to tell where the log is kept before explaining what students write in it?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-018", "prompt": "The instructions said to rotate the handle clockwise until the lid clicked into place.\nAs used in the text, “rotate” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "turn", "B": "wash", "C": "count", "D": "describe"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe flyer listed three items to bring to the workshop<u> a notebook, a pencil, and a water bottle.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "workshop: a notebook, a pencil, and a water bottle.", "C": "workshop a notebook: a pencil, and a water bottle.", "D": "workshop, a notebook: a pencil, and a water bottle."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-020", "prompt": "A class recorded how many students chose each lunch option.\nLunch option: Pasta | Tacos | Soup | Salad\nStudents: 18 | 27 | 12 | 15\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Tacos were chosen by the most students.", "B": "Soup was chosen by more students than pasta.", "C": "Pasta and salad were chosen by the same number of students.", "D": "Salad was chosen by fewer students than soup."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-021", "prompt": "A student is revising the following sentence:\nThe school website is convenient.\nWhich revision gives the most specific explanation of why the website is convenient?", "choices": {"A": "The school website is convenient because many people can visit websites.", "B": "The school website is convenient because families can find bus routes, lunch menus, and event dates in one place.", "C": "The school website is convenient, and it has several pages.", "D": "The school website is something students may use during the school year."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nOne of the yellow folders <u>contain</u> the permission forms.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "contains", "C": "are containing", "D": "have contained"}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-023", "prompt": "Some horses flick their ears toward sounds. This movement can help them pay attention to a noise and can also show other horses what they are noticing. Ear movement is one way horses respond to their surroundings.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Horses can use ear movement to respond to sounds and surroundings.", "B": "Horses use their ears mainly to make loud sounds.", "C": "Ear movement prevents horses from noticing other animals.", "D": "All horses keep their ears still at all times."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-024", "prompt": "While researching artist Jacob Lawrence, a student has taken these notes:\n• Jacob Lawrence was an American painter.\n• He often painted scenes from African American history and everyday life.\n• His works used bold colors and clear shapes.\n• The Migration Series is one of his best-known works.\nThe student wants to describe Lawrence’s painting style. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Jacob Lawrence was an American painter with a well-known work called The Migration Series.", "B": "Jacob Lawrence’s paintings often used bold colors and clear shapes.", "C": "Jacob Lawrence painted scenes from history and everyday life.", "D": "The Migration Series is connected to African American history."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe coach reminded players to stretch before practice, to drink water, and <u>checking their equipment.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "to check their equipment.", "C": "equipment should be checked.", "D": "checking equipment carefully."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-026", "prompt": "A paragraph about mirrors explains that smooth glass with a reflective coating can bounce light back toward the viewer. This is why a person can see an image in a mirror.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains the result of light reflecting from a mirror.", "B": "It argues that mirrors should be made from rough paper.", "C": "It describes why viewers cannot see images in mirrors.", "D": "It shows that mirrors work without light."}}, {"id": "RW12-M1-027", "prompt": "While researching community seed exchanges, a student has taken these notes:\n• Seed exchanges are events where people share seeds.\n• Gardeners may bring vegetable, flower, or herb seeds.\n• Seed exchanges can help people try new plants.\n• Participants often label seeds with plant names and growing tips.\nThe student wants to explain one benefit of seed exchanges. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Seed exchanges may include vegetable, flower, or herb seeds.", "B": "Community seed exchanges can help gardeners try new plants by sharing seeds with one another.", "C": "Participants often label seeds with plant names and growing tips.", "D": "Some people attend events where gardeners bring seeds."}}]]}, {"id": "reading-writing-module-2", "label": "Module 2 (Harder)", "exams": [[{"id": "RW1-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “museum” may suggest a quiet building filled with objects from the past, but some museums are designed for active participation. At a science museum, visitors might test the strength of different materials, compare animal footprints, or use a model wind tunnel. These activities encourage visitors to learn by ______ rather than only by reading labels.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "guessing", "B": "competing", "C": "experimenting", "D": "remembering"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-002", "prompt": "In 1943, scientist Janaki Ammal worked on plant genetics at a research station in England. Her studies of sugarcane and other plants helped researchers better understand how plant traits can be passed from one generation to the next. Although she worked during a time when few women were widely recognized in science, Ammal made lasting contributions to botany.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Janaki Ammal’s work in plant genetics contributed to the field of botany.", "B": "Janaki Ammal studied only plants that grew in England.", "C": "Plant genetics became important because few people had studied sugarcane before 1943.", "D": "Botany is mainly concerned with recording the names of plants."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe guidebook, along with several maps and brochures, <u>were</u> placed on the hotel desk.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "are", "C": "was", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-004", "prompt": "In a salt marsh, grasses grow in muddy areas where fresh water and ocean water mix. These grasses slow moving water, which allows soil to settle instead of washing away. ______, salt marshes can help protect shorelines from erosion.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "In addition", "B": "As a result", "C": "By contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching the photographer Gordon Parks, a student has taken these notes:\n• Gordon Parks was a photographer, writer, and filmmaker.\n• He photographed everyday life in the United States during the 1900s.\n• His photographs often showed both hardship and dignity.\n• Parks later directed films, including Shaft in 1971.\nThe student wants to emphasize the range of Parks’s creative work. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Gordon Parks photographed everyday life in the United States and often showed hardship.", "B": "Gordon Parks had a wide creative career, working as a photographer, writer, filmmaker, and film director.", "C": "Gordon Parks directed Shaft in 1971 after taking photographs during the 1900s.", "D": "Gordon Parks’s photographs often showed dignity as well as hardship."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-006", "prompt": "When Priya joined the debate club, she often wrote long arguments but struggled to choose her strongest evidence. After several practices, she began using a checklist: Does this evidence clearly support my claim? Is it from a reliable source? Can I explain why it matters? Her speeches became shorter but more convincing.\nWhich choice best describes the main change in Priya’s debate preparation?", "choices": {"A": "She stopped using evidence and focused only on speaking loudly.", "B": "She learned to select and explain evidence more effectively.", "C": "She began writing longer speeches with more examples.", "D": "She decided that reliable sources were less important than opinions."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe students visited a historic theater <u>it had been restored by local volunteers.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "theater, it had been restored by local volunteers.", "C": "theater that had been restored by local volunteers.", "D": "theater, had been restored by local volunteers."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-008", "prompt": "A nature center counted visitors at four weekend programs.\nProgram: Bird Walk | Pond Study | Tree ID | Night Hike\nVisitors: 18 | 27 | 21 | 24\nWhich choice accurately compares the program attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Pond Study had 3 more visitors than Night Hike.", "B": "Bird Walk had more visitors than Tree ID.", "C": "Tree ID had the greatest number of visitors.", "D": "Night Hike had 9 fewer visitors than Bird Walk."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-009", "prompt": "In the early 1900s, archaeologist Zelia Nuttall studied ancient Mexican manuscripts and artifacts. She argued that these objects should be understood as evidence of complex cultures, not as curiosities. Her work helped change how some museums and scholars presented the history of the Americas.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains the viewpoint that guided Nuttall’s work.", "B": "It gives an unrelated example of an ancient artifact.", "C": "It argues that museums should stop displaying manuscripts.", "D": "It describes the exact materials used to make ancient objects."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-010", "prompt": "The school orchestra’s first rehearsal sounded uneven because each section had practiced separately. After the conductor asked the musicians to listen closely to one another, the melody and harmony began to ______ into a single, balanced sound.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "blend", "B": "wander", "C": "argue", "D": "vanish"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe mural was painted by three artists <u>who each used a different style,</u> bright geometric shapes, soft realistic portraits, and bold lettering.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "who each used a different style:", "C": "who each used a different style;", "D": "who each used a different style"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Theo finds an old recipe card tucked inside a library book. He notices that the handwriting matches the labels on jars in his grandmother’s kitchen. Instead of returning the card right away, he slips it carefully into his notebook and plans to ask his grandmother about it after school.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Theo?", "choices": {"A": "He thinks the recipe card may be connected to his family.", "B": "He wants to hide the recipe card from the librarian forever.", "C": "He believes the recipe card has no personal meaning.", "D": "He already knows exactly who wrote the recipe card."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about urban trees. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Urban trees can make city streets more comfortable in summer.\n2. Their leaves provide shade, and water evaporating from their surfaces can cool nearby air.\n3. Some neighborhoods have far fewer trees than others.\n4. For this reason, planting trees can be one way to reduce extreme heat in cities.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to introduce a problem before explaining one possible solution?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe ceramic bowls on the top shelf <u>belongs</u> to my grandfather.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "belong", "C": "belonging", "D": "has belonged"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-015", "prompt": "A teacher asked students to test which paper towel absorbed the most water. The students used the same amount of water for each towel and measured how much water remained after one minute.\nPaper towel: Brand A | Brand B | Brand C\nWater remaining: 12 mL | 8 mL | 15 mL\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Brand B absorbed the most water.", "B": "Brand C absorbed the most water.", "C": "Brand A and Brand B absorbed the same amount of water.", "D": "The students used different amounts of water for each towel."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nAlthough the hiking trail was steep, <u>but</u> the group reached the overlook before noon.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "and", "C": "so", "D": "DELETE the underlined portion"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe guide explained the rules for the community garden.\nThe student wants to add information that shows what the guide explained. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The guide explained the rules for the community garden, which is located near the school.", "B": "The guide explained the rules for the community garden, including where to plant vegetables, how to share tools, and when to water.", "C": "The guide explained the rules for the community garden, and the garden has several raised beds.", "D": "The guide explained the rules for the community garden, which many neighbors enjoy visiting."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-018", "prompt": "In 2019, engineers tested a new type of pavement that could allow rainwater to pass through tiny spaces in its surface. In areas with heavy storms, this pavement might reduce puddles and help water reach the ground instead of flowing immediately into streets.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains a possible benefit of the pavement.", "B": "It lists the materials used to make the pavement.", "C": "It argues that ordinary streets are always unsafe.", "D": "It shows that engineers stopped testing the pavement."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nBefore the museum opened, the workers arranged the paintings, cleaned the glass cases, and <u>the lights were checked.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "checked the lights.", "C": "the checking of the lights happened.", "D": "they were checking the lights."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-020", "prompt": "Some animals use camouflage to avoid being seen. The walking stick insect, for example, has a long, thin body that resembles a twig. When it remains still among branches, predators may overlook it.\nAs used in the text, “resembles” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "carries", "B": "looks like", "C": "breaks into", "D": "searches for"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching the history of lunch counters in the United States, a student has taken these notes:\n• In 1960, four college students began a sit-in at a lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina.\n• The lunch counter refused to serve Black customers.\n• The students remained seated peacefully after being denied service.\n• The Greensboro sit-in inspired similar protests in other cities.\nThe student wants to explain the importance of the Greensboro sit-in. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "In 1960, four college students sat at a lunch counter in Greensboro, North Carolina.", "B": "The Greensboro sit-in was important because the students’ peaceful protest against unfair service inspired similar protests in other cities.", "C": "Some lunch counters in 1960 refused to serve Black customers.", "D": "The students in Greensboro remained seated after being denied service at a lunch counter."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nDuring the class discussion, Lena explained her idea clearly, listened to other students respectfully, and <u>was asking</u> thoughtful questions.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "asks", "C": "asked", "D": "had asked"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-023", "prompt": "At first, the new map of the school looked simple: classrooms were marked in blue, offices in green, and exits in red. ______, the map also included small symbols for elevators, water fountains, and wheelchair-accessible entrances.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "However", "B": "In addition", "C": "In contrast", "D": "Nevertheless"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about a local river cleanup. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by showing why the cleanup mattered?", "choices": {"A": "The river cleanup was held on a Saturday morning in early May.", "B": "Many volunteers wore gloves and carried large bags during the cleanup.", "C": "Before the cleanup, plastic bottles and food wrappers had collected along the riverbank where birds and fish live.", "D": "The cleanup ended when the volunteers returned their supplies to a storage shed."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe poet Joy Harjo, <u>who became the first Native American United States poet laureate in 2019</u> writes about memory, music, and history.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "who became the first Native American United States poet laureate in 2019,", "C": "who, became the first Native American United States poet laureate in 2019", "D": "who became the first Native American United States poet laureate, in 2019"}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying coral reefs have found that some fish make small sounds while defending territory or attracting mates. These sounds are not as loud as whale songs, but they can still be detected with underwater microphones. The discovery suggests that reef environments may be noisier and more socially active than many people assume.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some reef fish produce sounds, suggesting that coral reef environments may be more active than previously assumed.", "B": "Whale songs are the loudest sounds that can be heard with underwater microphones.", "C": "Coral reefs are quiet places because most fish do not defend territory.", "D": "Researchers study coral reefs only to compare fish sounds with whale songs."}}, {"id": "RW1-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching school composting programs, a student has taken these notes:\n• Composting turns food scraps into material that can improve soil.\n• Some schools collect fruit peels and vegetable scraps from cafeterias.\n• Compost bins must be managed so they do not attract pests.\n• Finished compost can be used in school gardens.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of school composting. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Some schools collect fruit peels and vegetable scraps from cafeterias for composting.", "B": "Composting can turn cafeteria food scraps into material for school gardens, but compost bins must be managed carefully to avoid attracting pests.", "C": "Finished compost can be used in school gardens after food scraps are collected.", "D": "Compost bins are sometimes used by schools that have cafeterias and gardens."}}], [{"id": "RW2-M2-001", "prompt": "When the student council first suggested replacing plastic event banners with reusable cloth ones, some members worried that cloth banners would cost more at the beginning. After comparing prices, however, the council found that the cloth banners could be used for many events. Over time, the reusable option would be more ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "colorful", "B": "economical", "C": "delicate", "D": "confusing"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-002", "prompt": "In the 1930s, artist Augusta Savage opened an art school in Harlem, a neighborhood in New York City. She taught young artists and helped them find chances to show their work. Savage’s own sculptures were important, but her support of other artists also shaped the Harlem arts community.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Augusta Savage influenced the Harlem arts community through both her sculptures and her support of other artists.", "B": "Augusta Savage opened an art school because she wanted to stop making sculptures.", "C": "Harlem artists in the 1930s usually worked without showing their art to anyone.", "D": "Augusta Savage became known mainly because she moved away from New York City."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe notebook containing sketches, ticket stubs, and old letters <u>belong</u> to my cousin.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "belongs", "C": "belonging", "D": "have belonged"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-004", "prompt": "The first weather balloons carried simple instruments and could rise high above the ground. ______, modern weather balloons can send temperature, humidity, and pressure data back to scientists while the balloons are still in the air.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Today", "B": "Instead", "C": "Nevertheless", "D": "For this reason"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching the writer Zitkala-Sa, a student has taken these notes:\n• Zitkala-Sa was a Yankton Dakota writer, musician, and activist.\n• She wrote essays and stories about Native American life and rights.\n• She also worked to protect Native American citizenship and voting rights.\n• Her opera The Sun Dance was first performed in 1913.\nThe student wants to emphasize that Zitkala-Sa worked in several different ways to express ideas and support rights. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Zitkala-Sa wrote essays and stories and also worked as an activist for Native American citizenship and voting rights.", "B": "Zitkala-Sa’s opera The Sun Dance was first performed in 1913.", "C": "Zitkala-Sa wrote about Native American life in stories and essays.", "D": "Zitkala-Sa was a musician whose opera was performed in the early twentieth century."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-006", "prompt": "A city installed a row of benches along a busy sidewalk. At first, officials expected the benches to serve mainly as resting places. After several months, however, they noticed that people also used the benches as meeting spots and that nearby shops had more visitors. The benches had effects beyond their original purpose.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A simple city improvement can have several positive effects.", "B": "Busy sidewalks should never include benches.", "C": "Shops receive fewer visitors when people have places to sit.", "D": "City officials usually know every effect of a project before it begins."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nTara wanted to join the coding club <u>she had never written a computer program before.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "club, although she had never written a computer program before.", "C": "club she, although had never written a computer program before.", "D": "club, she had never written a computer program before."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-008", "prompt": "A library tracked attendance at four teen workshops.\nWorkshop: Podcasting | Animation | Creative Writing | Photography\nAttendance: 16 | 19 | 14 | 22\nWhich choice accurately compares the workshop attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Photography had 3 more attendees than Animation.", "B": "Creative Writing had 2 more attendees than Podcasting.", "C": "Podcasting had the greatest attendance.", "D": "Animation had 5 fewer attendees than Creative Writing."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-009", "prompt": "In 1965, chemist Stephanie Kwolek helped develop a lightweight fiber that was extremely strong. The material, later called Kevlar, became useful in many products, including protective equipment and cables. Kwolek’s discovery shows how laboratory research can lead to practical materials.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It gives examples of how Kwolek’s discovery was used.", "B": "It explains why Kwolek stopped working as a chemist.", "C": "It lists the steps Kwolek followed in the laboratory.", "D": "It argues that cables are more important than protective equipment."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-010", "prompt": "In the school play, the actor playing the king first spoke in a proud, booming voice. Later, after the character lost the trust of his people, the actor lowered his voice and slowed his movements. These changes helped ______ the character’s sadness and defeat.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "conceal", "B": "convey", "C": "prevent", "D": "question"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe article explained three causes of the traffic delay<u> a fallen tree, road repairs, and a power outage.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "delay: a fallen tree, road repairs, and a power outage.", "C": "delay a fallen tree: road repairs, and a power outage.", "D": "delay, a fallen tree: road repairs, and a power outage."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Nina is preparing to move to a new apartment. She carefully packs her books and clothes, but she leaves one empty shoebox on her desk. Before going to sleep, she places inside it a bus ticket, a photo of her old building, and a key that no longer opens any door.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Nina?", "choices": {"A": "She wants to keep small objects that remind her of the place she is leaving.", "B": "She is planning to throw away all reminders of her old apartment.", "C": "She believes the key will unlock her new apartment.", "D": "She has forgotten to pack her books and clothes."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about classroom discussions. The sentences are shown below.\n1. In a good classroom discussion, students do more than state their own opinions.\n2. They also ask follow-up questions and connect their comments to what others have said.\n3. This kind of listening can help the group build a stronger understanding of the topic.\n4. Some discussions take place in science class, while others happen in history or English class.\nWhere should sentence 4 be placed if the writer wants to mention where discussions can happen before explaining what makes them effective?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 3"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nWhen the visitors entered the aquarium, <u>they</u> saw a tank filled with jellyfish that glowed under blue light.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "it", "C": "them", "D": "those"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-015", "prompt": "A science class measured how far three toy cars rolled after being released from the same ramp.\nCar: A | B | C\nDistance rolled: 210 cm | 185 cm | 240 cm\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Car C rolled the farthest.", "B": "Car B rolled farther than Car A.", "C": "All three cars rolled exactly 210 cm.", "D": "Car A rolled 55 cm farther than Car C."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe science club’s goal is <u>to observe local insects, recording their behaviors, and to share the results</u> with younger students.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "observing local insects, to record their behaviors, and sharing the results", "C": "to observe local insects, to record their behaviors, and to share the results", "D": "local insects are observed, their behaviors recorded, and the results are shared"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe documentary showed that the island community prepared carefully for storms.\nThe student wants to add information that supports the idea that the community prepared carefully. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The documentary showed that the island community prepared carefully for storms by practicing evacuation routes, storing emergency supplies, and checking radio systems.", "B": "The documentary showed that the island community prepared carefully for storms, and the island has beaches and fishing boats.", "C": "The documentary showed that storms can happen near islands in some seasons.", "D": "The documentary showed that the island community was located far from a large city."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-018", "prompt": "Some historians study old restaurant menus to learn about the past. Menus can reveal which foods were common, how much meals cost, and what words restaurants used to attract customers. Although menus may seem ordinary, they can provide clues about daily life.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains what kinds of historical information menus can provide.", "B": "It argues that menus are more valuable than all other historical sources.", "C": "It describes how restaurants print menus today.", "D": "It shows why historians avoid studying common objects."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe lanterns hanging above the stage <u>was</u> shaped like stars.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-020", "prompt": "The word “archive” can refer to a place where records are stored, but it can also refer to a collection of records itself. A town archive might include photographs, letters, and maps that help residents learn about earlier generations.\nAs used in the text, “collection” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "group", "B": "payment", "C": "prediction", "D": "performance"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching the invention of Braille, a student has taken these notes:\n• Louis Braille was born in France in 1809.\n• As a child, Braille lost his sight after an accident.\n• He developed a reading and writing system using raised dots.\n• The system allowed many people who are blind to read by touch.\nThe student wants to explain why Braille’s system was important. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Louis Braille was born in France in 1809 and lost his sight as a child.", "B": "Braille developed a system of raised dots that allowed many people who are blind to read by touch.", "C": "Braille’s system was created after he had an accident during childhood.", "D": "A reading and writing system can be used by people in many countries."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nTo prepare for the tournament, the chess team reviewed openings, solved practice puzzles, and <u>they studied</u> famous games.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "studying", "C": "studied", "D": "to study"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-023", "prompt": "The glass artist first gathers hot glass on the end of a metal pipe. ______, the artist shapes the softened glass by blowing into the pipe and turning it carefully.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "Similarly", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about a school policy that asks students to keep phones in backpacks during class. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining the purpose of the policy?", "choices": {"A": "The policy about phones was announced by the school principal on Monday morning.", "B": "Some students own phones with cases in different colors and designs.", "C": "The school asks students to keep phones in backpacks during class so lessons can continue with fewer distractions.", "D": "Students usually place backpacks beside their desks or under their chairs."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe novel’s final chapter, <u>which reveals why the main character left home</u> gives readers an answer to a question raised at the beginning of the story.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which reveals why the main character left home,", "C": "which, reveals why the main character left home", "D": "which reveals why the main character left home;"}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-026", "prompt": "A team of researchers placed small sensors on sea turtles to track their movements. The sensors showed that some turtles traveled along similar ocean routes year after year. The data helped researchers better understand how the turtles navigate and which areas may be important to protect.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Sensors helped researchers learn about sea turtle travel routes and possible protection areas.", "B": "Sea turtles avoid traveling through the same ocean routes more than once.", "C": "Researchers placed sensors on sea turtles to change the turtles’ swimming patterns.", "D": "Sea turtles can navigate only when researchers are nearby."}}, {"id": "RW2-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching community murals, a student has taken these notes:\n• Community murals are large artworks painted in public spaces.\n• Artists often work with local residents to choose themes.\n• Murals can celebrate neighborhood history or shared values.\n• Paint on outdoor murals may fade and need repairs over time.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a challenge of community murals. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Community murals are large artworks that are painted in public spaces by artists.", "B": "Community murals can celebrate neighborhood history or shared values, but outdoor paint may fade and require repairs.", "C": "Artists often work with local residents before painting community murals.", "D": "Outdoor murals are different from indoor artworks because they are in public spaces."}}], [{"id": "RW3-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “digital” is often used to describe technology such as tablets and computers. However, historians also use “digital archive” to describe a collection of scanned documents or photographs that people can view online. In this context, “digital” means that the materials are available in an electronic ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "format", "B": "argument", "C": "habit", "D": "decoration"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-002", "prompt": "In the 1920s, Bessie Coleman became the first African American woman and first Native American woman to earn a pilot’s license. Because flight schools in the United States would not admit her, Coleman studied in France. After returning to the United States, she performed in air shows and encouraged other Black Americans to learn about aviation.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Bessie Coleman overcame barriers to become a pilot and inspired others to consider aviation.", "B": "Bessie Coleman studied in France because she wanted to stop performing in air shows.", "C": "Flight schools in the United States admitted all students equally in the 1920s.", "D": "Aviation became popular only after Bessie Coleman earned a pilot’s license."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe set of measuring cups and spoons <u>are</u> stored in the top drawer.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "is", "C": "were", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-004", "prompt": "Early movie theaters often used live musicians to provide sound during silent films. ______, theaters later installed equipment that allowed recorded voices, music, and effects to play with the movie.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Later", "B": "Therefore", "C": "In other words", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching the mathematician Katherine Johnson, a student has taken these notes:\n• Katherine Johnson worked for NASA.\n• She calculated flight paths for space missions.\n• Her calculations helped support the first U.S. human spaceflight and later moon missions.\n• She was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2015.\nThe student wants to explain Johnson’s role in space exploration. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Katherine Johnson received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2015.", "B": "Katherine Johnson worked for NASA and calculated flight paths that helped support important space missions.", "C": "NASA has supported many space missions, including the first U.S. human spaceflight.", "D": "Katherine Johnson was one of many people whose work became better known over time."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-006", "prompt": "At first, residents complained that the empty lot near the bus stop was an eyesore. A neighborhood group then planted native flowers, added a bench, and painted a small sign explaining the plants. Within months, people were stopping there to rest, take photos, and watch butterflies. The project changed not only the lot’s appearance but also how people used the space.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A neighborhood project transformed an unused lot into a more welcoming public space.", "B": "Bus stops should always be moved away from empty lots.", "C": "Native flowers prevent people from sitting outdoors.", "D": "Neighborhood groups usually plant flowers only for scientific research."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe scientist compared two soil samples <u>one came from a forest, and the other came from a farm.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "samples; one came from a forest, and the other came from a farm.", "C": "samples one came from a forest, and the other came from a farm.", "D": "samples, one came from a forest and the other, came from a farm."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-008", "prompt": "A youth center tracked attendance at four afternoon activities.\nActivity: Robotics Lab | Mural Design | Cooking Basics | Board Games\nAttendance: 24 | 18 | 21 | 27\nWhich choice accurately compares the activity attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Board Games had 3 more attendees than Robotics Lab.", "B": "Mural Design had more attendees than Cooking Basics.", "C": "Cooking Basics had the greatest attendance.", "D": "Robotics Lab had 6 fewer attendees than Mural Design."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-009", "prompt": "In the 1800s, botanist Marianne North traveled to many parts of the world and painted plants in their natural surroundings. Instead of showing flowers alone against a plain background, her paintings often included nearby trees, rocks, insects, or buildings. This approach helped viewers understand the environments in which the plants grew.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains how North’s paintings differed from some other botanical images.", "B": "It argues that plants should never be painted with insects nearby.", "C": "It describes the supplies North used to paint flowers.", "D": "It shows that North painted only buildings and rocks."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-010", "prompt": "The editor liked the article’s facts but said its ending felt abrupt. To make the conclusion more effective, the writer added a final sentence that connected the article’s main points and left readers with a clear takeaway.\nAs used in the text, “abrupt” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "ending too suddenly", "B": "containing too many details", "C": "written in formal language", "D": "arranged in alphabetical order"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe student’s report described three methods for saving water at home<u> fixing leaks, taking shorter showers, and collecting rainwater for plants.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "home: fixing leaks, taking shorter showers, and collecting rainwater for plants.", "C": "home fixing leaks: taking shorter showers, and collecting rainwater for plants.", "D": "home, fixing leaks: taking shorter showers, and collecting rainwater for plants."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Malik finds a cracked picture frame in a box of things from his old room. He starts to throw it away, then notices a tiny sticker on the back from a museum trip he took with his father. Malik wipes the dust from the frame and sets it carefully on his desk.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Malik?", "choices": {"A": "He decides the frame has personal value because it reminds him of a shared experience.", "B": "He wants to forget every object connected to his old room.", "C": "He believes the cracked frame is worth a large amount of money.", "D": "He plans to return the frame to the museum."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about school announcements. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Clear school announcements help students know what events are happening and where they need to go.\n2. Announcements that include dates, locations, and simple directions are usually easier to follow.\n3. Some announcements are read aloud in the morning, while others are posted online.\n4. When announcements leave out important details, students may miss meetings or arrive at the wrong place.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to identify different announcement formats before explaining what makes announcements clear?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nSeveral pages from the instruction manual <u>was</u> missing from the box.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-015", "prompt": "A science class measured how much water three plants lost through their leaves during one hour.\nPlant: A | B | C\nWater lost: 6 mL | 11 mL | 8 mL\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Plant B lost the most water.", "B": "Plant A lost more water than Plant C.", "C": "All three plants lost the same amount of water.", "D": "Plant C lost 3 mL more water than Plant B."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe best way to prepare for the lab is <u>reading the directions, to gather the materials, and checking the safety rules.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "to read the directions, to gather the materials, and to check the safety rules.", "C": "the directions are read, the materials gathered, and the safety rules checked.", "D": "reading the directions, gathering the materials, and to check the safety rules."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe exhibit showed how people repaired clothing in the past.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of repair methods. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The exhibit showed how people repaired clothing in the past by patching worn fabric, replacing buttons, and restitching torn seams.", "B": "The exhibit showed how people repaired clothing in the past, and clothing is made from many materials.", "C": "The exhibit showed how people in the past sometimes owned fewer clothes than people do today.", "D": "The exhibit was located in a small room near the museum entrance."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-018", "prompt": "Some engineers study termite mounds because the structures can stay cooler inside than the air outside. By observing how air moves through the mounds, engineers hope to design buildings that use less energy for cooling. Nature can sometimes offer ideas for solving human design problems.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains how studying termite mounds may help engineers design better buildings.", "B": "It argues that termite mounds should be copied exactly in every city.", "C": "It describes the food termites eat inside their mounds.", "D": "It shows that engineers cannot solve problems without insects."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nBefore the science demonstration, the teacher measured the liquid, placed the beaker on the table, and <u>the timer was started.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "started the timer.", "C": "the starting of the timer happened.", "D": "was starting the timer."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-020", "prompt": "The librarian placed the rare manuscript in a protective case to preserve it. The case blocked dust and light that could damage the paper over time.\nAs used in the text, “preserve” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "protect", "B": "borrow", "C": "describe", "D": "copy"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching the history of paper, a student has taken these notes:\n• Paper was invented in China more than 1,800 years ago.\n• Early paper could be made from materials such as bark, cloth, and plant fibers.\n• Paper was lighter and easier to store than many earlier writing surfaces.\n• The spread of paper helped make written records more convenient to keep.\nThe student wants to explain one advantage of paper over some earlier writing surfaces. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Paper was invented in China more than 1,800 years ago.", "B": "Early paper could be made from bark, cloth, and plant fibers.", "C": "Paper was lighter and easier to store than many earlier writing surfaces, making written records more convenient to keep.", "D": "The materials used to make early paper included plant fibers and cloth."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe volunteers sorted the books into boxes, labeled each box by genre, and <u>they loaded</u> the boxes onto a cart.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "loading", "C": "loaded", "D": "to load"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-023", "prompt": "The baker chilled the dough for one hour before shaping it. ______, she placed the shaped dough on trays and let it rise.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "As a result", "D": "In contrast"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about the value of learning a second language. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a broad benefit?", "choices": {"A": "Some students begin learning a second language in middle school.", "B": "A second-language class may meet several times each week.", "C": "Learning a second language can help students communicate with more people and understand different cultures.", "D": "Many languages use letters, accents, or sounds that students may need to practice."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe town clock tower, <u>which was built more than one hundred years ago</u> still rings every hour.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which was built more than one hundred years ago,", "C": "which, was built more than one hundred years ago", "D": "which was built more than one hundred years ago;"}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-026", "prompt": "A group of researchers studied how different surfaces affect city temperatures. They found that light-colored roofs reflected more sunlight than dark roofs. The finding suggests that roof color can influence how much heat a building absorbs.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Light-colored roofs may help buildings absorb less heat by reflecting more sunlight.", "B": "Dark roofs reflect more sunlight than all other surfaces in a city.", "C": "Researchers study roof color mainly to decide which colors look best.", "D": "City temperatures cannot be affected by building materials."}}, {"id": "RW3-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching school gardens, a student has taken these notes:\n• School gardens can give students hands-on experience growing plants.\n• Gardens require regular watering, weeding, and seasonal planning.\n• Some classes use garden observations during science lessons.\n• Food grown in gardens may be used in cooking activities.\nThe student wants to present both an educational benefit and a responsibility of school gardens. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "School gardens can be used during science lessons, but they require regular watering, weeding, and planning.", "B": "Food grown in school gardens may be used in cooking activities after plants are watered.", "C": "School gardens are outdoor spaces where students can grow many kinds of plants.", "D": "Some classes observe gardens during science lessons and other classes cook with food."}}], [{"id": "RW4-M2-001", "prompt": "At first, the word “network” might make people think only of computers. But a network can also be any connected system. A subway map, for example, shows a network because it shows how stations and train lines are ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "connected", "B": "painted", "C": "delayed", "D": "hidden"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-002", "prompt": "In the late 1800s, journalist Ida B. Wells wrote articles about injustice and used evidence to challenge false claims. Her reporting was often dangerous work, but it helped bring national attention to problems many people wanted to ignore.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Ida B. Wells used journalism to expose injustice despite serious risks.", "B": "Ida B. Wells wrote mainly about the safest topics of her time.", "C": "Newspapers in the late 1800s refused to publish evidence.", "D": "Ida B. Wells became famous because she stopped writing articles."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe list of emergency numbers and office extensions <u>are</u> posted beside the phone.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "is", "C": "were", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-004", "prompt": "The first version of the app allowed users to record short notes. ______, the updated version lets users add photos, organize notes into folders, and search by keyword.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "By comparison", "B": "As a result", "C": "For instance", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching the artist and architect Maya Lin, a student has taken these notes:\n• Maya Lin designed the Vietnam Veterans Memorial in Washington, DC.\n• The memorial is made of polished black stone.\n• The stone surface reflects the people who visit it.\n• Lin has also created sculptures and environmental artworks.\nThe student wants to emphasize how one feature of the Vietnam Veterans Memorial affects visitors. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Maya Lin designed the Vietnam Veterans Memorial and later created other kinds of art.", "B": "The Vietnam Veterans Memorial is in Washington, DC, and was designed by Maya Lin.", "C": "The memorial’s polished black stone reflects visitors, making them visually part of the memorial.", "D": "Lin has created sculptures, environmental artworks, and a memorial in Washington, DC."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-006", "prompt": "When a new sidewalk was built near the community center, city workers also added curb cuts, which are small ramps at street corners. The ramps helped people using wheelchairs, but they also helped parents pushing strollers and travelers pulling suitcases. The change showed that a design meant to improve access for one group can make a space easier for many people to use.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "An accessibility feature can benefit many different users.", "B": "Curb cuts are useful only inside community centers.", "C": "Sidewalks become harder to use when ramps are added.", "D": "Parents with strollers are the only people who use curb cuts."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe researcher interviewed twenty students <u>each student answered the same five questions.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "students; each student answered the same five questions.", "C": "students each student; answered the same five questions.", "D": "students, each student answered, the same five questions."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-008", "prompt": "A museum counted visitors to four afternoon demonstrations.\nDemonstration: Weaving | Metalworking | Paper Marbling | Printmaking\nVisitors: 31 | 26 | 34 | 29\nWhich choice accurately compares the demonstration attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Paper Marbling had 3 more visitors than Weaving.", "B": "Metalworking had more visitors than Printmaking.", "C": "Weaving had the fewest visitors.", "D": "Printmaking had 5 more visitors than Paper Marbling."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-009", "prompt": "Astronomer Cecilia Payne-Gaposchkin studied light from stars and concluded that stars are made mostly of hydrogen and helium. At first, some scientists doubted her conclusion because it differed from common ideas of the time. Later research supported her finding, and it became a major part of modern astronomy.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains why Payne-Gaposchkin’s conclusion was not immediately accepted.", "B": "It lists the tools Payne-Gaposchkin used to study stars.", "C": "It argues that later research disproved Payne-Gaposchkin’s work.", "D": "It describes how hydrogen and helium are used on Earth."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-010", "prompt": "The school newspaper article included many facts about the new cafeteria menu, but the writer wanted readers to understand why the change mattered. She added quotes from students who said the menu gave them more choices. The quotes helped ______ the article’s main point.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "reinforce", "B": "erase", "C": "postpone", "D": "confuse"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe workshop covered three basic photography skills<u> framing a subject, adjusting light, and choosing a background.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "skills: framing a subject, adjusting light, and choosing a background.", "C": "skills framing: a subject, adjusting light, and choosing a background.", "D": "skills, framing a subject: adjusting light, and choosing a background."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Leila finds a packet of flower seeds in the pocket of a coat that belonged to her aunt. The packet is faded, but Leila can still read the name of the flower. She places the seeds on the windowsill and begins searching online for planting instructions.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Leila?", "choices": {"A": "She is interested in growing the flowers as a way to connect with her aunt.", "B": "She plans to throw the seeds away because the packet is faded.", "C": "She already knows that the seeds cannot grow.", "D": "She found the seeds in a garden store earlier that day."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about public art. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Public art can make ordinary places feel more interesting and memorable.\n2. A sculpture in a train station or a painted wall near a playground may catch people’s attention during daily routines.\n3. Some public artworks are temporary, while others remain for many years.\n4. Because people encounter public art outside museums, it can reach viewers who might not plan a special trip to see art.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to mention how long public art may last before explaining where people encounter it?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe boxes of donated pencils and markers <u>was</u> delivered to the art room.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how many paper clips different magnets could lift.\nMagnet: X | Y | Z\nPaper clips lifted: 18 | 24 | 21\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Magnet Y lifted the most paper clips.", "B": "Magnet X lifted more paper clips than Magnet Z.", "C": "Magnet Z lifted 6 more paper clips than Magnet Y.", "D": "All three magnets lifted the same number of paper clips."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe club’s plan is <u>to collect old uniforms, washing them, and to donate them</u> to younger players.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "collecting old uniforms, to wash them, and donating them", "C": "to collect old uniforms, to wash them, and to donate them", "D": "old uniforms are collected, washed, and donated"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe article explains how the bakery reduces waste.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of how the bakery reduces waste. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The article explains how the bakery reduces waste by donating unsold bread, using fruit peels in flavored syrups, and composting coffee grounds.", "B": "The article explains how the bakery reduces waste, and the bakery opens early each morning.", "C": "The article explains that many bakeries sell bread, pastries, and drinks.", "D": "The article explains how the bakery is located near a busy corner downtown."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-018", "prompt": "Some linguists study how new slang words spread. A word may first be used by a small group, then appear in songs, videos, or online posts, and eventually become familiar to many speakers. Studying this process can show how language changes through social contact.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It describes a possible path by which a slang word becomes widely known.", "B": "It argues that slang words should never be used in songs or videos.", "C": "It explains why language never changes over time.", "D": "It lists every slang word that linguists have studied."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe students in the front row <u>was</u> asked to pass the handouts backward.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-020", "prompt": "A city planner described the park’s design as flexible because the open field could be used for soccer games, outdoor movies, school events, or quiet reading.\nAs used in the text, “flexible” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "able to serve different purposes", "B": "made from soft materials", "C": "difficult to enter", "D": "hidden from public view"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching the geologist Florence Bascom, a student has taken these notes:\n• Florence Bascom was one of the first American women to earn a PhD in geology.\n• She created detailed maps of rock formations.\n• Her fieldwork helped explain the geologic history of parts of the eastern United States.\n• Bascom also taught and trained many geology students.\nThe student wants to explain Bascom’s contributions to geology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Florence Bascom contributed to geology by mapping rock formations, explaining regional geologic history, and training students.", "B": "Florence Bascom was one of the first American women to earn a PhD in geology.", "C": "Geology students often study maps of rock formations in the eastern United States.", "D": "Florence Bascom completed fieldwork after earning an advanced degree."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nDuring the fundraiser, volunteers greeted guests, served drinks, and <u>they sold</u> raffle tickets.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "selling", "C": "sold", "D": "to sell"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-023", "prompt": "The carpenter measured the wood twice before cutting it. ______, she made a pencil mark exactly where the cut should begin.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "Nevertheless", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about why schools should teach basic personal finance. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a practical benefit?", "choices": {"A": "Personal finance lessons can help students understand saving, budgeting, and responsible spending.", "B": "Some students carry wallets, while others use backpacks with small pockets.", "C": "A personal finance class might be held in a room near the library.", "D": "Many schools offer classes in math, science, history, and English."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-025", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe neighborhood pool, <u>which opens every summer after Memorial Day</u> offers swimming lessons for beginners.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which opens every summer after Memorial Day,", "C": "which, opens every summer after Memorial Day", "D": "which opens every summer after Memorial Day;"}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying penguins used small cameras to observe how the birds hunt underwater. The footage showed that some penguins swim together when chasing fish, which may help them guide prey into tighter groups. The study gave researchers a closer view of hunting behavior that is difficult to watch from the surface.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Cameras helped researchers learn more about how some penguins hunt underwater.", "B": "Penguins cannot swim together while chasing fish.", "C": "Researchers can easily watch all penguin behavior from the surface.", "D": "Penguins use cameras to guide fish into tighter groups."}}, {"id": "RW4-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching teen volunteer programs, a student has taken these notes:\n• Teen volunteer programs can help students learn responsibility.\n• Some programs require regular attendance and training.\n• Volunteers may help at animal shelters, libraries, or community events.\n• Students can gain experience while supporting local organizations.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and an expectation of teen volunteer programs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Teen volunteer programs may take place at animal shelters, libraries, or community events.", "B": "Teen volunteer programs can help students gain experience and learn responsibility, but some require regular attendance and training.", "C": "Some local organizations accept volunteers who are teenagers.", "D": "Students who volunteer may help in several different places in their community."}}], [{"id": "RW5-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “current” can describe something happening now, but in science it can also refer to the movement of water, air, or electricity. In a river, a strong current can carry leaves, sand, and small branches downstream because the water is ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "moving", "B": "freezing", "C": "disappearing", "D": "measuring"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-002", "prompt": "In the early 1900s, dancer and choreographer Pearl Primus studied dances from different cultures and brought some of those traditions to American stages. She believed dance could entertain audiences while also teaching them about history and human experience. Her work helped expand ideas about what concert dance could express.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Pearl Primus used dance to entertain audiences and communicate cultural and historical ideas.", "B": "Pearl Primus believed that dance should avoid teaching audiences anything.", "C": "American stages in the early 1900s presented only one style of dance.", "D": "Pearl Primus became known because she stopped studying cultural traditions."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe packet of permission slips and information forms <u>were</u> sent home with students on Friday.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-004", "prompt": "The earliest roller skates had wheels arranged in a single line, which made turning difficult. ______, later skates often placed wheels in two pairs, giving skaters more balance and control.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "By contrast", "B": "For this reason", "C": "In other words", "D": "As a result"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching microbiologist Alice Catherine Evans, a student has taken these notes:\n• Alice Catherine Evans studied bacteria in milk.\n• She found that certain bacteria in raw milk could cause illness.\n• Her research helped support the use of pasteurization.\n• Pasteurization heats milk to kill harmful bacteria.\nThe student wants to explain the public health importance of Evans’s research. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Alice Catherine Evans studied bacteria in milk and helped support pasteurization, a process that kills harmful bacteria.", "B": "Pasteurization is a process that heats milk, and milk is a common drink.", "C": "Alice Catherine Evans worked as a microbiologist who studied milk.", "D": "Raw milk may contain bacteria, and bacteria are too small to see without tools."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-006", "prompt": "At a community center, a reading room was originally created as a quiet place for adults. After a few months, staff noticed that students also used the room to do homework and that neighbors sometimes exchanged book recommendations there. The space became more than a quiet room; it became a small learning community.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A room designed for quiet reading also encouraged learning and connection.", "B": "Community centers should not include rooms for adults.", "C": "Students stopped doing homework after the reading room opened.", "D": "Book recommendations are useful only when shared online."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe counselor explained the new schedule <u>students would have longer lunch periods on Fridays.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "schedule; students would have longer lunch periods on Fridays.", "C": "schedule students, would have longer lunch periods on Fridays.", "D": "schedule, students would have longer lunch periods, on Fridays."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-008", "prompt": "A theater group tracked ticket sales for four performances.\nPerformance: Thursday | Friday | Saturday | Sunday\nTickets sold: 82 | 95 | 120 | 90\nWhich choice accurately compares the ticket sales?", "choices": {"A": "Saturday sold 25 more tickets than Friday.", "B": "Sunday sold more tickets than Friday.", "C": "Thursday sold the most tickets.", "D": "Friday sold 13 fewer tickets than Thursday."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-009", "prompt": "Mary Golda Ross, an engineer and mathematician, worked on early designs for aircraft and spacecraft. Because much of her work was confidential, many details were not publicly known during her career. Later, however, she became recognized as an important figure in the history of aerospace engineering.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains why Ross’s work was not widely known at first.", "B": "It lists the exact aircraft and spacecraft Ross designed.", "C": "It argues that Ross’s work was unimportant because it was confidential.", "D": "It describes why Ross chose to stop working in engineering."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-010", "prompt": "The student’s first draft of the report jumped from one idea to the next without clear connections. After adding topic sentences and transition words, she made the report more ______ for readers.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "organized", "B": "silent", "C": "risky", "D": "ordinary"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe guide described three ways to prepare for a long hike<u> checking the weather, packing enough water, and wearing sturdy shoes.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "hike: checking the weather, packing enough water, and wearing sturdy shoes.", "C": "hike checking: the weather, packing enough water, and wearing sturdy shoes.", "D": "hike, checking the weather: packing enough water, and wearing sturdy shoes."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Carlos finds an old recipe written on the back of a grocery receipt. The handwriting is unfamiliar, but the recipe is for the soup his grandfather always made on snowy days. Carlos folds the paper carefully and places it inside the family cookbook.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Carlos?", "choices": {"A": "He believes the recipe is connected to a family memory.", "B": "He plans to throw away the family cookbook.", "C": "He is certain that the grocery receipt is from a store he visited that day.", "D": "He dislikes the soup his grandfather made."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about murals in school hallways. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Murals in school hallways can make shared spaces feel welcoming.\n2. Students may help design images that represent school values, local landmarks, or favorite subjects.\n3. Some murals are painted directly on walls, while others are created on large panels.\n4. When students help create the artwork, they may feel more connected to the building.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to describe different mural formats before discussing how student involvement can affect students?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nSeveral pieces of broken pottery <u>was</u> discovered near the old well.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how many grams of salt dissolved in three cups of water at different temperatures.\nWater temperature: Cold | Warm | Hot\nSalt dissolved: 18 g | 28 g | 39 g\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Hot water dissolved the most salt.", "B": "Cold water dissolved more salt than warm water.", "C": "Warm and hot water dissolved the same amount of salt.", "D": "Cold water dissolved 21 g more salt than hot water."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe team’s goal is <u>to interview local business owners, creating a map of shops, and to present the map</u> at the town meeting.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "interviewing local business owners, to create a map of shops, and presenting the map", "C": "to interview local business owners, to create a map of shops, and to present the map", "D": "local business owners are interviewed, a map is created, and presenting the map"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe article shows that the museum made its exhibits easier to understand.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of how the museum made its exhibits easier to understand. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The article shows that the museum made its exhibits easier to understand by adding larger labels, simple timelines, and audio recordings.", "B": "The article shows that the museum has exhibits in several rooms on the first floor.", "C": "The article shows that museums sometimes display objects from many time periods.", "D": "The article shows that many visitors enter the museum through the main doors."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-018", "prompt": "Some scientists study lichens, which are organisms that can grow on rocks, tree bark, and old walls. Because certain lichens are sensitive to air pollution, their presence or absence can give scientists clues about air quality. These organisms can act as natural indicators of environmental conditions.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It explains why lichens can help scientists learn about air quality.", "B": "It argues that lichens grow only in polluted air.", "C": "It describes the exact color of every lichen species.", "D": "It shows that scientists study lichens only because they grow on walls."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe notebooks on the lower shelf <u>contains</u> field notes from last year’s study.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "contain", "C": "is containing", "D": "has contained"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-020", "prompt": "The coach described the final drill as optional, meaning that players could choose whether to complete it after regular practice.\nAs used in the text, “optional” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "not required", "B": "repeated often", "C": "difficult to explain", "D": "already finished"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching the composer Florence Price, a student has taken these notes:\n• Florence Price was an American composer and pianist.\n• She wrote symphonies, songs, and piano music.\n• In 1933, the Chicago Symphony Orchestra performed her Symphony No. 1.\n• She was the first Black woman composer to have a symphony performed by a major U.S. orchestra.\nThe student wants to explain the historical importance of Price’s Symphony No. 1 performance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Florence Price wrote symphonies, songs, and piano music during her career.", "B": "The 1933 performance of Price’s Symphony No. 1 was historically important because it made her the first Black woman composer to have a symphony performed by a major U.S. orchestra.", "C": "Florence Price was both a composer and a pianist who wrote different types of music.", "D": "The Chicago Symphony Orchestra performed music by many composers in the 1930s."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nAt the open house, students demonstrated science projects, answered visitors’ questions, and <u>they handed</u> out information sheets.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "handing", "C": "handed", "D": "to hand"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-023", "prompt": "The student first read the poem silently to understand its meaning. ______, she read it aloud to hear the rhythm of the lines.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Then", "B": "However", "C": "For example", "D": "In contrast"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about why communities should maintain public benches near walking paths. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a practical benefit?", "choices": {"A": "Public benches near walking paths give people places to rest during walks.", "B": "Some walking paths are made of gravel, while others are paved.", "C": "Public benches may be painted brown, green, or blue.", "D": "Many communities have walking paths that pass trees and lawns."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-025", "prompt": "The science fair display, <u>which explained how shadows change during the day</u> won first prize in its category.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which explained how shadows change during the day,", "C": "which, explained how shadows change during the day", "D": "which explained how shadows change during the day;"}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying tree rings can learn about past growing conditions. Wide rings often show years when a tree had enough water and favorable weather, while narrow rings may show years of drought or stress. By comparing rings from many trees, researchers can build a picture of past climate patterns.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Tree rings can help researchers understand past climate conditions.", "B": "Trees stop growing rings during every year with favorable weather.", "C": "Researchers study tree rings mainly to count how many trees are in a forest.", "D": "Wide tree rings always show that a tree was cut down during a drought."}}, {"id": "RW5-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching school makerspaces, a student has taken these notes:\n• A makerspace is an area where students can design and build projects.\n• Makerspaces may include cardboard, craft supplies, simple tools, or technology.\n• Students can practice creativity and problem solving in makerspaces.\n• Makerspaces require clear safety rules and cleanup routines.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of school makerspaces. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Makerspaces may include cardboard, craft supplies, simple tools, or technology.", "B": "School makerspaces can help students practice creativity and problem solving, but they require clear safety rules and cleanup routines.", "C": "A makerspace is an area where students can design and build projects.", "D": "Students sometimes use technology in makerspaces when building projects."}}], [{"id": "RW6-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “medium” can describe a size between small and large. In art, however, a medium is the material an artist uses to create a work. Clay, watercolor paint, and charcoal are different artistic ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "materials", "B": "schedules", "C": "distances", "D": "questions"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-002", "prompt": "In the 1940s, computer programmer Jean Jennings Bartik worked on ENIAC, one of the earliest electronic computers. She and other programmers had to understand both mathematics and the machine’s complex wiring to make it solve problems. Their work helped show that programming required creativity as well as technical skill.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Jean Jennings Bartik’s work on an early computer showed that programming involved creative and technical thinking.", "B": "Jean Jennings Bartik worked on ENIAC because early computers were simple to operate.", "C": "Early electronic computers solved problems without help from programmers.", "D": "Programming in the 1940s required no knowledge of mathematics."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe bowl of sliced peaches and berries <u>were</u> placed in the refrigerator.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-004", "prompt": "The old train station had only one narrow entrance, so crowds moved slowly during busy hours. ______, the renovated station has three wider entrances and a larger waiting area.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "By contrast", "B": "As a result", "C": "For example", "D": "In other words"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching marine biologist Eugenie Clark, a student has taken these notes:\n• Eugenie Clark studied sharks and other fish.\n• She challenged the idea that sharks were only dangerous and unintelligent.\n• Clark trained some sharks to respond to signals.\n• She wrote books and gave talks to teach the public about marine life.\nThe student wants to explain how Clark changed public understanding of sharks. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Eugenie Clark studied several kinds of ocean animals, including sharks and other fish.", "B": "Clark challenged negative ideas about sharks by showing they could learn to respond to signals and by teaching the public about marine life.", "C": "Eugenie Clark wrote books and gave talks during her career as a marine biologist.", "D": "Some people once believed that sharks were dangerous animals."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-006", "prompt": "A town replaced a cracked concrete plaza with a small performance space, shade trees, and movable chairs. Before the renovation, people usually hurried across the plaza without stopping. Afterward, they stayed to listen to music, eat lunch, or talk with friends. The redesign changed the plaza from a pass-through area into a gathering place.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A redesigned public space encouraged people to spend more time there.", "B": "Cracked concrete plazas are always safer than performance spaces.", "C": "Movable chairs prevent people from talking with friends.", "D": "Towns should avoid planting shade trees in public areas."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe historian examined two letters from the same family <u>one was written in 1910, and the other was written in 1918.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "family; one was written in 1910, and the other was written in 1918.", "C": "family one was written in 1910, and the other was written in 1918.", "D": "family, one was written in 1910 and the other, was written in 1918."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-008", "prompt": "A community center tracked enrollment in four weekend classes.\nClass: Pottery | Coding | Yoga | Cooking\nStudents enrolled: 17 | 26 | 21 | 24\nWhich choice accurately compares class enrollment?", "choices": {"A": "Coding had 2 more students than Cooking.", "B": "Pottery had more students than Yoga.", "C": "Cooking had the greatest enrollment.", "D": "Yoga had 5 fewer students than Pottery."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-009", "prompt": "Inventor Elijah McCoy designed an automatic oiling device for steam engines. Before devices like his, workers had to stop machines to add oil by hand. McCoy’s device allowed engines to be oiled while running, which helped machines operate more efficiently.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains the problem that McCoy’s invention helped solve.", "B": "It lists the materials used to build McCoy’s device.", "C": "It argues that workers preferred to stop machines often.", "D": "It describes why steam engines did not need oil."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-010", "prompt": "The student’s presentation included useful facts, but the slides were crowded with long paragraphs. Her teacher suggested replacing some sentences with short labels and clear images to make the information more ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "readable", "B": "distant", "C": "private", "D": "uneven"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe class discussed three qualities of a strong group project<u> clear roles, shared deadlines, and respectful communication.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "project: clear roles, shared deadlines, and respectful communication.", "C": "project clear roles: shared deadlines, and respectful communication.", "D": "project, clear roles: shared deadlines, and respectful communication."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Amina finds a faded train ticket tucked inside her father’s old notebook. The ticket is from the city where he grew up, a place he rarely talks about. Amina places the ticket beside her homework and decides to ask him about it after dinner.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Amina?", "choices": {"A": "She thinks the ticket may help her learn more about her father’s past.", "B": "She plans to throw away the notebook before dinner.", "C": "She already knows every detail about the city on the ticket.", "D": "She believes the train ticket belongs to her homework teacher."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about outdoor classrooms. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Outdoor classrooms give students a place to learn outside the traditional school building.\n2. Lessons in outdoor classrooms may include weather observations, reading circles, or art activities.\n3. Some outdoor classrooms use logs or benches for seating, while others have tables under a shelter.\n4. Learning outside can help students connect classroom ideas to the natural world around them.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to describe possible classroom designs before giving examples of lessons?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe pages from the travel journal <u>was</u> copied for the history project.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how many drops of water fit on three different coins before spilling.\nCoin: Penny | Nickel | Quarter\nDrops of water: 24 | 19 | 31\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "The quarter held the most drops of water.", "B": "The nickel held more drops than the penny.", "C": "The penny and quarter held the same number of drops.", "D": "The nickel held 12 more drops than the quarter."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe committee’s plan is <u>to survey students, creating a report, and to share the results</u> with the principal.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "surveying students, to create a report, and sharing the results", "C": "to survey students, to create a report, and to share the results", "D": "students are surveyed, a report is created, and to share the results"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe article explains how the bus system became easier to use.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of improvements to the bus system. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The article explains how the bus system became easier to use by adding clearer route maps, digital arrival signs, and benches at busy stops.", "B": "The article explains that buses are vehicles that carry passengers along roads.", "C": "The article explains how the bus system runs in a city with many streets.", "D": "The article explains that some people ride buses in the morning and others ride in the afternoon."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-018", "prompt": "Some historians study school yearbooks to learn about communities in the past. Yearbooks may show which clubs students joined, what clothing styles were common, and how schools celebrated events. Though yearbooks were made for students, they can also become useful records for researchers.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It gives examples of information yearbooks can provide about the past.", "B": "It argues that yearbooks are useful only to the students shown in them.", "C": "It explains why schools should stop printing yearbooks.", "D": "It describes the exact process used to take yearbook photographs."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe murals along the hallway <u>shows</u> scenes from the town’s history.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "show", "C": "is showing", "D": "has shown"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-020", "prompt": "The coach asked the runners to maintain their speed during the final lap, meaning they should keep going at the same pace instead of slowing down.\nAs used in the text, “maintain” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "keep", "B": "measure", "C": "forget", "D": "announce"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching the writer and activist Helen Keller, a student has taken these notes:\n• Helen Keller lost both sight and hearing as a young child.\n• She learned to read and write with the help of her teacher Anne Sullivan.\n• Keller became an author and speaker.\n• She advocated for people with disabilities and other social causes.\nThe student wants to explain Keller’s public work. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Helen Keller lost both sight and hearing when she was young and later learned to read and write.", "B": "Helen Keller became an author and speaker who advocated for people with disabilities and other social causes.", "C": "Anne Sullivan helped Helen Keller learn to read and write after Keller lost sight and hearing.", "D": "Helen Keller’s childhood experiences were different from those of many other children."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nDuring the workshop, students measured fabric, pinned the pattern, and <u>they cut</u> the pieces carefully.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "cutting", "C": "cut", "D": "to cut"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-023", "prompt": "The gardener first loosened the soil with a small rake. ______, she planted the seedlings in straight rows.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "Nevertheless", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about the value of school art shows. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a benefit to students?", "choices": {"A": "School art shows give students a chance to share their creative work with classmates, families, and teachers.", "B": "Some art shows are held in hallways, gyms, or libraries.", "C": "Paintings, drawings, and photographs can be displayed on tables or walls.", "D": "Many students use pencils, markers, or paint in art class."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-025", "prompt": "The robotics exhibit, <u>which featured student-built machines</u> drew visitors from several nearby schools.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which featured student-built machines,", "C": "which, featured student-built machines", "D": "which featured student-built machines;"}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying prairie dogs have found that the animals make different alarm calls for different threats. One call may warn of a hawk overhead, while another may warn of a coyote nearby. These calls help other prairie dogs respond to danger in more specific ways.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Prairie dogs use different alarm calls to warn one another about specific dangers.", "B": "Prairie dogs make only one sound no matter what kind of threat is nearby.", "C": "Hawks and coyotes use prairie dog calls to find food.", "D": "Researchers study prairie dogs mainly because the animals never face danger."}}, {"id": "RW6-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching student-run school stores, a student has taken these notes:\n• Student-run school stores may sell pencils, notebooks, or spirit items.\n• Students can practice customer service and basic money skills.\n• Stores require careful tracking of inventory and sales.\n• Some stores are open only during lunch or before school.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of student-run school stores. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Student-run school stores may sell pencils, notebooks, or spirit items.", "B": "Student-run school stores can help students practice customer service and money skills, but they require careful tracking of inventory and sales.", "C": "Some school stores are open during lunch or before school.", "D": "Students who work in school stores may sell items to classmates."}}], [{"id": "RW7-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “draft” can mean a current of air, but writers use the word differently. In writing, a draft is an early version of a text that can be revised before it is ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "finished", "B": "frozen", "C": "lifted", "D": "measured"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-002", "prompt": "In the 1950s, architect Norma Merrick Sklarek helped design large public buildings at a time when few women, especially Black women, were widely recognized in architecture. She later became the first Black woman licensed as an architect in New York and in California. Her career opened doors in a profession that had often excluded people like her.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Norma Merrick Sklarek achieved important firsts and helped expand possibilities in architecture.", "B": "Norma Merrick Sklarek designed only small houses during her career.", "C": "Architecture in the 1950s included equal recognition for all groups.", "D": "Public buildings were no longer designed after Sklarek became licensed."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe bundle of old newspapers and magazines <u>were</u> tied with string.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-004", "prompt": "The first school website listed only the address, phone number, and office hours. ______, the current website includes homework links, lunch menus, club calendars, and teacher pages.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "By comparison", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For example", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching environmental scientist Wangari Maathai, a student has taken these notes:\n• Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement in Kenya.\n• The movement encouraged communities to plant trees.\n• Tree planting helped reduce soil erosion and provided firewood.\n• Maathai also spoke about democracy and community responsibility.\nThe student wants to explain how the Green Belt Movement connected environmental action with community needs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement in Kenya and spoke about democracy.", "B": "The Green Belt Movement encouraged communities to plant trees, helping reduce soil erosion and provide firewood.", "C": "Tree planting can happen in many countries where communities need more firewood.", "D": "Wangari Maathai was an environmental scientist who became known outside Kenya."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-006", "prompt": "A middle school replaced a rarely used storage room with a peer tutoring center. At first, the space was expected to help students who needed extra practice in math. Soon, however, students also used it to prepare for presentations, review science vocabulary, and practice study habits. The center’s usefulness grew beyond its original purpose.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A peer tutoring center became useful for several kinds of academic support.", "B": "Storage rooms are always better for students than tutoring centers.", "C": "Students stopped practicing math after the tutoring center opened.", "D": "Presentations and science vocabulary cannot be studied in the same place."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe scientist tested two samples from the pond <u>one was taken near the shore, and the other was taken from deeper water.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "pond; one was taken near the shore, and the other was taken from deeper water.", "C": "pond one was taken near the shore, and the other was taken from deeper water.", "D": "pond, one was taken near the shore and the other, was taken from deeper water."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-008", "prompt": "A school tracked participation in four lunchtime clubs.\nClub: Debate | Origami | Garden Design | Film Review\nStudents: 23 | 18 | 27 | 21\nWhich choice accurately compares club participation?", "choices": {"A": "Garden Design had 4 more students than Debate.", "B": "Origami had more students than Film Review.", "C": "Debate had the fewest students.", "D": "Film Review had 6 more students than Garden Design."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-009", "prompt": "Biologist Ernest Everett Just studied how egg cells develop after fertilization. At a time when many scientists focused mainly on the cell nucleus, Just argued that the outer layer of the cell also played an important role. His work encouraged other researchers to look more closely at the whole cell.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains how Just’s view differed from a common scientific focus of his time.", "B": "It lists every kind of cell that Just studied.", "C": "It argues that the cell nucleus has no role in development.", "D": "It describes why Just stopped working as a biologist."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-010", "prompt": "The editor said that the article about the school orchestra included strong examples, but some details did not clearly connect to the main idea. The writer removed those details to make the article more ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "focused", "B": "crowded", "C": "colorful", "D": "doubtful"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe safety lesson included three reminders<u> wear goggles, tie back long hair, and keep bags away from the lab tables.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "reminders: wear goggles, tie back long hair, and keep bags away from the lab tables.", "C": "reminders wear goggles: tie back long hair, and keep bags away from the lab tables.", "D": "reminders, wear goggles: tie back long hair, and keep bags away from the lab tables."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Jun helps his grandfather clean out a closet and finds a box of neatly folded paper cranes. His grandfather explains that he made one crane each evening during his first year in a new country. Jun asks if they can hang some of the cranes in the living room.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Jun?", "choices": {"A": "He values the cranes as reminders of his grandfather’s experience.", "B": "He thinks the cranes should be thrown away immediately.", "C": "He already knew exactly why every crane was made.", "D": "He wants to hide the cranes so no one can see them."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about school morning broadcasts. The sentences are shown below.\n1. School morning broadcasts can share announcements in a way that reaches many classrooms at once.\n2. Some broadcasts include student hosts, short interviews, or reminders about upcoming events.\n3. Broadcasts may be recorded in a small studio or filmed with a tablet in the library.\n4. When students help produce the broadcasts, they can practice speaking, planning, and teamwork.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to describe production locations before giving examples of broadcast content?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe rows of folding chairs <u>was</u> arranged before the ceremony began.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how long three different fabrics took to dry after being sprayed with the same amount of water.\nFabric: Cotton | Nylon | Wool\nDrying time: 45 min | 25 min | 60 min\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Nylon dried the fastest.", "B": "Wool dried faster than cotton.", "C": "Cotton and nylon took the same amount of time to dry.", "D": "Wool dried 35 minutes faster than nylon."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe council’s proposal is <u>to add recycling bins, creating signs for them, and to teach students</u> what can be recycled.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "adding recycling bins, to create signs for them, and teaching students", "C": "to add recycling bins, to create signs for them, and to teach students", "D": "recycling bins are added, signs are created, and teaching students"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe article shows that the theater improved audience comfort.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of improvements. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The article shows that the theater improved audience comfort by widening aisles, adding cushioned seats, and improving air flow.", "B": "The article shows that the theater is located on a street near several restaurants.", "C": "The article shows that many theaters present plays, concerts, and community events.", "D": "The article shows that audiences usually enter a theater before a performance begins."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-018", "prompt": "Some researchers study old ship logs to learn about weather in the past. Sailors often recorded wind direction, storms, sea conditions, and temperatures during voyages. These records can help scientists compare past weather patterns with present ones.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It gives examples of weather information that ship logs may contain.", "B": "It argues that ship logs should be used only by sailors.", "C": "It explains why sailors never wrote about storms.", "D": "It describes the exact route of every historic voyage."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe paintings in the east gallery <u>was</u> selected for a special exhibition.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-020", "prompt": "The principal described the new schedule as temporary because it would be used only while repairs were being made to the cafeteria.\nAs used in the text, “temporary” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "lasting for a limited time", "B": "difficult to explain", "C": "chosen by students", "D": "repeated every year"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching the chemist Percy Lavon Julian, a student has taken these notes:\n• Percy Lavon Julian was an American chemist.\n• He developed ways to make useful medicines from plant compounds.\n• His work helped make some medicines more available.\n• He also created products from soybeans, including firefighting foam.\nThe student wants to explain Julian’s scientific contributions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Percy Lavon Julian was an American chemist who worked with plants and soybeans.", "B": "Julian developed ways to make medicines from plant compounds and also created useful soybean-based products.", "C": "Some medicines can be made from compounds that come from plants.", "D": "Firefighting foam is one of many products used in emergencies."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nDuring the cleanup day, volunteers swept the sidewalk, trimmed the bushes, and <u>they planted</u> flowers near the entrance.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "planting", "C": "planted", "D": "to plant"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-023", "prompt": "The student first gathered three sources about the topic. ______, she compared the sources to find ideas they had in common.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For instance"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about the value of school clubs. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a benefit to students?", "choices": {"A": "School clubs can help students explore interests, meet classmates, and develop new skills.", "B": "Some school clubs meet in classrooms, gyms, or outdoor spaces.", "C": "Students may sign up for clubs by filling out a form.", "D": "Many schools list club meeting times on bulletin boards or websites."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-025", "prompt": "The student newspaper, <u>which is published every Friday</u> includes articles, interviews, reviews, and photographs.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which is published every Friday,", "C": "which, is published every Friday", "D": "which is published every Friday;"}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying elephants have found that the animals sometimes use low sounds that can travel long distances. Other elephants may respond even when they are too far away to be seen. These sounds help elephants communicate across large spaces.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Elephants can use low sounds to communicate over long distances.", "B": "Elephants communicate only when they are standing close together.", "C": "Researchers study elephants because the animals never respond to sounds.", "D": "Low sounds prevent elephants from finding one another."}}, {"id": "RW7-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching school recycling teams, a student has taken these notes:\n• School recycling teams collect paper, bottles, or cans from classrooms.\n• Students can learn about waste reduction by joining a team.\n• Teams must sort materials correctly so they can be recycled.\n• Some teams make posters to remind classmates what belongs in each bin.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of school recycling teams. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "School recycling teams may collect paper, bottles, or cans from classrooms.", "B": "School recycling teams can help students learn about waste reduction, but teams must sort materials correctly.", "C": "Some school recycling teams make posters about bins.", "D": "Students can join teams that collect materials from classrooms."}}], [{"id": "RW8-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “scale” can refer to the size of something, but mapmakers use the word in a more specific way. On a map, a scale shows how distance on the map ______ distance in the real world.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "represents", "B": "forgets", "C": "interrupts", "D": "decorates"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-002", "prompt": "In the early twentieth century, photographer James Van Der Zee became known for portraits of people in Harlem, New York. His carefully arranged photographs showed weddings, clubs, families, and community leaders. Together, these images created a rich record of Black life and culture during the Harlem Renaissance.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "James Van Der Zee’s portraits documented many parts of Harlem community life.", "B": "James Van Der Zee photographed only empty streets in Harlem.", "C": "The Harlem Renaissance ended because people stopped taking portraits.", "D": "Community leaders avoided being photographed in the early twentieth century."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe folder of maps and bus passes <u>were</u> left on the information desk.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-004", "prompt": "A traditional paper notebook can be used without electricity. ______, a digital notebook may allow users to search notes quickly and add audio recordings.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "On the other hand", "B": "As a result", "C": "For this reason", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching physicist Chien-Shiung Wu, a student has taken these notes:\n• Chien-Shiung Wu was a Chinese American physicist.\n• She designed careful experiments in nuclear physics.\n• One famous experiment helped test whether a law called parity was always followed.\n• Her work provided evidence that changed scientists’ understanding of particle behavior.\nThe student wants to explain the importance of Wu’s scientific work. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Chien-Shiung Wu was a Chinese American physicist who worked in nuclear physics.", "B": "Wu designed experiments that provided evidence changing scientists’ understanding of particle behavior.", "C": "A law called parity was studied by scientists who worked in physics.", "D": "Nuclear physics includes experiments that may require careful planning."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-006", "prompt": "A school changed an unused courtyard by adding shade sails, benches, and raised planters. Before the change, students rarely spent time there. Afterward, classes used the courtyard for reading, science observations, and small group discussions. The project turned an overlooked space into a flexible learning area.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A courtyard redesign made an unused school space useful for learning.", "B": "Students stopped reading after benches were added to the courtyard.", "C": "Raised planters make outdoor areas impossible to use for science.", "D": "Schools should keep courtyards empty so students can avoid them."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe archaeologist compared two clay tablets <u>one had writing on both sides, and the other had writing on only one side.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "tablets; one had writing on both sides, and the other had writing on only one side.", "C": "tablets one had writing on both sides, and the other had writing on only one side.", "D": "tablets, one had writing on both sides and the other, had writing on only one side."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-008", "prompt": "A nature club recorded attendance at four weekend hikes.\nTrail: Creek Loop | Pine Ridge | Meadow Path | Hilltop Trail\nHikers: 28 | 35 | 31 | 24\nWhich choice accurately compares hike attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Pine Ridge had 4 more hikers than Meadow Path.", "B": "Hilltop Trail had more hikers than Creek Loop.", "C": "Meadow Path had the fewest hikers.", "D": "Creek Loop had 7 more hikers than Pine Ridge."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-009", "prompt": "Paleontologist Mary Anning found important fossils along the cliffs of southern England in the early 1800s. At the time, many scientific societies did not allow women to become members, so Anning did not receive the recognition many male scientists did. Even so, her discoveries helped scientists learn more about life from long ago.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains one reason Anning’s work was not fully recognized during her lifetime.", "B": "It describes the tools Anning used to remove fossils from cliffs.", "C": "It argues that Anning’s discoveries were not useful to science.", "D": "It explains why all scientific societies welcomed women in the early 1800s."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-010", "prompt": "The student’s essay included many examples, but they appeared in no clear order. Her teacher suggested grouping related examples together so the essay would feel more ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "organized", "B": "private", "C": "expensive", "D": "uncertain"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe guide explained three steps for using the telescope<u> adjust the height, focus the lens, and avoid touching the glass.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "telescope: adjust the height, focus the lens, and avoid touching the glass.", "C": "telescope adjust: the height, focus the lens, and avoid touching the glass.", "D": "telescope, adjust the height: focus the lens, and avoid touching the glass."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Ellis finds a small wooden whistle in a drawer while helping his aunt clean. His aunt says his great-grandfather carved it while working on a farm many years earlier. Ellis turns the whistle over in his hands and asks if he can write a report about it for school.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Ellis?", "choices": {"A": "He becomes interested in the whistle as a link to his family history.", "B": "He thinks the whistle should be thrown away immediately.", "C": "He already knows everything about his great-grandfather’s farm.", "D": "He plans to use the whistle to interrupt his class."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about classroom debates. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Classroom debates can help students practice forming clear claims.\n2. Students may prepare by reading sources, listing evidence, and predicting opposing arguments.\n3. Some debates are held in pairs, while others involve the whole class.\n4. During a debate, students must also listen carefully so they can respond to what others say.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to describe possible debate formats before explaining how students prepare?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe copies of the school handbook <u>was</u> stacked beside the office door.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how far three balls rolled across the same gym floor after being pushed with equal force.\nBall: Foam | Rubber | Plastic\nDistance rolled: 8 m | 14 m | 11 m\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "The rubber ball rolled the farthest.", "B": "The foam ball rolled farther than the plastic ball.", "C": "All three balls rolled the same distance.", "D": "The plastic ball rolled 6 m farther than the rubber ball."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe group’s plan is <u>to record interviews, editing the audio, and to post the episode</u> on the class website.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "recording interviews, to edit the audio, and posting the episode", "C": "to record interviews, to edit the audio, and to post the episode", "D": "interviews are recorded, the audio is edited, and to post the episode"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe report explains how the town made the park safer.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of safety improvements. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The report explains how the town made the park safer by adding brighter lights, repairing uneven paths, and trimming bushes near entrances.", "B": "The report explains that the town park includes trees, grass, and several picnic tables.", "C": "The report explains that many people visit parks on weekends or after school.", "D": "The report explains how the park is located near the center of town."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-018", "prompt": "Some historians study household receipts to learn about daily life in the past. Receipts can show what families bought, how much goods cost, and which stores people visited. These small papers can reveal patterns that might not appear in official records.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It gives examples of information household receipts can provide.", "B": "It argues that receipts are never useful to historians.", "C": "It explains why families stopped buying goods.", "D": "It lists every store that existed in the past."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe lamps above the reading tables <u>provides</u> soft light.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "provide", "C": "is providing", "D": "has provided"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-020", "prompt": "The teacher described the book’s ending as unexpected because it surprised most students while still making sense with earlier events in the story.\nAs used in the text, “unexpected” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "surprising", "B": "unfinished", "C": "carefully copied", "D": "easy to carry"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching poet Phillis Wheatley Peters, a student has taken these notes:\n• Phillis Wheatley Peters was one of the first Black women to publish a book of poetry in English.\n• Her poetry was published in 1773.\n• She wrote about religion, learning, and public events.\n• Her work became known in both North America and Europe.\nThe student wants to explain Wheatley Peters’s literary importance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Phillis Wheatley Peters wrote poems about religion, learning, and public events.", "B": "Wheatley Peters was one of the first Black women to publish a book of poetry in English, and her work became known in North America and Europe.", "C": "Phillis Wheatley Peters published poetry in 1773, a year in the eighteenth century.", "D": "Some poets write about public events as well as personal experiences."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nFor the festival, students painted signs, arranged chairs, and <u>they welcomed</u> guests at the entrance.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "welcoming", "C": "welcomed", "D": "to welcome"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-023", "prompt": "The student first wrote a question for her survey. ______, she asked five classmates to test whether the question was clear.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about the value of school gardens for science classes. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining an educational benefit?", "choices": {"A": "School gardens can help students observe plant growth, soil conditions, and insects directly.", "B": "School gardens may be located near playgrounds, sidewalks, or classroom windows.", "C": "Some school gardens contain flowers, vegetables, or herbs.", "D": "Students who visit gardens may wear hats or bring notebooks."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-025", "prompt": "The local history display, <u>which includes photographs from the 1920s</u> attracts many visitors each spring.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which includes photographs from the 1920s,", "C": "which, includes photographs from the 1920s", "D": "which includes photographs from the 1920s;"}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying bats have found that some species use different calls when searching for food than when communicating with other bats. The changes in sound may help bats share information while also locating insects. These findings suggest that bat calls can serve more than one purpose.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some bat calls may serve different purposes, including finding food and communicating.", "B": "Bats are unable to change their calls in different situations.", "C": "Researchers study bats because the animals do not use sound.", "D": "Bat calls prevent bats from locating insects."}}, {"id": "RW8-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching student news podcasts, a student has taken these notes:\n• Student news podcasts can share school announcements.\n• Students may write scripts, record audio, and edit episodes.\n• Podcast teams must check facts before publishing.\n• Producing podcasts can help students practice communication skills.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of student news podcasts. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Student news podcasts can share announcements with people at school.", "B": "Producing student news podcasts can help students practice communication skills, but podcast teams must check facts before publishing.", "C": "Students may write scripts, record audio, and edit podcast episodes.", "D": "Podcast teams sometimes publish episodes about school announcements."}}], [{"id": "RW9-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “channel” can describe a television station, but in geography it can also mean a path through which water flows. A narrow channel between two islands allows ocean water to ______ between them.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "pass", "B": "vanish", "C": "climb", "D": "freeze"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-002", "prompt": "In the late 1800s, Dr. Susan La Flesche Picotte became one of the first Native American physicians in the United States. She treated patients in the Omaha community and worked to improve public health, sometimes traveling long distances to reach people who needed care. Her career combined medical skill with service to her community.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Susan La Flesche Picotte used her medical training to serve and improve the health of her community.", "B": "Susan La Flesche Picotte avoided traveling to see patients.", "C": "Public health work in the late 1800s did not involve doctors.", "D": "The Omaha community had no need for medical care."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe collection of postcards and travel stamps <u>were</u> kept in a blue album.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-004", "prompt": "A printed recipe card can be easy to use because it stays on the counter while someone cooks. ______, a cooking video can show each step in motion, which may help viewers understand a technique.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "On the other hand", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For this reason", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching engineer Fazlur Rahman Khan, a student has taken these notes:\n• Fazlur Rahman Khan was a structural engineer.\n• He developed tube-frame designs for tall buildings.\n• Tube-frame designs use strong outer walls to support a building.\n• His ideas helped make very tall skyscrapers more practical to build.\nThe student wants to explain the importance of Khan’s engineering ideas. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Fazlur Rahman Khan was a structural engineer who worked on tall buildings.", "B": "Khan’s tube-frame designs used strong outer walls, helping make very tall skyscrapers more practical to build.", "C": "Tall buildings can be supported in several different ways by engineers.", "D": "Some skyscrapers are known for their height and their outer walls."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-006", "prompt": "A town library replaced several fixed computer desks with movable tables and chairs. The change allowed the same room to be used for computer classes in the morning, tutoring sessions in the afternoon, and community meetings in the evening. By changing the furniture, the library made the room useful for more activities.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Movable furniture made a library room more flexible for different activities.", "B": "Libraries should remove all computers from public rooms.", "C": "Tutoring sessions can happen only in rooms with fixed desks.", "D": "Community meetings are less useful than computer classes."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-007", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe journalist checked two sources before publishing the story <u>one was an interview, and the other was a public record.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "story; one was an interview, and the other was a public record.", "C": "story one was an interview, and the other was a public record.", "D": "story, one was an interview and the other, was a public record."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-008", "prompt": "A school tracked attendance at four evening events.\nEvent: Science Night | Poetry Reading | Math Games | Art Showcase\nAttendance: 74 | 58 | 69 | 81\nWhich choice accurately compares event attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Art Showcase had 7 more attendees than Science Night.", "B": "Poetry Reading had more attendees than Math Games.", "C": "Science Night had the greatest attendance.", "D": "Math Games had 23 fewer attendees than Poetry Reading."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-009", "prompt": "Mathematician Gladys West helped develop models of Earth’s shape using satellite data. Her work contributed to systems that later made GPS technology more accurate. Although many people use GPS every day, they may not know that careful mathematical modeling helped make it possible.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains a practical result of West’s mathematical work.", "B": "It describes why West stopped studying satellite data.", "C": "It argues that GPS technology does not use models.", "D": "It lists every device that contains GPS technology."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-010", "prompt": "The student’s speech had strong evidence, but the examples appeared before the audience understood the main claim. Her teacher suggested beginning with a clear claim so the evidence would seem more ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "relevant", "B": "hidden", "C": "crowded", "D": "accidental"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe guide listed three ways to protect old photographs<u> keep them dry, avoid direct sunlight, and handle them with clean hands.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "photographs: keep them dry, avoid direct sunlight, and handle them with clean hands.", "C": "photographs keep them: dry, avoid direct sunlight, and handle them with clean hands.", "D": "photographs, keep them dry: avoid direct sunlight, and handle them with clean hands."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Rosa finds a stack of postcards tied with ribbon in her grandmother’s desk. The cards show cities her grandmother visited before moving to the United States. Rosa reads the dates carefully, then asks her grandmother if they can mark the cities on a map together.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Rosa?", "choices": {"A": "She wants to learn more about her grandmother’s earlier life.", "B": "She thinks the postcards should be hidden from her grandmother.", "C": "She already knows every place her grandmother visited.", "D": "She plans to mail the old postcards to new addresses."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about peer review in writing class. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Peer review gives students a chance to receive comments from classmates before turning in a final draft.\n2. Reviewers may point out confusing sentences, missing evidence, or places where ideas could be reordered.\n3. Some peer review is done with printed drafts, while other peer review happens in shared online documents.\n4. Using classmates’ feedback can help writers revise with a reader’s point of view in mind.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to describe possible peer-review formats before explaining what reviewers may do?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe notebooks from the research project <u>was</u> stored in a labeled box.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how many minutes three materials stayed warm after being heated to the same temperature.\nMaterial: Sand | Soil | Gravel\nMinutes stayed warm: 28 | 35 | 22\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Soil stayed warm the longest.", "B": "Gravel stayed warm longer than sand.", "C": "Sand and soil stayed warm for the same amount of time.", "D": "Gravel stayed warm 7 minutes longer than soil."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe group’s goal is <u>to collect oral histories, organizing the recordings, and to create an online exhibit</u> for the community.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "collecting oral histories, to organize the recordings, and creating an online exhibit", "C": "to collect oral histories, to organize the recordings, and to create an online exhibit", "D": "oral histories are collected, recordings are organized, and to create an online exhibit"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe article explains how the school made the cafeteria line move faster.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of changes. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The article explains how the school made the cafeteria line move faster by adding a second checkout station, posting menus earlier, and placing trays near the entrance.", "B": "The article explains that the cafeteria is located at the end of the main hallway.", "C": "The article explains that students eat lunch at different tables with their classmates.", "D": "The article explains how cafeterias may serve sandwiches, fruit, soup, or other foods."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-018", "prompt": "Some historians study handmade quilts to learn about the people who made them. Fabric choices, stitching patterns, and signs of repair can offer clues about available materials, family needs, and personal style. Though quilts are practical objects, they can also carry historical information.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It gives examples of what quilts may reveal about the past.", "B": "It argues that quilts should never be repaired.", "C": "It explains why fabric choices are unrelated to history.", "D": "It lists every quilt pattern used by families."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe videos in the online lesson <u>explains</u> how to solve each problem.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "explain", "C": "is explaining", "D": "has explained"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-020", "prompt": "The counselor described the application deadline as firm because late forms would not be accepted for any reason.\nAs used in the text, “firm” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "not flexible", "B": "easy to carry", "C": "written by hand", "D": "full of detail"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching labor leader Luisa Moreno, a student has taken these notes:\n• Luisa Moreno organized workers in several industries.\n• She advocated for better pay and safer working conditions.\n• She helped bring together workers from different backgrounds.\n• Moreno also spoke publicly about civil rights.\nThe student wants to explain Moreno’s advocacy. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Luisa Moreno organized workers and advocated for better pay, safer working conditions, and civil rights.", "B": "Luisa Moreno worked with people from several industries and backgrounds.", "C": "Some workers in different industries have organized for better conditions.", "D": "Public speaking can be one way for people to share ideas about work."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nDuring the training session, lifeguards reviewed safety rules, practiced rescue signals, and <u>they checked</u> the first-aid kits.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "checking", "C": "checked", "D": "to check"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-023", "prompt": "The student first sorted the survey responses by grade level. ______, he looked for patterns in how students answered each question.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about why school libraries should display student book reviews. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a benefit?", "choices": {"A": "Student book reviews can help classmates discover books they may want to read.", "B": "Some book reviews are written on paper, while others are posted online.", "C": "School libraries may arrange books by author, title, or subject.", "D": "Students sometimes write book reviews after finishing a novel."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-025", "prompt": "The community theater program, <u>which offers free acting classes</u> accepts students from several nearby schools.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which offers free acting classes,", "C": "which, offers free acting classes", "D": "which offers free acting classes;"}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying snow monkeys in Japan have observed that some monkeys wash sweet potatoes in water before eating them. Younger monkeys often learn the behavior by watching older monkeys. The observations suggest that some animal behaviors can spread through a group by learning rather than by instinct alone.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some snow monkeys learn food-washing behavior by watching others.", "B": "Snow monkeys are unable to learn from members of their group.", "C": "Researchers study snow monkeys because the animals never eat potatoes.", "D": "Food washing prevents young monkeys from observing older monkeys."}}, {"id": "RW9-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching school mediation programs, a student has taken these notes:\n• Mediation programs help students discuss conflicts calmly.\n• Trained peer mediators listen to both sides of a disagreement.\n• Mediation can help students practice communication and problem-solving skills.\n• Mediators must remain neutral and not take sides.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of school mediation programs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "School mediation programs may involve students who are trained as peer mediators.", "B": "School mediation programs can help students practice communication and problem solving, but mediators must remain neutral.", "C": "Peer mediators listen to both sides when students have a disagreement.", "D": "Some student conflicts can be discussed calmly with help from a program."}}], [{"id": "RW10-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “volume” can refer to how loud a sound is, but in math and science it can mean the amount of space something takes up. A measuring cup can show the volume of a liquid because it marks how much space the liquid ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "occupies", "B": "forgets", "C": "sharpens", "D": "predicts"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-002", "prompt": "In the early twentieth century, Mary McLeod Bethune founded a school for Black girls in Florida with only a few students and limited supplies. Over time, the school grew into a college. Bethune also worked as an adviser to national leaders and advocated for education and civil rights.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Mary McLeod Bethune expanded educational opportunities and supported civil rights.", "B": "Mary McLeod Bethune founded a school that remained small and unchanged.", "C": "National leaders advised Bethune to stop working in education.", "D": "Schools in Florida had unlimited supplies in the early twentieth century."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe crate of stage lights and extension cords <u>were</u> moved behind the curtain.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-004", "prompt": "An audio recording of an interview lets listeners hear a speaker’s tone of voice. ______, a written transcript can make it easier to search for a specific word or sentence.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "On the other hand", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For this reason", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching inventor Lewis Latimer, a student has taken these notes:\n• Lewis Latimer was an inventor and draftsman.\n• He helped improve electric light technology.\n• He developed a longer-lasting carbon filament for light bulbs.\n• He also prepared detailed technical drawings for other inventors.\nThe student wants to explain Latimer’s contributions to invention and technology. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Lewis Latimer was an inventor and draftsman who improved light-bulb technology and prepared technical drawings.", "B": "Lewis Latimer worked during a time when many people used lamps and candles.", "C": "A carbon filament is one part that can be found inside some light bulbs.", "D": "Technical drawings are sometimes used to show the parts of an invention."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-006", "prompt": "A recreation center replaced a row of unused vending machines with a water-bottle filling station, a notice board, and a small table for community flyers. The area, once passed without attention, became a place where visitors stopped for water and learned about local events. A minor change made the space more useful.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A small change turned an overlooked area into a more useful space.", "B": "Vending machines always make recreation centers more welcoming.", "C": "Visitors stopped reading community flyers after the area was changed.", "D": "Water-bottle filling stations can be used only outdoors."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-007", "prompt": "The student compared two newspaper articles about the same storm <u>one focused on road closures, and the other focused on power outages.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "storm; one focused on road closures, and the other focused on power outages.", "C": "storm one focused on road closures, and the other focused on power outages.", "D": "storm, one focused on road closures and the other, focused on power outages."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-008", "prompt": "A school tracked attendance at four afternoon workshops.\nWorkshop: Songwriting | Chess Strategy | Clay Modeling | Screen Printing\nAttendance: 36 | 29 | 42 | 33\nWhich choice accurately compares workshop attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Clay Modeling had 6 more attendees than Songwriting.", "B": "Chess Strategy had more attendees than Screen Printing.", "C": "Songwriting had the fewest attendees.", "D": "Screen Printing had 9 more attendees than Clay Modeling."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-009", "prompt": "Botanist George Washington Carver studied peanuts, sweet potatoes, and other crops. At a time when many farms in the southern United States depended heavily on cotton, Carver encouraged farmers to grow different crops to improve soil and support food production. His work connected scientific research with practical farming needs.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains the farming problem Carver’s advice was meant to address.", "B": "It lists every crop grown in the southern United States.", "C": "It argues that Carver wanted farmers to grow only cotton.", "D": "It describes why peanuts cannot be studied scientifically."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-010", "prompt": "The student’s article about the marching band included interviews with two musicians, but it did not explain when or where the band would perform. Adding that information would make the article more ______ for readers who wanted to attend.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "useful", "B": "private", "C": "careless", "D": "distant"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe training session covered three ways to prevent sports injuries<u> warming up, using proper equipment, and reporting pain early.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "injuries: warming up, using proper equipment, and reporting pain early.", "C": "injuries warming: up, using proper equipment, and reporting pain early.", "D": "injuries, warming up: using proper equipment, and reporting pain early."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Mateo helps his mother clean the attic and finds a notebook filled with sketches of boats. His mother explains that the sketches were drawn by Mateo’s uncle before he became a ship mechanic. Mateo studies the pages for a long time and asks if he can copy one of the drawings.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Mateo?", "choices": {"A": "He becomes interested in the notebook because it shows part of his uncle’s past.", "B": "He wants to throw away the notebook before his mother sees it.", "C": "He already knows why every boat was drawn.", "D": "He believes the sketches are instructions for cleaning the attic."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about student presentations. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Student presentations can help classmates learn information from one another.\n2. Presenters may use slides, posters, models, or live demonstrations.\n3. Some presentations are given individually, while others are shared by small groups.\n4. After presenting, students may answer questions that help clarify their ideas.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to describe presentation formats before listing presentation materials?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe folders from the leadership meeting <u>was</u> placed in the main office.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "were", "C": "is", "D": "has been"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how much weight three paper bridges could hold before bending.\nBridge: A | B | C\nWeight held: 450 g | 620 g | 530 g\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Bridge B held the most weight.", "B": "Bridge A held more weight than Bridge C.", "C": "All three bridges held the same weight.", "D": "Bridge C held 170 g more than Bridge B."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe committee’s goal is <u>to design a logo, choosing team colors, and to create a display</u> for the club fair.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "designing a logo, to choose team colors, and creating a display", "C": "to design a logo, to choose team colors, and to create a display", "D": "a logo is designed, team colors are chosen, and to create a display"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe article explains how the science lab became more efficient.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of changes that made the lab more efficient. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The article explains how the science lab became more efficient by labeling supply drawers, creating cleanup stations, and posting equipment-checkout rules.", "B": "The article explains that the science lab has tables, chairs, windows, and sinks.", "C": "The article explains that many students take science classes during the school year.", "D": "The article explains how the science lab is located near the end of a hallway."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-018", "prompt": "Some historians study old theater programs to learn about entertainment in the past. Programs can show the names of performers, ticket prices, advertisements, and the order of acts. These printed materials can reveal details about both performances and audiences.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It gives examples of information theater programs can provide.", "B": "It argues that theater programs are useless after a show ends.", "C": "It explains why performers never appear in programs.", "D": "It lists every theater that existed in the past."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe shelves beside the art sink <u>holds</u> jars of brushes and sponges.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "hold", "C": "is holding", "D": "has held"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-020", "prompt": "The teacher described the group’s solution as practical because it could be completed with the time and materials the class already had.\nAs used in the text, “practical” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "realistic", "B": "colorful", "C": "accidental", "D": "difficult to hear"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching journalist Ethel L. Payne, a student has taken these notes:\n• Ethel L. Payne was an American journalist.\n• She reported on civil rights and international events.\n• She asked challenging questions at presidential press conferences.\n• She became known as the “First Lady of the Black Press.”\nThe student wants to explain Payne’s work as a journalist. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Ethel L. Payne reported on civil rights and international events and asked challenging questions at presidential press conferences.", "B": "Ethel L. Payne became known by a nickname connected to journalism.", "C": "Some journalists report on events in the United States and in other countries.", "D": "Presidential press conferences include questions from reporters."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nDuring the rehearsal, actors reviewed their lines, adjusted their costumes, and <u>they practiced</u> the final scene.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "practicing", "C": "practiced", "D": "to practice"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-023", "prompt": "The researcher first labeled each jar with a number. ______, she recorded the temperature of the water inside each jar.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about the value of school mentorship programs. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a benefit to younger students?", "choices": {"A": "School mentorship programs can give younger students guidance from older students who have already faced similar challenges.", "B": "Some mentorship meetings happen during lunch, while others happen after school.", "C": "Older students may wear name tags when they volunteer as mentors.", "D": "Schools often announce mentorship programs on bulletin boards or websites."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-025", "prompt": "The astronomy club meeting, <u>which begins at seven o’clock</u> will include a lesson about identifying winter constellations.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which begins at seven o’clock,", "C": "which, begins at seven o’clock", "D": "which begins at seven o’clock;"}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying meerkats have observed that adults sometimes bring young meerkats food that is difficult to handle. By practicing with this food, young meerkats gradually learn hunting skills they will need later. The behavior suggests that some animals may help younger group members learn through guided practice.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Adult meerkats may help young meerkats develop hunting skills through practice.", "B": "Young meerkats are born knowing every hunting skill they will need.", "C": "Researchers study meerkats because the animals never share food.", "D": "Difficult food prevents young meerkats from learning anything."}}, {"id": "RW10-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching school welcome committees, a student has taken these notes:\n• School welcome committees help new students learn about the school.\n• Committee members may give tours or answer questions.\n• Serving on a committee can help students practice leadership.\n• Committee members must give accurate information and be respectful.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of school welcome committees. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "School welcome committees may give tours to new students.", "B": "School welcome committees can help members practice leadership, but members must give accurate information respectfully.", "C": "New students sometimes need help learning about a school.", "D": "Students on welcome committees may answer questions from classmates."}}], [{"id": "RW11-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “cell” can refer to a small room, but in biology it refers to the basic unit that makes up living things. A leaf contains many cells, each with structures that help the plant ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "function", "B": "disappear", "C": "apologize", "D": "measure"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-002", "prompt": "In the mid-twentieth century, geochemist Katsuko Saruhashi developed methods for measuring carbon dioxide in seawater. Her work helped scientists better understand how oceans absorb gases from the atmosphere. She also studied radioactive materials in seawater, showing how careful measurements could reveal changes in the environment.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Katsuko Saruhashi used careful measurements to study important changes in seawater.", "B": "Katsuko Saruhashi studied only the animals that live near the ocean surface.", "C": "Scientists cannot learn about the atmosphere by studying seawater.", "D": "Carbon dioxide can be measured only in the air and never in water."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe set of spare keys and name tags <u>were</u> kept in the front office.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-004", "prompt": "A paper calendar is easy to hang where everyone can see it. ______, an online calendar can send reminders and update automatically when an event changes.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "On the other hand", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For this reason", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching dancer Maria Tallchief, a student has taken these notes:\n• Maria Tallchief was a member of the Osage Nation.\n• She became a leading ballerina in the twentieth century.\n• She danced major roles with the New York City Ballet.\n• She helped raise the profile of American ballet.\nThe student wants to explain Tallchief’s importance in dance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Maria Tallchief was a member of the Osage Nation and a leading ballerina who helped raise the profile of American ballet.", "B": "Maria Tallchief danced in the twentieth century with other performers.", "C": "The New York City Ballet has included many dancers in major roles.", "D": "Ballet is a type of dance that may be performed on a stage."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-006", "prompt": "A school changed a wide hallway by adding display cases, a few stools, and shelves of student-created books. Previously, students hurried through the hallway on the way to class. After the change, many stopped to read, talk quietly, or look at classmates’ projects. The hallway became a place for sharing work rather than just passing through.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A hallway redesign turned a pass-through area into a place for reading and sharing student work.", "B": "Display cases prevent students from looking at their classmates’ projects.", "C": "Schools should remove all books from hallways.", "D": "Students stopped going to class after stools were placed in the hallway."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-007", "prompt": "The student compared two recordings of the same folk song <u>one was played on guitar, and the other was played on fiddle.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "song; one was played on guitar, and the other was played on fiddle.", "C": "song one was played on guitar, and the other was played on fiddle.", "D": "song, one was played on guitar and the other, was played on fiddle."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-008", "prompt": "A school tracked attendance at four family learning nights.\nEvent: Astronomy Night | Coding Night | History Night | Poetry Night\nAttendance: 96 | 88 | 74 | 91\nWhich choice accurately compares event attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Astronomy Night had 5 more attendees than Poetry Night.", "B": "History Night had more attendees than Coding Night.", "C": "Coding Night had the greatest attendance.", "D": "Poetry Night had 17 fewer attendees than History Night."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-009", "prompt": "Inventor Sarah E. Goode designed a folding cabinet bed in the late 1800s. At the time, many people living in small apartments needed furniture that saved space. Goode’s design could be used as a desk when folded, making it useful in rooms with limited space.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains the need that Goode’s invention helped address.", "B": "It lists every kind of furniture used in apartments.", "C": "It argues that Goode’s design made rooms smaller.", "D": "It describes why people stopped using desks in the late 1800s."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-010", "prompt": "The student’s report included strong facts about the school garden, but the introduction did not tell readers why the topic mattered. She added a sentence explaining that the garden helped classes connect science lessons with real plant growth. This addition made the introduction more ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "meaningful", "B": "distant", "C": "silent", "D": "accidental"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-011", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe science handout listed three signs of a chemical reaction<u> a color change, gas bubbles, and a temperature change.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "reaction: a color change, gas bubbles, and a temperature change.", "C": "reaction a color: change, gas bubbles, and a temperature change.", "D": "reaction, a color change: gas bubbles, and a temperature change."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Imani opens a music box that belonged to her mother as a child. The tune is faint, but her mother immediately hums along from the doorway. Imani closes the lid gently and asks her mother to teach her the song.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Imani?", "choices": {"A": "She recognizes that the music box is connected to her mother’s memories.", "B": "She wants to break the music box before her mother sees it.", "C": "She already knows every word of the song.", "D": "She thinks the tune has no meaning to her family."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about science notebooks. The sentences are shown below.\n1. Science notebooks help students keep a record of observations, questions, and results.\n2. Students may include labeled diagrams, data tables, or short written explanations.\n3. Some notebooks are paper journals, while others are digital files shared with a teacher.\n4. Reviewing earlier entries can help students notice patterns in an experiment.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to describe possible notebook formats before listing what students may include in them?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe folders on the conference table <u>contains</u> schedules for the visiting speakers.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "contain", "C": "is containing", "D": "has contained"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how many sheets of paper three different paper clips could hold before sliding off.\nPaper clip: Small | Medium | Large\nSheets held: 9 | 15 | 22\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "The large paper clip held the most sheets.", "B": "The small paper clip held more sheets than the medium paper clip.", "C": "The medium and large paper clips held the same number of sheets.", "D": "The small paper clip held 13 more sheets than the large paper clip."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-016", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe group’s goal is <u>to research local birds, creating a field guide, and to share copies</u> with the nature center.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "researching local birds, to create a field guide, and sharing copies", "C": "to research local birds, to create a field guide, and to share copies", "D": "local birds are researched, a field guide is created, and to share copies"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe article explains how the town improved its winter festival.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of improvements. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The article explains how the town improved its winter festival by adding clearer signs, heated rest areas, and earlier event schedules.", "B": "The article explains that winter festivals sometimes include music, lights, and food.", "C": "The article explains that the town’s winter festival is held during a cold month.", "D": "The article explains how many people enjoy festivals in public spaces."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-018", "prompt": "Some historians study old store catalogs to learn about the past. Catalogs can show what products were available, how items were advertised, and how prices changed over time. These pages can reveal details about everyday shopping habits.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It gives examples of information store catalogs can provide.", "B": "It argues that catalogs cannot help historians study daily life.", "C": "It explains why stores never changed prices.", "D": "It lists every product sold in old catalogs."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe panels beside the auditorium entrance <u>shows</u> photographs from past performances.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "show", "C": "is showing", "D": "has shown"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-020", "prompt": "The teacher described the student’s explanation as thorough because it included each step of the process and answered several possible questions.\nAs used in the text, “thorough” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "complete", "B": "brief", "C": "noisy", "D": "repeated"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching writer N. Scott Momaday, a student has taken these notes:\n• N. Scott Momaday is a Kiowa author.\n• His novel House Made of Dawn won the Pulitzer Prize.\n• His writing often includes Kiowa history, memory, and oral tradition.\n• He has written novels, essays, poems, and plays.\nThe student wants to explain the range and cultural focus of Momaday’s writing. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "N. Scott Momaday has written novels, essays, poems, and plays that often include Kiowa history, memory, and oral tradition.", "B": "House Made of Dawn is a novel that won the Pulitzer Prize.", "C": "N. Scott Momaday is an author whose work has received a major prize.", "D": "Kiowa history and oral tradition can appear in several types of writing."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nDuring the library event, students chose books, met local authors, and <u>they joined</u> small discussion groups.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "joining", "C": "joined", "D": "to join"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-023", "prompt": "The student first placed the soil sample in a clear container. ______, she added water and stirred the mixture gently.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about the value of school orientation days. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a benefit to new students?", "choices": {"A": "School orientation days can help new students learn where classrooms are, meet staff members, and understand daily routines.", "B": "Some orientation days are held before the first week of school begins.", "C": "Students may receive folders, schedules, or name tags during orientation.", "D": "Many schools have hallways, offices, cafeterias, and gyms."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-025", "prompt": "The field research station, <u>which sits beside a freshwater marsh</u> hosts students studying plants, insects, and birds.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which sits beside a freshwater marsh,", "C": "which, sits beside a freshwater marsh", "D": "which sits beside a freshwater marsh;"}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying hummingbirds have found that some species remember which flowers they have recently visited. This memory may help them avoid returning too soon to flowers that have little nectar left. The finding suggests that memory can play an important role in how hummingbirds search for food.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Some hummingbirds use memory to make their search for nectar more efficient.", "B": "Hummingbirds cannot remember flowers they have visited.", "C": "Researchers study hummingbirds because the birds avoid all flowers.", "D": "Flowers lose nectar only when researchers are nearby."}}, {"id": "RW11-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching school peer-reading programs, a student has taken these notes:\n• Peer-reading programs pair students to read together.\n• Older students may listen to younger students read aloud.\n• Younger students can gain reading practice and confidence.\n• Older students must listen patiently and give respectful feedback.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of school peer-reading programs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Peer-reading programs may pair older and younger students.", "B": "School peer-reading programs can help younger students gain reading practice and confidence, but older students must listen patiently and give respectful feedback.", "C": "Older students sometimes listen while younger students read aloud.", "D": "Reading aloud can happen in programs where students work together."}}], [{"id": "RW12-M2-001", "prompt": "The word “range” can mean a stove, but in geography it often refers to a connected group of mountains. A mountain range is not one mountain but a series of mountains that ______ across an area.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "extends", "B": "whispers", "C": "melts", "D": "apologizes"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-002", "prompt": "In the early 1900s, botanist Ynés Mexía began collecting plant specimens after starting her scientific career later in life. She traveled through parts of North, Central, and South America and collected thousands of specimens, some of which represented species new to science. Her work shows that important scientific contributions can come from people who enter a field by an unusual path.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Ynés Mexía made valuable botanical contributions after beginning science later in life.", "B": "Ynés Mexía collected plants only from one small garden.", "C": "Scientific careers must always begin during childhood.", "D": "Botanists avoid studying plant specimens from different regions."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-003", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe carton of markers and glue sticks <u>were</u> delivered to the art classroom.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "was", "C": "are", "D": "have been"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-004", "prompt": "A printed dictionary can be useful when internet access is unavailable. ______, an online dictionary may include audio pronunciations and quick search tools.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "On the other hand", "B": "Therefore", "C": "For this reason", "D": "In conclusion"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-005", "prompt": "While researching architect Minoru Yamasaki, a student has taken these notes:\n• Minoru Yamasaki was an American architect.\n• He designed buildings with tall arches and decorative patterns.\n• He believed buildings could feel calm and graceful.\n• His projects included schools, offices, and public buildings.\nThe student wants to explain Yamasaki’s design approach. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Minoru Yamasaki designed schools, offices, and public buildings during his career.", "B": "Yamasaki used features such as tall arches and decorative patterns to create buildings that could feel calm and graceful.", "C": "Some architects design many different kinds of buildings for public and private use.", "D": "Minoru Yamasaki was an American architect who completed many projects."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-006", "prompt": "A middle school turned an unused corner of the cafeteria into a “quiet lunch” area with a few small tables and a sign explaining the expectations. The area was created for students who needed a calmer place to eat. Soon, students also used it to read, finish homework, or talk quietly with a friend. The change gave students another way to use a busy shared space.\nWhich choice best describes the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "A quiet lunch area made the cafeteria useful for students with different needs.", "B": "Cafeterias should never include signs or tables.", "C": "Students stopped eating lunch after the quiet area opened.", "D": "Homework can be completed only in classrooms."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-007", "prompt": "The student examined two photographs of the same street <u>one was taken before the bridge was built, and the other was taken after it opened.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "street; one was taken before the bridge was built, and the other was taken after it opened.", "C": "street one was taken before the bridge was built, and the other was taken after it opened.", "D": "street, one was taken before the bridge was built and the other, was taken after it opened."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-008", "prompt": "A school tracked the number of students who attended four skill clinics.\nClinic: First Aid | Public Speaking | Digital Design | Study Skills\nStudents: 34 | 41 | 37 | 29\nWhich choice accurately compares clinic attendance?", "choices": {"A": "Public Speaking had 4 more students than Digital Design.", "B": "First Aid had more students than Public Speaking.", "C": "Study Skills had the greatest attendance.", "D": "Digital Design had 8 fewer students than Study Skills."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-009", "prompt": "Engineer Lillian Moller Gilbreth studied how people worked in kitchens, offices, and factories. She looked for ways to arrange tools and tasks so workers could move less and work more comfortably. Her research helped connect engineering with everyday human needs.\nWhich choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?", "choices": {"A": "It explains the practical goal of Gilbreth’s research.", "B": "It lists every tool used in kitchens and factories.", "C": "It argues that workers should move as much as possible.", "D": "It describes why engineering cannot affect daily life."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-010", "prompt": "The student’s review of the graphic novel praised the artwork but did not mention the story. Her teacher suggested adding a sentence about the plot so the review would be more ______.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most precise word?", "choices": {"A": "balanced", "B": "hidden", "C": "temporary", "D": "noisy"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-011", "prompt": "The museum label named three materials used in the ancient necklace<u> shell beads, copper wire, and polished stones.</u>\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "necklace: shell beads, copper wire, and polished stones.", "C": "necklace shell: beads, copper wire, and polished stones.", "D": "necklace, shell beads: copper wire, and polished stones."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-012", "prompt": "In a short story, Elena finds a small notebook filled with pressed leaves in her grandfather’s workshop. Beside each leaf is a date and the name of a hiking trail. Elena runs her finger over the careful labels and asks her grandfather which trail was his favorite.\nWhich choice best describes what can reasonably be inferred about Elena?", "choices": {"A": "She is curious about the experiences recorded in her grandfather’s notebook.", "B": "She wants to remove all the labels from the notebook.", "C": "She already knows the story of every leaf in the notebook.", "D": "She believes the notebook belongs in the trash."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-013", "prompt": "A student is writing a paragraph about school service projects. The sentences are shown below.\n1. School service projects can help students contribute to their community.\n2. Projects might include collecting supplies, writing thank-you cards, or cleaning a shared space.\n3. Some projects are completed by one class, while others involve the whole school.\n4. Working on a service project can also help students practice planning and cooperation.\nWhere should sentence 3 be placed if the writer wants to describe possible project sizes before giving examples of project activities?", "choices": {"A": "Before sentence 1", "B": "After sentence 1", "C": "After sentence 2", "D": "After sentence 4"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-014", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe labels on the storage bins <u>helps</u> students return materials to the correct places.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "help", "C": "is helping", "D": "has helped"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-015", "prompt": "A class tested how many centimeters three rubber bands stretched before breaking.\nRubber band: A | B | C\nStretch before breaking: 12 cm | 18 cm | 15 cm\nWhich choice is best supported by the data?", "choices": {"A": "Rubber band B stretched the farthest before breaking.", "B": "Rubber band A stretched farther than rubber band C.", "C": "All three rubber bands stretched the same distance.", "D": "Rubber band C stretched 6 cm farther than rubber band B."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-016", "prompt": "The team’s plan is <u>to photograph local landmarks, writing captions, and to create a slideshow</u> for the history club.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "photographing local landmarks, to write captions, and creating a slideshow", "C": "to photograph local landmarks, to write captions, and to create a slideshow", "D": "local landmarks are photographed, captions are written, and to create a slideshow"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-017", "prompt": "A student is revising this sentence:\nThe article explains how the school improved its morning announcements.\nThe student wants to add information that gives specific examples of improvements. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?", "choices": {"A": "The article explains how the school improved its morning announcements by adding clearer deadlines, student-created reminders, and captions on the video screen.", "B": "The article explains that morning announcements happen near the beginning of the school day.", "C": "The article explains how students usually enter the building before classes begin.", "D": "The article explains that many schools share information with students."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-018", "prompt": "Some historians study old family cookbooks to learn about daily life. Recipes can show which ingredients were available, what tools people used, and which dishes were saved for special occasions. A cookbook may therefore reveal more than instructions for preparing food.\nWhich choice best describes the purpose of the second sentence?", "choices": {"A": "It gives examples of information family cookbooks can reveal.", "B": "It argues that cookbooks contain only modern recipes.", "C": "It explains why people stopped cooking at home.", "D": "It lists every dish served on special occasions."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-019", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nThe curtains beside the stage <u>moves</u> whenever the back door opens.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "move", "C": "is moving", "D": "has moved"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-020", "prompt": "The teacher described the student’s question as thoughtful because it connected the day’s lesson to an idea from the previous week.\nAs used in the text, “thoughtful” most nearly means", "choices": {"A": "showing careful thinking", "B": "difficult to hear", "C": "written in pencil", "D": "happening quickly"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-021", "prompt": "While researching aviator Hazel Ying Lee, a student has taken these notes:\n• Hazel Ying Lee was a Chinese American pilot.\n• She joined the Women Airforce Service Pilots during World War II.\n• She helped move military aircraft from factories to bases.\n• Her service challenged narrow ideas about who could be a pilot.\nThe student wants to explain Lee’s role in aviation history. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "Hazel Ying Lee was a Chinese American pilot who served during World War II and helped move military aircraft.", "B": "Some military aircraft were moved from factories to bases during World War II.", "C": "Women Airforce Service Pilots worked during a time of war.", "D": "Ideas about who could become a pilot have changed over time."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-022", "prompt": "The following sentence is from a student’s draft:\nAt the science expo, students displayed models, explained their data, and <u>they answered</u> questions from visitors.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "answering", "C": "answered", "D": "to answer"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-023", "prompt": "The student first marked the important dates on a timeline. ______, she wrote a short note explaining why each date mattered.\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": {"A": "Next", "B": "However", "C": "In contrast", "D": "For example"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-024", "prompt": "A student is writing an essay about the value of classroom discussion circles. Which sentence would most effectively introduce the topic by explaining a benefit to students?", "choices": {"A": "Classroom discussion circles can help students listen closely, share ideas, and respond respectfully to classmates.", "B": "Some discussion circles happen when chairs are moved away from desks.", "C": "Students may sit on chairs, stools, or the floor during a discussion.", "D": "Many classrooms include posters, bookshelves, and windows."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-025", "prompt": "The poetry contest, <u>which accepts entries from all grade levels</u> will announce winners in May.\nWhich choice completes the text so that it follows the rules of Standard English?", "choices": {"A": "NO CHANGE", "B": "which accepts entries from all grade levels,", "C": "which, accepts entries from all grade levels", "D": "which accepts entries from all grade levels;"}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-026", "prompt": "Researchers studying raccoons have found that the animals can remember how to solve certain puzzles for months after first learning them. This memory may help raccoons return to food sources or solve problems in changing environments. The findings suggest that raccoons can use past experience to guide later behavior.\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": {"A": "Raccoons may use memory from past experience to solve later problems.", "B": "Raccoons forget every puzzle immediately after solving it.", "C": "Researchers study raccoons because the animals avoid food sources.", "D": "Changing environments prevent raccoons from remembering anything."}}, {"id": "RW12-M2-027", "prompt": "While researching school tech-helper programs, a student has taken these notes:\n• Tech-helper programs train students to assist with classroom technology.\n• Helpers may set up projectors, organize charging carts, or explain basic tools.\n• Students can practice problem solving and communication.\n• Helpers must follow teacher directions and handle equipment carefully.\nThe student wants to present both a benefit and a responsibility of school tech-helper programs. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes?", "choices": {"A": "School tech-helper programs may involve projectors, charging carts, and classroom tools.", "B": "School tech-helper programs can help students practice problem solving and communication, but helpers must follow directions and handle equipment carefully.", "C": "Some students learn to assist teachers with classroom technology.", "D": "Helpers may organize charging carts before or after classes."}}]]}, {"id": "math-module-1", "label": "Module 1", "exams": [[{"id": "MATH1-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to the equation 3x + 7 = 22?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "7", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-002", "prompt": "A linear function has the values shown.\nx | 0 | 1 | 2\ny | 1 | 3 | 5\nIf the pattern continues, what is the value of y when x = 5?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "10", "C": "11", "D": "13"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-003", "prompt": "A jacket originally costs $40. It is on sale for 15% off. What is the sale price of the jacket?", "choices": {"A": "$25", "B": "$30", "C": "$34", "D": "$38"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-004", "prompt": "In a box, the ratio of red pencils to blue pencils is 3 to 5. If there are 32 pencils in the box, how many are red?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "12", "C": "15", "D": "20"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-005", "prompt": "A rectangle has a length of 9 centimeters and a width of 4 centimeters. What is the area of the rectangle?", "choices": {"A": "13 cm²", "B": "26 cm²", "C": "36 cm²", "D": "40 cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-006", "prompt": "What is the mean of the numbers 6, 8, 10, and 12?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "9", "C": "10", "D": "11"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of n that make n + 4 ≤ 13 true?", "choices": {"A": "n ≤ 9", "B": "n ≥ 9", "C": "n ≤ 17", "D": "n ≥ 17"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\ny = x + 2\ny = 2x − 1\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(1, 3)", "B": "(2, 4)", "C": "(3, 5)", "D": "(5, 7)"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of 2³ × 2²?", "choices": {"A": "16", "B": "24", "C": "32", "D": "64"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-010", "prompt": "A right triangle has legs with lengths 6 inches and 8 inches. What is the length of the hypotenuse?", "choices": {"A": "10 inches", "B": "12 inches", "C": "14 inches", "D": "48 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-011", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 4(a + 3) − 2a?", "choices": {"A": "2a + 3", "B": "2a + 12", "C": "6a + 3", "D": "6a + 12"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-012", "prompt": "A plant’s height, in inches, is modeled by a linear pattern. At week 1, the plant is 9 inches tall. At week 3, it is 15 inches tall. If the plant continues to grow at the same rate, how tall will it be at week 5?", "choices": {"A": "18 inches", "B": "19 inches", "C": "21 inches", "D": "24 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-013", "prompt": "A bag contains 5 blue marbles, 3 green marbles, and 2 yellow marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability that the marble is not green?", "choices": {"A": "3/10", "B": "5/10", "C": "7/10", "D": "8/10"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-014", "prompt": "What is the solution to x/4 + 6 = 11?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "17", "C": "20", "D": "44"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-015", "prompt": "What is the slope of the line that passes through the points (2, 5) and (6, 13)?", "choices": {"A": "1", "B": "2", "C": "4", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-016", "prompt": "A rectangular prism has a length of 5 inches, a width of 3 inches, and a height of 4 inches. What is its volume?", "choices": {"A": "12 in³", "B": "20 in³", "C": "47 in³", "D": "60 in³"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-017", "prompt": "A number increases from 50 to 60. What is the percent increase?", "choices": {"A": "10%", "B": "15%", "C": "20%", "D": "25%"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = x² + 2, what is f(4)?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "14", "C": "16", "D": "18"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-019", "prompt": "Two angles form a straight line. One angle measures 115°. What is the measure of the other angle?", "choices": {"A": "55°", "B": "65°", "C": "75°", "D": "115°"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-020", "prompt": "A fitness center charges a $12 registration fee plus $4 per class. If a student pays a total of $44, how many classes did the student take?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "11", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-021", "prompt": "What is the median of the data set 4, 7, 9, 11, 14?", "choices": {"A": "7", "B": "9", "C": "10", "D": "11"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M1-022", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 6x − 3(2 − x)?", "choices": {"A": "3x − 6", "B": "9x − 6", "C": "9x + 6", "D": "12x − 6"}}], [{"id": "MATH2-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to the equation 2m + 7 = 31?", "choices": {"A": "10", "B": "12", "C": "14", "D": "19"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-002", "prompt": "The table shows a linear relationship between x and y.\nx | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6\ny | 10 | 14 | 18 | 22\nIf the relationship continues, what is the value of y when x = 7?", "choices": {"A": "23", "B": "24", "C": "25", "D": "28"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-003", "prompt": "A student completed 18 of 24 assignments. What percent of the assignments did the student complete?", "choices": {"A": "60%", "B": "72%", "C": "75%", "D": "80%"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-004", "prompt": "A punch recipe uses 3 cups of juice for every 2 cups of sparkling water. If 12 cups of juice are used, how many cups of sparkling water are needed?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "10", "D": "18"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-005", "prompt": "A triangle has a base of 12 centimeters and a height of 5 centimeters. What is the area of the triangle?", "choices": {"A": "17 cm²", "B": "30 cm²", "C": "60 cm²", "D": "120 cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-006", "prompt": "The numbers of books checked out from a library on five days were 21, 18, 25, 30, and 16. What is the range of these numbers?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "12", "C": "14", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of p that make 5p > 30 true?", "choices": {"A": "p > 6", "B": "p < 6", "C": "p > 25", "D": "p < 25"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\nx + y = 11\nx − y = 3\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(4, 7)", "B": "(5, 6)", "C": "(7, 4)", "D": "(8, 3)"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of 5⁴ ÷ 5²?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "10", "C": "25", "D": "125"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-010", "prompt": "The points A(−3, 2) and B(4, 2) are plotted in the coordinate plane. What is the distance between points A and B?", "choices": {"A": "1", "B": "5", "C": "7", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-011", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 2(3b − 4) + 5b?", "choices": {"A": "6b − 3", "B": "8b − 8", "C": "11b − 8", "D": "11b + 8"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation c = 80 − 6w gives the number of cards c left in a box after w weeks. What does the number 6 represent in this situation?", "choices": {"A": "The box starts with 6 cards.", "B": "The box starts with 80 cards.", "C": "The number of cards decreases by 6 each week.", "D": "The number of weeks increases by 6 each card."}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-013", "prompt": "A spinner has 8 equal sections. Exactly 3 of the sections are shaded. If the spinner is spun once, what is the probability that it lands on a shaded section?", "choices": {"A": "1/8", "B": "3/8", "C": "5/8", "D": "3/5"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-014", "prompt": "What is the solution to 18 − x = 11?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "11", "D": "29"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-015", "prompt": "A line has a slope of −2 and passes through the point (0, 5). Which equation represents the line?", "choices": {"A": "y = −2x + 5", "B": "y = 2x + 5", "C": "y = −5x + 2", "D": "y = 5x − 2"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-016", "prompt": "A cube has side length 4 inches. What is the volume of the cube?", "choices": {"A": "12 in³", "B": "16 in³", "C": "48 in³", "D": "64 in³"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-017", "prompt": "A class has 28 students. If 7 students are absent, what fraction of the class is absent?", "choices": {"A": "1/7", "B": "1/4", "C": "3/4", "D": "7/21"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = 3x − 4, what is f(6)?", "choices": {"A": "10", "B": "14", "C": "18", "D": "22"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-019", "prompt": "An angle measures 42°. What is the measure of its complement?", "choices": {"A": "38°", "B": "48°", "C": "90°", "D": "138°"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-020", "prompt": "A store sells notebooks in packs of 6. Which equation can be used to find the total number of notebooks n in p packs?", "choices": {"A": "n = p + 6", "B": "n = p − 6", "C": "n = 6p", "D": "n = p/6"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of minutes a student practiced piano each day.\nDay | Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday\nMinutes | 20 | 35 | 30 | 25\nHow many total minutes did the student practice during these four days?", "choices": {"A": "90", "B": "100", "C": "110", "D": "120"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes x² = 49 true?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "7", "C": "14", "D": "21"}}], [{"id": "MATH3-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to x/3 − 2 = 7?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "21", "C": "27", "D": "33"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 5c − (2c − 9)?", "choices": {"A": "3c − 9", "B": "3c + 9", "C": "7c − 9", "D": "7c + 9"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-003", "prompt": "A student reads 150 pages in 5 days at a constant rate. How many pages does the student read per day?", "choices": {"A": "25", "B": "30", "C": "35", "D": "45"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-004", "prompt": "The number 18 is 30% of what number?", "choices": {"A": "48", "B": "54", "C": "60", "D": "72"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-005", "prompt": "A trapezoid has bases of length 8 centimeters and 12 centimeters. Its height is 5 centimeters. What is the area of the trapezoid? Use A = (1/2)(b₁ + b₂)h.", "choices": {"A": "25 cm²", "B": "40 cm²", "C": "50 cm²", "D": "100 cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-006", "prompt": "The mean of four test scores is 80. Three of the scores are 72, 84, and 88. What is the fourth score?", "choices": {"A": "74", "B": "76", "C": "78", "D": "80"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of s that make 2s + 1 < 15 true?", "choices": {"A": "s < 7", "B": "s > 7", "C": "s < 8", "D": "s > 8"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\ny = 3x\nx + y = 20\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(4, 16)", "B": "(5, 15)", "C": "(10, 10)", "D": "(15, 5)"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of √81 + 4?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "13", "C": "17", "D": "85"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-010", "prompt": "The points P(−2, −5) and Q(−2, 6) are plotted in the coordinate plane. What is the distance between points P and Q?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "8", "C": "11", "D": "13"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-011", "prompt": "A gym charges $9 per visit. Which equation gives the total cost c, in dollars, for v visits?", "choices": {"A": "c = v + 9", "B": "c = v − 9", "C": "c = 9v", "D": "c = 9/v"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation t = 45 − 3m gives the number of minutes t left in a study session after m minutes have passed. What is the value of t when m = 8?", "choices": {"A": "21", "B": "24", "C": "37", "D": "53"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-013", "prompt": "A jar contains 6 black buttons, 4 white buttons, and 5 gray buttons. If one button is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is white?", "choices": {"A": "4/15", "B": "5/15", "C": "6/15", "D": "11/15"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-014", "prompt": "What is the solution to 5n − 6 = 19?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "13", "D": "25"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-015", "prompt": "The ordered pairs (1, 7), (2, 11), and (3, 15) lie on the graph of a linear function. Which equation represents this function?", "choices": {"A": "y = 3x + 4", "B": "y = 4x + 3", "C": "y = 5x + 2", "D": "y = 7x"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-016", "prompt": "A rectangular floor is 10 feet long and 6 feet wide. A rug covers 24 square feet of the floor. How many square feet of the floor are not covered by the rug?", "choices": {"A": "24", "B": "30", "C": "36", "D": "60"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-017", "prompt": "A value of 80 is decreased by 25%. What is the new value?", "choices": {"A": "20", "B": "55", "C": "60", "D": "75"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function g(x) = x² − 1, what is g(5)?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "16", "C": "24", "D": "26"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-019", "prompt": "Two angles are vertical angles. One of the angles measures 68°. What is the measure of the other angle?", "choices": {"A": "22°", "B": "68°", "C": "112°", "D": "180°"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-020", "prompt": "A bus travels 42 miles in 1.5 hours at a constant speed. What is the speed of the bus in miles per hour?", "choices": {"A": "21", "B": "28", "C": "36", "D": "63"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of cans collected by a club during three weeks.\nWeek | 1 | 2 | 3\nCans collected | 38 | 44 | 51\nHow many cans did the club collect in all during these three weeks?", "choices": {"A": "95", "B": "113", "C": "123", "D": "133"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes 4x − 10 = 2x + 6 true?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "10"}}], [{"id": "MATH4-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to (r + 6)/3 = 9?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "21", "C": "27", "D": "33"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 7d + 4 − (3d + 10)?", "choices": {"A": "4d − 6", "B": "4d + 14", "C": "10d − 6", "D": "10d + 14"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-003", "prompt": "A printer prints 84 pages in 7 minutes at a constant rate. How many pages does the printer print per minute?", "choices": {"A": "10", "B": "12", "C": "14", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-004", "prompt": "A display has 150 books. Of these books, 45 are mystery books. What percent of the books are mystery books?", "choices": {"A": "15%", "B": "25%", "C": "30%", "D": "45%"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-005", "prompt": "A parallelogram has a base of 11 inches and a height of 6 inches. What is the area of the parallelogram?", "choices": {"A": "17 in²", "B": "34 in²", "C": "66 in²", "D": "72 in²"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-006", "prompt": "The number of songs in four playlists are 14, 18, 21, and 27. What is the mean number of songs in these playlists?", "choices": {"A": "18", "B": "20", "C": "21", "D": "24"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of x that make x − 8 ≥ 13 true?", "choices": {"A": "x ≥ 5", "B": "x ≤ 5", "C": "x ≥ 21", "D": "x ≤ 21"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\ny = x − 4\nx + y = 18\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(7, 11)", "B": "(9, 5)", "C": "(11, 7)", "D": "(14, 4)"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of 3² + 4²?", "choices": {"A": "7", "B": "14", "C": "25", "D": "49"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-010", "prompt": "A point has coordinates (−5, 4). It is moved 6 units to the right and 3 units down. What are the coordinates of the new point?", "choices": {"A": "(−11, 1)", "B": "(−2, 10)", "C": "(1, 1)", "D": "(1, 7)"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-011", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 3(2x + 5) − x?", "choices": {"A": "5x + 5", "B": "5x + 15", "C": "6x + 15", "D": "7x + 15"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation a = 12 + 4t gives the total amount a, in dollars, in an account after t weeks. What does the number 12 represent in this situation?", "choices": {"A": "The amount added each week", "B": "The starting amount in the account", "C": "The number of weeks", "D": "The total amount after 4 weeks"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-013", "prompt": "A bag contains 9 red tiles and 6 blue tiles. If one tile is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is blue?", "choices": {"A": "2/5", "B": "3/5", "C": "6/9", "D": "9/15"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-014", "prompt": "If 5z = 45, what is the value of z − 2?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "9", "D": "43"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-015", "prompt": "The ordered pairs (0, 2), (2, 8), and (4, 14) lie on the graph of a linear function. What is the slope of the function?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "6"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-016", "prompt": "A rectangular wall is 8 feet wide and 9 feet tall. A window on the wall has an area of 15 square feet. What is the area of the wall not covered by the window?", "choices": {"A": "57 square feet", "B": "63 square feet", "C": "72 square feet", "D": "87 square feet"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-017", "prompt": "A movie ticket costs $12. A student discount reduces the cost by $3. What percent of the original cost is the discount?", "choices": {"A": "20%", "B": "25%", "C": "30%", "D": "40%"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = 2x², what is f(3)?", "choices": {"A": "12", "B": "18", "C": "36", "D": "72"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-019", "prompt": "In a triangle, two angles measure 40° and 75°. What is the measure of the third angle?", "choices": {"A": "55°", "B": "65°", "C": "75°", "D": "115°"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-020", "prompt": "A tank contains 96 gallons of water. Water is drained at a rate of 8 gallons per minute. How many gallons of water are left after 5 minutes?", "choices": {"A": "40", "B": "56", "C": "88", "D": "104"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of points a team scored in four games.\nGame | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nPoints | 12 | 16 | 9 | 18\nWhat is the total number of points the team scored in these four games?", "choices": {"A": "45", "B": "50", "C": "55", "D": "60"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes |x| = 8 true?", "choices": {"A": "−8", "B": "−4", "C": "0", "D": "16"}}], [{"id": "MATH5-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 4q − 9 = 19?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "9", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 2(5a − 1) − 3(a + 4)?", "choices": {"A": "7a − 14", "B": "7a + 10", "C": "13a − 14", "D": "13a + 10"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-003", "prompt": "A machine prints 72 labels in 9 minutes at a constant rate. How many labels does the machine print per minute?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "9", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-004", "prompt": "At a school fair, 35% of the 60 volunteers worked at game booths. How many volunteers worked at game booths?", "choices": {"A": "18", "B": "21", "C": "24", "D": "35"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-005", "prompt": "A rectangular mat is 7 feet long and 5 feet wide. What is the perimeter of the mat?", "choices": {"A": "12 feet", "B": "24 feet", "C": "35 feet", "D": "70 feet"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-006", "prompt": "The data set 6, 9, 9, 12, 14 shows the number of minutes five students spent warming up before a race. What is the mode of the data set?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "9", "C": "10", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of x that make 3 + x/2 ≤ 10 true?", "choices": {"A": "x ≤ 7", "B": "x ≥ 7", "C": "x ≤ 14", "D": "x ≥ 14"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\n2x + y = 13\ny = 5\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(3, 5)", "B": "(4, 5)", "C": "(5, 3)", "D": "(9, 5)"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of 4³ ÷ 8?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-010", "prompt": "The point (3, −2) is reflected across the x-axis. What are the coordinates of the reflected point?", "choices": {"A": "(−3, −2)", "B": "(−3, 2)", "C": "(3, 2)", "D": "(2, 3)"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-011", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 9y − 4y + 6?", "choices": {"A": "5y + 6", "B": "5y − 6", "C": "13y + 6", "D": "13y − 6"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation w = 25 + 2d gives the total weight w, in pounds, of a box after d books are added. What does the number 2 represent in this situation?", "choices": {"A": "The box weighs 2 pounds before books are added.", "B": "Each book adds 2 pounds to the total weight.", "C": "There are 2 books in the box.", "D": "The total weight is 2 pounds after d days."}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-013", "prompt": "A bowl contains 4 apple slices, 7 orange slices, and 9 pear slices. If one slice is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is an orange slice?", "choices": {"A": "4/20", "B": "7/20", "C": "9/20", "D": "13/20"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-014", "prompt": "If u + 14 = 3u, what is the value of u?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "10", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-015", "prompt": "A line passes through the points (0, −3) and (2, 5). What is the slope of the line?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-016", "prompt": "A square tile has side length 9 centimeters. What is the area of the tile?", "choices": {"A": "18 cm²", "B": "36 cm²", "C": "81 cm²", "D": "90 cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-017", "prompt": "A tank is filled with 30 gallons of water. Then 12 gallons are added. What percent of the original amount was added?", "choices": {"A": "25%", "B": "30%", "C": "40%", "D": "60%"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = 10 − x², what is f(2)?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-019", "prompt": "A pair of supplementary angles has one angle that measures 124°. What is the measure of the other angle?", "choices": {"A": "34°", "B": "46°", "C": "56°", "D": "66°"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-020", "prompt": "A music app charges $5 per month plus $1 for each downloaded song. If a user downloads 8 songs in one month, what is the total charge?", "choices": {"A": "$8", "B": "$13", "C": "$18", "D": "$40"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of laps a swimmer completed on four days.\nDay | Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday\nLaps | 12 | 15 | 18 | 15\nWhat was the total number of laps completed on Tuesday and Thursday?", "choices": {"A": "27", "B": "30", "C": "33", "D": "60"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes x² − 4 = 32 true?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "8"}}], [{"id": "MATH6-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 5 + y/2 = 17?", "choices": {"A": "12", "B": "22", "C": "24", "D": "34"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 4(2n + 1) + 3(1 − n)?", "choices": {"A": "5n + 7", "B": "5n + 1", "C": "11n + 7", "D": "11n + 1"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-003", "prompt": "A car uses 14 gallons of gas to travel 392 miles. At this rate, how many miles does the car travel per gallon of gas?", "choices": {"A": "24", "B": "26", "C": "28", "D": "30"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-004", "prompt": "The number 48 is what percent of 160?", "choices": {"A": "20%", "B": "25%", "C": "30%", "D": "40%"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-005", "prompt": "A circle has a radius of 4 meters. What is the area of the circle? Use A = πr².", "choices": {"A": "8π m²", "B": "16π m²", "C": "32π m²", "D": "64π m²"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-006", "prompt": "The range of a data set is 17. If the greatest value in the data set is 28, what is the least value?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "11", "C": "17", "D": "45"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of w that make 12 ≥ 3w true?", "choices": {"A": "w ≥ 4", "B": "w ≤ 4", "C": "w ≥ 9", "D": "w ≤ 9"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\nx = 2y\nx + y = 18\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(6, 12)", "B": "(9, 9)", "C": "(12, 6)", "D": "(18, 9)"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of 2⁴ − 3?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "8", "C": "13", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-010", "prompt": "In the coordinate plane, point A has coordinates (−4, 3), and point B has coordinates (2, 3). What is the midpoint of segment AB?", "choices": {"A": "(−1, 3)", "B": "(−1, 6)", "C": "(3, −1)", "D": "(6, 3)"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-011", "prompt": "A rectangle has an area of 45 square inches. Its width is 5 inches. What is its length?", "choices": {"A": "8 inches", "B": "9 inches", "C": "10 inches", "D": "40 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation P = 18 + 5s gives the total number of points P a player has after scoring s additional goals. How many total points does the player have after scoring 6 additional goals?", "choices": {"A": "23", "B": "29", "C": "30", "D": "48"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-013", "prompt": "A number cube has faces labeled 1 through 6. If the cube is rolled once, what is the probability of rolling a number greater than 4?", "choices": {"A": "1/6", "B": "1/3", "C": "1/2", "D": "2/3"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-014", "prompt": "If 3r + 2 = 20, what is the value of r + 4?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "10", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-015", "prompt": "The table shows a linear relationship between x and y.\nx | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\ny | 9 | 14 | 19 | 24\nWhat is the slope of the relationship?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "8", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-016", "prompt": "A rectangular playground is 15 meters long and 10 meters wide. A square sandbox with side length 4 meters is inside the playground. How many square meters of the playground are not part of the sandbox?", "choices": {"A": "46", "B": "134", "C": "146", "D": "166"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-017", "prompt": "The price of a book increased from $20 to $23. What was the percent increase?", "choices": {"A": "3%", "B": "10%", "C": "15%", "D": "20%"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = 2x + 9, what is f(−3)?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "6", "C": "12", "D": "15"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-019", "prompt": "A triangle has angle measures 55°, 55°, and x°. What is the value of x?", "choices": {"A": "55", "B": "70", "C": "90", "D": "110"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-020", "prompt": "A streaming service charges $8 each month. A customer also pays a one-time setup fee of $15. What is the total amount the customer pays for 4 months of service, including the setup fee?", "choices": {"A": "$23", "B": "$32", "C": "$47", "D": "$60"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of tickets sold during three hours.\nHour | 1 | 2 | 3\nTickets sold | 18 | 25 | 31\nHow many more tickets were sold during hour 3 than during hour 1?", "choices": {"A": "7", "B": "13", "C": "25", "D": "49"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes x² + 5 = 30 true?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "25"}}], [{"id": "MATH7-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 2(x + 4) = 30?", "choices": {"A": "7", "B": "11", "C": "15", "D": "19"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 6m − 2(4 − m) + 3?", "choices": {"A": "4m − 5", "B": "4m + 11", "C": "8m − 5", "D": "8m + 11"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-003", "prompt": "A store sells 4 pounds of apples for $7.60. At this rate, what is the cost of 1 pound of apples?", "choices": {"A": "$1.60", "B": "$1.80", "C": "$1.90", "D": "$2.10"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-004", "prompt": "A student answered 24 questions correctly on a quiz with 80 questions. What percent of the questions did the student answer correctly?", "choices": {"A": "24%", "B": "30%", "C": "40%", "D": "56%"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-005", "prompt": "A regular hexagon has a side length of 6 centimeters. What is the perimeter of the hexagon?", "choices": {"A": "12 centimeters", "B": "30 centimeters", "C": "36 centimeters", "D": "42 centimeters"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-006", "prompt": "The numbers of visitors to a museum on five days were 42, 55, 48, 61, and 44. What is the median number of visitors?", "choices": {"A": "44", "B": "48", "C": "50", "D": "55"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of v that make −2v + 5 ≥ 13 true?", "choices": {"A": "v ≤ −4", "B": "v ≥ −4", "C": "v ≤ 4", "D": "v ≥ 4"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\nx = y + 4\nx + y = 22\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(9, 13)", "B": "(11, 11)", "C": "(13, 9)", "D": "(18, 4)"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of 3 × 2³ + 1?", "choices": {"A": "13", "B": "19", "C": "25", "D": "49"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-010", "prompt": "The point (−6, 5) is reflected across the y-axis. What are the coordinates of the reflected point?", "choices": {"A": "(6, 5)", "B": "(−6, −5)", "C": "(5, −6)", "D": "(6, −5)"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-011", "prompt": "A rectangle has a length of 13 inches and a width of 4 inches. What is the perimeter of the rectangle?", "choices": {"A": "17 inches", "B": "34 inches", "C": "52 inches", "D": "68 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation H = 72 − 4d gives the number of hours H remaining on a project after d days of work. How many hours remain after 9 days?", "choices": {"A": "28", "B": "36", "C": "63", "D": "68"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-013", "prompt": "A basket contains 5 peaches, 8 plums, and 7 pears. If one fruit is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is a peach?", "choices": {"A": "1/4", "B": "5/20", "C": "8/20", "D": "15/20"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-014", "prompt": "If 2t − 5 = 17, what is the value of 3t?", "choices": {"A": "11", "B": "22", "C": "33", "D": "51"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-015", "prompt": "The table shows a linear relationship between x and y.\nx | 0 | 3 | 6 | 9\ny | 4 | 10 | 16 | 22\nWhat is the slope of the relationship?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "6"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-016", "prompt": "A square garden has side length 12 feet. A path along one side of the garden has an area of 24 square feet. How many square feet of the garden are not part of the path?", "choices": {"A": "96", "B": "120", "C": "144", "D": "168"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-017", "prompt": "A class goal was to collect 200 cans for a food drive. The class collected 230 cans. By what percent did the class exceed its goal?", "choices": {"A": "10%", "B": "15%", "C": "20%", "D": "30%"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = x² + x, what is f(4)?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "16", "C": "20", "D": "24"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-019", "prompt": "Two angles are complementary. One angle measures 27°. What is the measure of the other angle?", "choices": {"A": "53°", "B": "63°", "C": "73°", "D": "153°"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-020", "prompt": "A delivery company charges $4 for each package plus a $9 service fee. What is the total charge for delivering 6 packages?", "choices": {"A": "$13", "B": "$24", "C": "$33", "D": "$54"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of minutes four students spent exercising.\nStudent | J | K | L | M\nMinutes | 25 | 40 | 35 | 50\nHow many more minutes did student M exercise than student J?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "20", "C": "25", "D": "75"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes √x = 9 true?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "9", "C": "18", "D": "81"}}], [{"id": "MATH8-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to (2x − 5)/3 = 7?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "11", "C": "13", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 8 − 3(2 − k)?", "choices": {"A": "3k + 2", "B": "3k + 14", "C": "−3k + 2", "D": "−3k + 14"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-003", "prompt": "A pack of 5 identical notebooks costs $11.25. What is the cost of 1 notebook?", "choices": {"A": "$1.75", "B": "$2.00", "C": "$2.25", "D": "$2.50"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-004", "prompt": "A team won 14 of its 40 games. What percent of its games did the team win?", "choices": {"A": "14%", "B": "25%", "C": "35%", "D": "40%"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-005", "prompt": "A triangle has side lengths 7 inches, 10 inches, and 12 inches. What is the perimeter of the triangle?", "choices": {"A": "19 inches", "B": "24 inches", "C": "29 inches", "D": "84 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-006", "prompt": "The table shows the number of pages four students read.\nStudent | A | B | C | D\nPages | 32 | 28 | 41 | 35\nWhat is the total number of pages read by the four students?", "choices": {"A": "104", "B": "126", "C": "136", "D": "146"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of x that make 4x − 3 < 17 true?", "choices": {"A": "x < 5", "B": "x > 5", "C": "x < 20", "D": "x > 20"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\ny = 2x + 1\ny = 9\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(3, 9)", "B": "(4, 9)", "C": "(5, 9)", "D": "(9, 4)"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of 6² − 5²?", "choices": {"A": "1", "B": "11", "C": "25", "D": "36"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-010", "prompt": "A point has coordinates (4, −7). It is moved 5 units left and 9 units up. What are the coordinates of the new point?", "choices": {"A": "(−1, 2)", "B": "(−1, −16)", "C": "(9, 2)", "D": "(9, −16)"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-011", "prompt": "A rectangle has a perimeter of 30 centimeters. Its length is 9 centimeters. What is its width?", "choices": {"A": "6 centimeters", "B": "12 centimeters", "C": "15 centimeters", "D": "21 centimeters"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation S = 120 − 15b gives the number of stickers S left after b boxes of stickers are packed. How many stickers are left after 4 boxes are packed?", "choices": {"A": "45", "B": "60", "C": "75", "D": "105"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-013", "prompt": "A jar contains 3 red beads, 10 blue beads, and 7 green beads. If one bead is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is red?", "choices": {"A": "3/20", "B": "7/20", "C": "10/20", "D": "17/20"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-014", "prompt": "If 4p − 3 = 29, what is the value of p/2?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "8", "C": "13", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-015", "prompt": "A linear function has the values shown.\nx | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\ny | 6 | 9 | 12 | 15\nWhich equation represents the function?", "choices": {"A": "y = 2x + 4", "B": "y = 3x + 3", "C": "y = 4x + 2", "D": "y = 6x"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-016", "prompt": "A rectangular banner is 11 feet wide and 3 feet tall. A logo on the banner covers 6 square feet. How many square feet of the banner are not covered by the logo?", "choices": {"A": "17", "B": "27", "C": "33", "D": "39"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-017", "prompt": "A number is increased from 30 to 39. What is the percent increase?", "choices": {"A": "9%", "B": "15%", "C": "25%", "D": "30%"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = 4x − 11, what is f(7)?", "choices": {"A": "17", "B": "21", "C": "28", "D": "39"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-019", "prompt": "In a triangle, one angle measures 90° and another angle measures 38°. What is the measure of the third angle?", "choices": {"A": "38°", "B": "52°", "C": "62°", "D": "128°"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-020", "prompt": "A library charges a late fee of $0.25 per day for each overdue book. How much is the late fee for 3 overdue books after 4 days?", "choices": {"A": "$1.00", "B": "$2.00", "C": "$3.00", "D": "$4.00"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of ounces of water in a bottle after different amounts of water are poured out.\nOunces poured out | 0 | 4 | 8 | 12\nOunces left | 28 | 24 | 20 | 16\nHow many ounces are left after 12 ounces are poured out?", "choices": {"A": "12", "B": "16", "C": "20", "D": "28"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes x² = 121 true?", "choices": {"A": "10", "B": "11", "C": "12", "D": "121"}}], [{"id": "MATH9-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 9 − x/5 = 3?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "25", "C": "30", "D": "60"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 3(4a + 2) − 2(5 − a)?", "choices": {"A": "10a − 4", "B": "10a + 16", "C": "14a − 4", "D": "14a + 16"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-003", "prompt": "A carton contains 96 ounces of juice. The juice is poured equally into 12 bottles. How many ounces of juice are in each bottle?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "12", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-004", "prompt": "There are 90 students in a grade. Of these students, 27 ride the bus to school. What percent of the students ride the bus to school?", "choices": {"A": "27%", "B": "30%", "C": "33%", "D": "63%"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-005", "prompt": "A rhombus has diagonals with lengths 10 centimeters and 14 centimeters. What is the area of the rhombus? Use A = (1/2)d₁d₂.", "choices": {"A": "24 cm²", "B": "48 cm²", "C": "70 cm²", "D": "140 cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-006", "prompt": "The numbers of minutes five students spent on a homework assignment are 18, 22, 25, 31, and 34. What is the median of these numbers?", "choices": {"A": "22", "B": "25", "C": "26", "D": "31"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of x that make 16 < 4 − 3x true?", "choices": {"A": "x < −4", "B": "x > −4", "C": "x < 4", "D": "x > 4"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\ny = 4x − 3\n2x + y = 15\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(2, 5)", "B": "(3, 9)", "C": "(4, 13)", "D": "(6, 9)"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of 3² × 3?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "12", "C": "18", "D": "27"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-010", "prompt": "The point (−3, 7) is reflected across the origin. What are the coordinates of the reflected point?", "choices": {"A": "(3, −7)", "B": "(−3, −7)", "C": "(3, 7)", "D": "(7, −3)"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-011", "prompt": "A circle has a radius of 5 inches. What is the circumference of the circle? Use C = 2πr.", "choices": {"A": "5π inches", "B": "10π inches", "C": "25π inches", "D": "50π inches"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation T = 2.5h + 6 gives the total distance T, in miles, a person has walked after h hours. What does the number 6 represent?", "choices": {"A": "The person walks 6 miles each hour.", "B": "The person had already walked 6 miles before h hours.", "C": "The person walks for 6 hours.", "D": "The total distance is always 6 miles."}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-013", "prompt": "A number cube has faces labeled 1 through 6. If the cube is rolled once, what is the probability of rolling an even number less than 5?", "choices": {"A": "1/6", "B": "1/3", "C": "1/2", "D": "2/3"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-014", "prompt": "If 6g − 8 = 28, what is the value of g − 1?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "7", "D": "20"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-015", "prompt": "A line has equation y = 5x − 2. What is the y-intercept of the line?", "choices": {"A": "−2", "B": "2", "C": "5", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-016", "prompt": "A rectangular closet floor is 6 feet long and 5 feet wide. A square storage bin with side length 2 feet is placed on the floor. How many square feet of the closet floor are not covered by the bin?", "choices": {"A": "20", "B": "24", "C": "26", "D": "34"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-017", "prompt": "A club had 32 members last year. This year, the club has 40 members. What is the percent increase in the number of members?", "choices": {"A": "8%", "B": "20%", "C": "25%", "D": "32%"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = x² − 3x, what is f(6)?", "choices": {"A": "12", "B": "18", "C": "24", "D": "36"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-019", "prompt": "A straight angle is divided into two angles. One angle measures 78°. What is the measure of the other angle?", "choices": {"A": "78°", "B": "92°", "C": "102°", "D": "112°"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-020", "prompt": "A science lab uses 15 milliliters of solution for each trial. How many milliliters of solution are needed for 8 trials?", "choices": {"A": "23", "B": "80", "C": "120", "D": "150"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of points a player scored in three games.\nGame | 1 | 2 | 3\nPoints | 17 | 24 | 19\nWhat is the mean number of points scored in the three games?", "choices": {"A": "19", "B": "20", "C": "21", "D": "60"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes x² − 9 = 40 true?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "9", "D": "49"}}], [{"id": "MATH10-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 7 + (2x)/5 = 13?", "choices": {"A": "10", "B": "12", "C": "15", "D": "20"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 5(3 − 2p) + 4p?", "choices": {"A": "15 − 6p", "B": "15 − 14p", "C": "19 − 6p", "D": "19 − 14p"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-003", "prompt": "A machine fills 48 bottles in 6 minutes at a constant rate. How many bottles does the machine fill in 1 minute?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "42", "D": "54"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-004", "prompt": "A school club has 85 members. Of the members, 40% are in grade 9. How many members are in grade 9?", "choices": {"A": "30", "B": "34", "C": "40", "D": "45"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-005", "prompt": "A right triangle has legs with lengths 9 centimeters and 10 centimeters. What is the area of the triangle?", "choices": {"A": "19 cm²", "B": "45 cm²", "C": "90 cm²", "D": "100 cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-006", "prompt": "The table shows the number of miles four students biked in one week.\nStudent | A | B | C | D\nMiles | 11 | 15 | 13 | 17\nWhat is the mean number of miles biked by these four students?", "choices": {"A": "13", "B": "14", "C": "15", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of x that make 2(x − 3) ≥ 14 true?", "choices": {"A": "x ≥ 4", "B": "x ≤ 4", "C": "x ≥ 10", "D": "x ≤ 10"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\nx − y = 2\n2x + y = 19\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(5, 3)", "B": "(6, 4)", "C": "(7, 5)", "D": "(8, 6)"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of √64 − 2²?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "4", "C": "6", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-010", "prompt": "A point is located at (−4, −6) in the coordinate plane. In which quadrant is the point located?", "choices": {"A": "Quadrant I", "B": "Quadrant II", "C": "Quadrant III", "D": "Quadrant IV"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-011", "prompt": "Which expression represents “3 less than 4 times a number n”?", "choices": {"A": "3 − 4n", "B": "4n − 3", "C": "4(n − 3)", "D": "3n − 4"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation C = 40 + 5m gives the total cost C, in dollars, of renting equipment for m months. What does the number 5 represent in this situation?", "choices": {"A": "The number of months", "B": "The starting cost before any monthly charges", "C": "The cost added for each month", "D": "The total cost after 5 months"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-013", "prompt": "A box contains 8 green cubes, 6 yellow cubes, and 4 blue cubes. If one cube is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is not yellow?", "choices": {"A": "1/3", "B": "2/3", "C": "6/18", "D": "8/18"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-014", "prompt": "If k/4 = 9, what is the value of k − 5?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "31", "C": "36", "D": "41"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-015", "prompt": "A line passes through the points (2, 1) and (5, 10). What is the slope of the line?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-016", "prompt": "A square has an area of 49 square feet. What is the side length of the square?", "choices": {"A": "7 feet", "B": "14 feet", "C": "24.5 feet", "D": "49 feet"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-017", "prompt": "A number decreases from 75 to 60. What is the percent decrease?", "choices": {"A": "15%", "B": "20%", "C": "25%", "D": "30%"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = x² + x + 1, what is f(−2)?", "choices": {"A": "−5", "B": "−1", "C": "3", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-019", "prompt": "An isosceles triangle has a vertex angle that measures 40°. The two base angles have equal measures. What is the measure of each base angle?", "choices": {"A": "40°", "B": "50°", "C": "70°", "D": "140°"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-020", "prompt": "A bakery sells muffins for $3 each. A customer buys 5 muffins and uses a $4 coupon. What is the final cost?", "choices": {"A": "$8", "B": "$11", "C": "$15", "D": "$19"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the temperature at different times.\nTime | 8 a.m. | 10 a.m. | 12 p.m. | 2 p.m.\nTemperature | 52°F | 58°F | 66°F | 71°F\nHow many degrees did the temperature increase from 8 a.m. to 2 p.m.?", "choices": {"A": "13°F", "B": "19°F", "C": "23°F", "D": "71°F"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes x² + 4x = 21 true?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "5", "D": "7"}}], [{"id": "MATH11-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to (3x)/4 + 5 = 17?", "choices": {"A": "12", "B": "16", "C": "20", "D": "24"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 2(3u − 5) + 7(2 − u)?", "choices": {"A": "u + 4", "B": "4 − u", "C": "13u − 24", "D": "13u + 4"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-003", "prompt": "A cyclist travels 18 kilometers in 45 minutes at a constant rate. How many kilometers does the cyclist travel in 15 minutes?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "9", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-004", "prompt": "In a school chorus, the ratio of sopranos to altos is 4 to 5. If there are 36 students in the chorus, how many are altos?", "choices": {"A": "16", "B": "18", "C": "20", "D": "24"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-005", "prompt": "A semicircle has a radius of 6 centimeters. What is the area of the semicircle? Use A = (1/2)πr².", "choices": {"A": "6π cm²", "B": "12π cm²", "C": "18π cm²", "D": "36π cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-006", "prompt": "The data set 12, 14, 18, 20, 22, 28 shows the number of minutes several students spent reading. What is the median of the data set?", "choices": {"A": "18", "B": "19", "C": "20", "D": "21"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of x that make 6 − x/2 > 1 true?", "choices": {"A": "x < 10", "B": "x > 10", "C": "x < 14", "D": "x > 14"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\n3x + y = 18\ny = 6\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(4, 6)", "B": "(6, 4)", "C": "(8, 6)", "D": "(12, 6)"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of (2²)³?", "choices": {"A": "12", "B": "16", "C": "32", "D": "64"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-010", "prompt": "Point A has coordinates (−2, 5), and point B has coordinates (6, 5). What is the length of segment AB?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "10"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-011", "prompt": "A rectangle has an area of 84 square meters and a length of 12 meters. What is the width of the rectangle?", "choices": {"A": "6 meters", "B": "7 meters", "C": "8 meters", "D": "9 meters"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-012", "prompt": "The equation R = 250 − 12d gives the number of remaining flyers R after d days. What does the number 12 represent in this situation?", "choices": {"A": "The starting number of flyers", "B": "The number of flyers given out each day", "C": "The number of days flyers are given out", "D": "The number of flyers remaining after 12 days"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-013", "prompt": "A bag contains 4 square tokens, 9 circular tokens, and 7 triangular tokens. If one token is selected at random, what is the probability that it is triangular?", "choices": {"A": "4/20", "B": "7/20", "C": "9/20", "D": "13/20"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-014", "prompt": "If 2a + 5 = 3a − 7, what is the value of a?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "7", "C": "12", "D": "17"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-015", "prompt": "A line contains the points (1, 4) and (4, 16). What is the slope of the line?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "5", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-016", "prompt": "A rectangular garden is 14 feet long and 9 feet wide. A rectangular flower bed inside the garden is 5 feet long and 4 feet wide. How many square feet of the garden are not part of the flower bed?", "choices": {"A": "70", "B": "86", "C": "106", "D": "126"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-017", "prompt": "A store had 240 bags of rice in stock. By the end of the day, 15% of the bags had been sold. How many bags were sold?", "choices": {"A": "24", "B": "30", "C": "36", "D": "45"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = 3x² − 2, what is f(2)?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "10", "C": "12", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-019", "prompt": "In a triangle, the measure of one angle is 48°. The other two angles have equal measures. What is the measure of each of the other two angles?", "choices": {"A": "48°", "B": "66°", "C": "84°", "D": "132°"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-020", "prompt": "A parking garage charges $6 for the first hour and $3 for each additional hour. What is the total charge for parking for 5 hours?", "choices": {"A": "$15", "B": "$18", "C": "$21", "D": "$30"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the amount of money a student saved over several weeks.\nWeek | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nAmount saved | $8 | $16 | $24 | $32\nBased on the table, how much money did the student save in 4 weeks?", "choices": {"A": "$24", "B": "$32", "C": "$40", "D": "$80"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes x² − 16 = 48 true?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "12"}}], [{"id": "MATH12-M1-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 4x − 11 = 25?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "9", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-002", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 9 − 2(3a − 4)?", "choices": {"A": "17 − 6a", "B": "1 − 6a", "C": "6a + 1", "D": "6a + 17"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-003", "prompt": "A garden hose fills 36 gallons of water in 4 minutes at a constant rate. How many gallons does the hose fill in 1 minute?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "9", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-004", "prompt": "A class has 30 students. Of the students, 18 brought lunch from home. What percent of the students brought lunch from home?", "choices": {"A": "40%", "B": "50%", "C": "60%", "D": "70%"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-005", "prompt": "A rectangle has a length of 16 centimeters and a width of 5 centimeters. What is the perimeter of the rectangle?", "choices": {"A": "21 centimeters", "B": "42 centimeters", "C": "80 centimeters", "D": "160 centimeters"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-006", "prompt": "The data set 7, 8, 12, 13, 15, 17 shows the number of hours several students spent volunteering. What is the median of the data set?", "choices": {"A": "12", "B": "12.5", "C": "13", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-007", "prompt": "Which inequality represents all values of n that make 4n + 6 ≤ 34 true?", "choices": {"A": "n ≤ 7", "B": "n ≥ 7", "C": "n ≤ 10", "D": "n ≥ 10"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-008", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\nx + y = 17\n2x − y = 4\nWhat is the solution (x, y) to the system?", "choices": {"A": "(5, 12)", "B": "(6, 11)", "C": "(7, 10)", "D": "(8, 9)"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-009", "prompt": "What is the value of √49 + 3²?", "choices": {"A": "10", "B": "14", "C": "16", "D": "58"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-010", "prompt": "In the coordinate plane, point A has coordinates (2, −3), and point B has coordinates (2, 4). What is the length of segment AB?", "choices": {"A": "1", "B": "5", "C": "7", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-011", "prompt": "The table shows a linear relationship between x and y.\nx | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6\ny | 3 | 9 | 15 | 21\nWhich equation represents the relationship?", "choices": {"A": "y = 2x + 3", "B": "y = 3x + 3", "C": "y = 6x + 3", "D": "y = 9x + 3"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-012", "prompt": "The formula P = 2l + 2w gives the perimeter P of a rectangle with length l and width w. If P = 34 and l = 11, what is the value of w?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "12", "D": "23"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-013", "prompt": "A bag contains 11 green marbles, 4 blue marbles, and 5 orange marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is blue?", "choices": {"A": "1/5", "B": "1/4", "C": "4/15", "D": "5/20"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-014", "prompt": "If 3p − 7 = 20, what is the value of p + 5?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "12", "C": "14", "D": "27"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-015", "prompt": "A line passes through the points (1, 10) and (4, 1). What is the slope of the line?", "choices": {"A": "−4", "B": "−3", "C": "3", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-016", "prompt": "A square rug has side length 8 feet. A square table that covers 9 square feet is placed on the rug. How many square feet of the rug are not covered by the table?", "choices": {"A": "55", "B": "64", "C": "73", "D": "81"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-017", "prompt": "A store has 120 water bottles. By noon, 45 water bottles have been sold. What percent of the water bottles have been sold?", "choices": {"A": "25%", "B": "32.5%", "C": "37.5%", "D": "45%"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-018", "prompt": "For the function f(x) = x² + 2x, what is f(3)?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "12", "C": "15", "D": "21"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-019", "prompt": "In a triangle, one angle measures 35° and another angle measures 85°. What is the measure of the third angle?", "choices": {"A": "50°", "B": "60°", "C": "70°", "D": "95°"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-020", "prompt": "A school store sells pencils for $0.50 each and erasers for $0.75 each. What is the total cost of 6 pencils and 4 erasers?", "choices": {"A": "$3.00", "B": "$4.50", "C": "$6.00", "D": "$7.50"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-021", "prompt": "The table shows the number of points a team scored in the first and second halves of three games.\nGame | 1 | 2 | 3\nFirst-half points | 8 | 11 | 9\nSecond-half points | 12 | 10 | 15\nIn which game did the team score the greatest total number of points?", "choices": {"A": "Game 1", "B": "Game 2", "C": "Game 3", "D": "Games 1 and 2"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M1-022", "prompt": "Which value of x makes x² − 6x = 16 true?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "4", "C": "8", "D": "10"}}]]}, {"id": "math-module-2", "label": "Module 2 (Harder)", "exams": [[{"id": "MATH1-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to the equation 5(2x − 3) = 3x + 20?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "5", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-002", "prompt": "A line passes through the point (0, −4) and has a slope of 3. Which equation represents the line?", "choices": {"A": "y = 3x − 4", "B": "y = −3x − 4", "C": "y = 4x + 3", "D": "y = 3x + 4"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-003", "prompt": "A school club sold adult tickets and student tickets to a show. Adult tickets cost $8 each, and student tickets cost $5 each. The club sold 40 tickets and collected $260. How many adult tickets were sold?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "20", "C": "25", "D": "30"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-004", "prompt": "The function g is defined by g(x) = 2x² − 3x. What is g(−2)?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "10", "C": "14", "D": "22"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-005", "prompt": "A recipe uses 2 cups of oats for every 5 cups of flour. If a baker uses 12 cups of oats, how many cups of flour are needed?", "choices": {"A": "24", "B": "30", "C": "36", "D": "42"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the number of pages Mia read over several days.\nDay | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nTotal pages read | 18 | 36 | 54 | 72\nIf this pattern continues, which equation gives the total number of pages p Mia has read after d days?", "choices": {"A": "p = d + 18", "B": "p = 18d", "C": "p = 36d", "D": "p = 72d"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of x makes the expression 3x + 2 greater than 17?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-008", "prompt": "A square has a perimeter of 48 meters. What is the area of the square?", "choices": {"A": "64 m²", "B": "96 m²", "C": "144 m²", "D": "192 m²"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 4x² + 12x can be written in the form 4x(x + k). What is the value of k?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "8", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-010", "prompt": "A data set has the values 12, 15, 17, 18, and 23. A value of 25 is added to the data set. How does the mean change?", "choices": {"A": "It decreases by 2.", "B": "It increases by 1.", "C": "It increases by 2.", "D": "It stays the same."}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-011", "prompt": "A rectangular garden has a length that is 3 feet more than twice its width. The width is 7 feet. What is the perimeter of the garden?", "choices": {"A": "34 feet", "B": "44 feet", "C": "48 feet", "D": "68 feet"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-012", "prompt": "The graph of a line contains the points (−1, 6) and (3, −2). What is the y-intercept of the line?", "choices": {"A": "−4", "B": "2", "C": "4", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-013", "prompt": "A number is multiplied by 6, and then 9 is subtracted. The result is equal to 3 more than twice the original number. What is the original number?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "5"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-014", "prompt": "The value of a computer decreases by 20% each year. If the computer is worth $900 now, what will it be worth after 1 year?", "choices": {"A": "$180", "B": "$720", "C": "$880", "D": "$1,080"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, one acute angle measures 35°. What is the measure of the other acute angle?", "choices": {"A": "35°", "B": "45°", "C": "55°", "D": "65°"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (x + 5)(x + 2)?", "choices": {"A": "x² + 7x + 10", "B": "x² + 10x + 7", "C": "x² + 3x + 10", "D": "2x² + 7x + 10"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-017", "prompt": "A line has equation y = −2x + 9. Which point lies on the line?", "choices": {"A": "(1, 6)", "B": "(2, 5)", "C": "(3, 4)", "D": "(4, 3)"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-018", "prompt": "The table shows a linear relationship between x and y.\nx | 2 | 4 | 6 | 8\ny | 13 | 19 | 25 | 31\nWhat is the equation that represents the relationship?", "choices": {"A": "y = 2x + 9", "B": "y = 3x + 7", "C": "y = 4x + 5", "D": "y = 6x + 1"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-019", "prompt": "A cylinder has a radius of 3 centimeters and a height of 10 centimeters. What is the volume of the cylinder? Use V = πr²h.", "choices": {"A": "30π cm³", "B": "60π cm³", "C": "90π cm³", "D": "300π cm³"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-020", "prompt": "A set of cards is numbered 1 through 12. If one card is selected at random, what is the probability that the number on the card is a multiple of 3 or a multiple of 4?", "choices": {"A": "1/3", "B": "1/2", "C": "7/12", "D": "2/3"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-021", "prompt": "If 2x − 5y = 14 and x = 12, what is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "1", "B": "2", "C": "3", "D": "4"}}, {"id": "MATH1-M2-022", "prompt": "A quadratic function is defined by h(x) = x² − 6x + 8. Which value of x makes h(x) = 0?", "choices": {"A": "1", "B": "2", "C": "6", "D": "8"}}], [{"id": "MATH2-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to the equation 4x + 9 = 2x + 27?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "9", "D": "18"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-002", "prompt": "A line passes through the points (2, 3) and (6, 11). Which equation represents the line?", "choices": {"A": "y = x + 1", "B": "y = 2x − 1", "C": "y = 2x + 1", "D": "y = 4x − 5"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-003", "prompt": "A teacher bought 4 folders and 3 binders for $29. Each folder cost $2. What was the cost of each binder?", "choices": {"A": "$5", "B": "$6", "C": "$7", "D": "$8"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-004", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = x² + 4x − 1. What is h(3)?", "choices": {"A": "14", "B": "20", "C": "22", "D": "26"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-005", "prompt": "A map uses a scale in which 1 inch represents 6 miles. Two towns are 3.5 inches apart on the map. What is the actual distance between the towns?", "choices": {"A": "9.5 miles", "B": "18 miles", "C": "21 miles", "D": "24 miles"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the cost to rent a kayak for different numbers of hours.\nHours | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4\nCost, in dollars | 17 | 24 | 31 | 38\nWhich equation gives the cost c, in dollars, to rent the kayak for h hours?", "choices": {"A": "c = 7h + 10", "B": "c = 10h + 7", "C": "c = 17h", "D": "c = 24h − 7"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of r is a solution to 3r − 8 ≥ 13?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "7", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-008", "prompt": "A rectangle has an area of 72 square feet. Its length is 12 feet. What is its perimeter?", "choices": {"A": "18 feet", "B": "24 feet", "C": "36 feet", "D": "48 feet"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-009", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 15t − 5t²?", "choices": {"A": "5t(3 − t)", "B": "5t(t − 3)", "C": "10t²", "D": "20t − 5"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-010", "prompt": "The data set 8, 10, 10, 12, 15, 17 has a median of 11. A number is added to the data set, and the new median is 12. Which number could have been added?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "10", "C": "12", "D": "18"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-011", "prompt": "A rectangular poster has a width of 8 inches. Its length is 5 inches less than 3 times its width. What is the area of the poster?", "choices": {"A": "64 in²", "B": "104 in²", "C": "152 in²", "D": "192 in²"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-012", "prompt": "The value of y is 4 less than twice the value of x. If x = 9, what is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "14", "B": "18", "C": "22", "D": "32"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-013", "prompt": "A number k is divided by 5, and then 4 is added. The result is 13. What is the value of k?", "choices": {"A": "17", "B": "25", "C": "45", "D": "65"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-014", "prompt": "A backpack costs $48 before tax. The sales tax is 8% of the cost. What is the total cost of the backpack, including tax?", "choices": {"A": "$51.84", "B": "$52.00", "C": "$55.68", "D": "$56.00"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, the length of one leg is 9 centimeters and the length of the hypotenuse is 15 centimeters. What is the length of the other leg?", "choices": {"A": "6 centimeters", "B": "10 centimeters", "C": "12 centimeters", "D": "24 centimeters"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (x − 3)(x + 4)?", "choices": {"A": "x² + x − 12", "B": "x² − x − 12", "C": "x² + 7x + 12", "D": "x² − 7x + 12"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-017", "prompt": "The equation y = 14 − 2x gives the number of gallons of water y left in a tank after x minutes. What is the x-intercept of the graph of this equation?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "7", "C": "12", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-018", "prompt": "The table shows a linear relationship between x and y.\nx | −2 | 0 | 2 | 4\ny | 9 | 5 | 1 | −3\nWhich statement describes the relationship?", "choices": {"A": "y increases by 2 when x increases by 1.", "B": "y decreases by 2 when x increases by 1.", "C": "y increases by 4 when x increases by 1.", "D": "y decreases by 4 when x increases by 1."}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-019", "prompt": "A circle has a diameter of 18 inches. What is the circumference of the circle?", "choices": {"A": "9π inches", "B": "18π inches", "C": "36π inches", "D": "81π inches"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-020", "prompt": "A survey asked 50 students whether they prefer apples or oranges. Of the students, 32 prefer apples. If one of these students is chosen at random, what is the probability that the student prefers oranges?", "choices": {"A": "9/25", "B": "16/25", "C": "18/25", "D": "32/50"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-021", "prompt": "If 3a + 2b = 31 and b = 8, what is the value of a?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "7", "D": "15"}}, {"id": "MATH2-M2-022", "prompt": "The expression x² + 9x + 20 can be written as (x + a)(x + b), where a and b are positive integers. Which could be the values of a and b?", "choices": {"A": "1 and 20", "B": "2 and 10", "C": "4 and 5", "D": "6 and 3"}}], [{"id": "MATH3-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 6(x − 2) + 5 = 2x + 17?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-002", "prompt": "The function f is defined by f(x) = 12 − 2x. If f(k) = −4, what is the value of k?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "10"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-003", "prompt": "A concert hall has balcony seats and floor seats. There are 18 more floor seats than balcony seats. If there are 126 seats total, how many balcony seats are there?", "choices": {"A": "45", "B": "54", "C": "63", "D": "72"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-004", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to 3x² − 6x + 4x − 8?", "choices": {"A": "3x² − 2x − 8", "B": "3x² − 10x − 8", "C": "7x² − 2x − 8", "D": "7x² − 10x − 8"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-005", "prompt": "The table shows a linear relationship between x and y.\nx | 1 | 3 | 5 | 7\ny | 12 | 20 | 28 | 36\nWhat is the y-intercept of the graph of this relationship?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "8", "C": "12", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-006", "prompt": "A store sells two sizes of trail mix bags. A 10-ounce bag costs $3.20, and a 16-ounce bag costs $4.64. Which statement is true?", "choices": {"A": "The 10-ounce bag has the lower cost per ounce.", "B": "The 16-ounce bag has the lower cost per ounce.", "C": "Both bags have the same cost per ounce.", "D": "The 16-ounce bag costs twice as much per ounce as the 10-ounce bag."}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-007", "prompt": "A line passes through the points (−3, 2) and (1, 10). What is the slope of the line?", "choices": {"A": "1/2", "B": "2", "C": "4", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-008", "prompt": "A rectangle has a perimeter of 58 inches. Its length is 8 inches greater than its width. What is the width of the rectangle?", "choices": {"A": "10.5 inches", "B": "13 inches", "C": "18 inches", "D": "21 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 6p² + 9p can be written as 3p(ap + b), where a and b are integers. What is the value of a + b?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-010", "prompt": "A set of 5 numbers has a mean of 18. A sixth number is added, and the new mean is 20. What number was added?", "choices": {"A": "22", "B": "24", "C": "28", "D": "30"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = (x − 2)². Which value of x gives h(x) = 25?", "choices": {"A": "−7", "B": "−3", "C": "3", "D": "5"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-012", "prompt": "A school orders chairs in boxes. Each box contains the same number of chairs. The school orders 7 boxes and receives 84 chairs. Which equation represents the total number of chairs c the school receives when it orders b boxes?", "choices": {"A": "c = 7b", "B": "c = 12b", "C": "c = 84b", "D": "c = b + 84"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-013", "prompt": "A line has equation y = (1/2)x + 6. Which point lies on the line?", "choices": {"A": "(2, 5)", "B": "(4, 8)", "C": "(6, 12)", "D": "(8, 14)"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-014", "prompt": "A snack bar increases the price of a sandwich from $5.00 to $5.75. What is the percent increase?", "choices": {"A": "7.5%", "B": "10%", "C": "15%", "D": "20%"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, tan A = 3/4. Which could be the lengths of the side opposite angle A and the side adjacent to angle A?", "choices": {"A": "opposite: 3, adjacent: 4", "B": "opposite: 4, adjacent: 3", "C": "opposite: 3, adjacent: 5", "D": "opposite: 4, adjacent: 5"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-016", "prompt": "The expression x² − 2x − 15 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x − 5)(x + 3)", "B": "(x + 5)(x − 3)", "C": "(x − 15)(x + 1)", "D": "(x + 15)(x − 1)"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-017", "prompt": "A scatterplot shows the relationship between the number of hours students studied for a quiz and their quiz scores. The points generally rise from left to right. Which statement best describes the association?", "choices": {"A": "As study time increases, quiz score tends to decrease.", "B": "As study time increases, quiz score tends to increase.", "C": "Study time and quiz score have no possible relationship.", "D": "The quiz score is always equal to the study time."}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 9x − 4y = 20 is true for the ordered pair (4, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "4", "C": "6", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-019", "prompt": "A cone has a radius of 3 inches and a height of 8 inches. What is the volume of the cone? Use V = (1/3)πr²h.", "choices": {"A": "8π in³", "B": "24π in³", "C": "36π in³", "D": "72π in³"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-020", "prompt": "A password is made by choosing one letter from A, B, C, or D and then choosing one digit from 1, 2, or 3. How many different passwords are possible?", "choices": {"A": "7", "B": "9", "C": "12", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has no solution?", "choices": {"A": "y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x − 4", "B": "y = 2x + 1 and y = −2x + 1", "C": "y = 2x + 1 and y = x + 1", "D": "y = 2x + 1 and y = 3x − 4"}}, {"id": "MATH3-M2-022", "prompt": "The function q is defined by q(x) = x² − 8x + 16. What is q(6)?", "choices": {"A": "0", "B": "2", "C": "4", "D": "8"}}], [{"id": "MATH4-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 3(2x + 1) = 4x + 19?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "11"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-002", "prompt": "A linear function has a y-intercept of 7 and contains the point (3, 16). What is the slope of the function?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "7", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-003", "prompt": "At a school event, 68 students chose either a workshop or a tour. The number of students who chose the workshop was 14 fewer than twice the number of students who chose the tour. How many students chose the tour?", "choices": {"A": "22", "B": "26", "C": "30", "D": "36"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-004", "prompt": "The function g is defined by g(x) = 3x² − 2x + 5. What is g(−1)?", "choices": {"A": "0", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "10"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-005", "prompt": "A runner’s distance from the starting line, in meters, is modeled by d = 250 − 5t, where t is the time in seconds after the runner begins moving back toward the starting line. What is the distance from the starting line when t = 18?", "choices": {"A": "90 meters", "B": "160 meters", "C": "232 meters", "D": "340 meters"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the total cost for ordering different numbers of identical pens, including a one-time shipping fee.\nNumber of pens | 2 | 4 | 6 | 8\nTotal cost, in dollars | 11 | 17 | 23 | 29\nWhich equation gives the total cost c, in dollars, for ordering p pens?", "choices": {"A": "c = 3p + 5", "B": "c = 5p + 3", "C": "c = 6p − 1", "D": "c = 11p"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of n is a solution to 24 − 5n < 4?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "5"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-008", "prompt": "A rectangular field has a length of 24 meters. The width is 3/4 of the length. What is the perimeter of the field?", "choices": {"A": "42 meters", "B": "60 meters", "C": "84 meters", "D": "96 meters"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 8x² − 20x can be written as 4x(ax − b), where a and b are positive integers. What is the value of a + b?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "10", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-010", "prompt": "The ages of 6 students are 13, 14, 14, 15, 16, and 18. If the age 18 is removed from the data set, how does the median change?", "choices": {"A": "It decreases by 0.5.", "B": "It decreases by 1.", "C": "It increases by 0.5.", "D": "It stays the same."}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = x² − 10. If h(k) = 15 and k is positive, what is the value of k?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "15", "D": "25"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-012", "prompt": "A landscaping company charges a fixed fee plus an hourly fee. For 2 hours of work, the total charge is $90. For 5 hours of work, the total charge is $180. What is the fixed fee?", "choices": {"A": "$20", "B": "$30", "C": "$45", "D": "$60"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-013", "prompt": "Which point lies on the line represented by 2x + y = 11?", "choices": {"A": "(2, 5)", "B": "(3, 4)", "C": "(4, 2)", "D": "(5, 0)"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-014", "prompt": "A school has 320 students. The number of students in the art club is 12.5% of the total number of students. How many students are in the art club?", "choices": {"A": "24", "B": "32", "C": "40", "D": "48"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, sin A = 5/13. Which could be the lengths of the side opposite angle A and the hypotenuse?", "choices": {"A": "opposite: 5, hypotenuse: 12", "B": "opposite: 5, hypotenuse: 13", "C": "opposite: 12, hypotenuse: 13", "D": "opposite: 13, hypotenuse: 5"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (x + 6)(x − 2)?", "choices": {"A": "x² + 4x − 12", "B": "x² − 4x − 12", "C": "x² + 8x − 12", "D": "x² + 4x + 12"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-017", "prompt": "A line has equation y = 5 − x. Which statement about the graph is true?", "choices": {"A": "The slope is 5 and the y-intercept is −1.", "B": "The slope is 1 and the y-intercept is 5.", "C": "The slope is −1 and the y-intercept is 5.", "D": "The slope is −5 and the y-intercept is 1."}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 4x + 3y = 39 is true for the ordered pair (6, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "9", "D": "15"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-019", "prompt": "A sphere has a radius of 3 centimeters. What is the volume of the sphere? Use V = (4/3)πr³.", "choices": {"A": "12π cm³", "B": "27π cm³", "C": "36π cm³", "D": "108π cm³"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-020", "prompt": "A cafeteria offers 3 types of sandwiches and 4 types of drinks. A meal consists of 1 sandwich and 1 drink. How many different meals are possible?", "choices": {"A": "7", "B": "9", "C": "12", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has exactly one solution?", "choices": {"A": "y = 4x + 2 and y = 4x + 9", "B": "y = −3x + 5 and y = −3x − 1", "C": "y = 2x − 6 and y = −x + 3", "D": "y = x + 7 and y = x + 7"}}, {"id": "MATH4-M2-022", "prompt": "The quadratic expression x² − 12x + 36 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x − 6)²", "B": "(x + 6)²", "C": "(x − 12)²", "D": "(x − 3)(x − 12)"}}], [{"id": "MATH5-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 7x − 4 = 3(x + 8)?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "7", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-002", "prompt": "A line has a slope of −3 and passes through the point (2, 1). Which equation represents the line?", "choices": {"A": "y = −3x − 5", "B": "y = −3x + 7", "C": "y = 3x − 5", "D": "y = 3x + 7"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-003", "prompt": "A school bought boxes of markers and boxes of pencils. Each box of markers cost $9, and each box of pencils cost $4. The school bought 3 more boxes of pencils than boxes of markers and spent $71. How many boxes of markers did the school buy?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-004", "prompt": "The function p is defined by p(x) = 2x² + x − 6. What is p(−3)?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "6", "C": "9", "D": "15"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-005", "prompt": "A storage container holds 18 small bins. Each small bin holds 12 craft kits. If 5 bins are removed from the container, how many craft kits can the remaining bins hold?", "choices": {"A": "60", "B": "96", "C": "144", "D": "156"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the value of a savings account after different numbers of months.\nMonths | 0 | 2 | 4 | 6\nValue, in dollars | 50 | 62 | 74 | 86\nWhich equation gives the value v, in dollars, after m months?", "choices": {"A": "v = 6m + 50", "B": "v = 12m + 50", "C": "v = 50m + 6", "D": "v = 56m"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of k is NOT a solution to 2k + 5 > 17?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "9", "D": "11"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-008", "prompt": "A triangle has side lengths 9 inches, 12 inches, and 15 inches. Which statement is true?", "choices": {"A": "The triangle is a right triangle because 9² + 12² = 15².", "B": "The triangle is a right triangle because 9 + 12 = 15.", "C": "The triangle is not a right triangle because 9² + 12² < 15².", "D": "The triangle is not a right triangle because 9² + 12² > 15²."}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 10m² − 25m can be written as 5m(2m − k). What is the value of k?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "10", "D": "25"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-010", "prompt": "The table shows the number of messages sent by four students.\nStudent | A | B | C | D\nMessages | 14 | 22 | 18 | 26\nA fifth student sent 30 messages. What is the mean number of messages for all five students?", "choices": {"A": "20", "B": "21", "C": "22", "D": "24"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = (x + 1)² − 4. What is h(3)?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "12", "C": "16", "D": "20"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-012", "prompt": "The total cost C, in dollars, for renting a bike is C = 15 + 6h, where h is the number of hours. If the total cost is $51, for how many hours was the bike rented?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-013", "prompt": "Which ordered pair is a solution to the equation 3x − 2y = 12?", "choices": {"A": "(2, −3)", "B": "(4, 0)", "C": "(6, 2)", "D": "(8, 4)"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-014", "prompt": "A sweater is discounted by 20%, and the sale price is $36. What was the original price of the sweater?", "choices": {"A": "$40", "B": "$45", "C": "$48", "D": "$56"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, cos A = 8/17. Which could be the lengths of the side adjacent to angle A and the hypotenuse?", "choices": {"A": "adjacent: 8, hypotenuse: 15", "B": "adjacent: 8, hypotenuse: 17", "C": "adjacent: 15, hypotenuse: 17", "D": "adjacent: 17, hypotenuse: 8"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (x − 7)(x − 1)?", "choices": {"A": "x² − 8x + 7", "B": "x² − 6x + 7", "C": "x² + 8x + 7", "D": "x² + 6x − 7"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-017", "prompt": "A scatterplot compares the age of several used bicycles and their resale prices. The points generally fall from left to right. Which statement best describes the association?", "choices": {"A": "Older bicycles tend to have higher resale prices.", "B": "Older bicycles tend to have lower resale prices.", "C": "All bicycles have the same resale price.", "D": "Resale price causes the age of a bicycle to change."}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 5x − 2y = 28 is true for the ordered pair (8, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-019", "prompt": "A cylinder has a diameter of 10 centimeters and a height of 6 centimeters. What is the volume of the cylinder? Use V = πr²h.", "choices": {"A": "60π cm³", "B": "150π cm³", "C": "300π cm³", "D": "600π cm³"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-020", "prompt": "A drawer contains 5 blue socks, 4 black socks, and 3 white socks. If one sock is selected at random, what is the probability that it is black or white?", "choices": {"A": "1/3", "B": "5/12", "C": "7/12", "D": "3/4"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-021", "prompt": "A system of equations is shown.\n2x + 3y = 21\nx = 3\nWhat is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH5-M2-022", "prompt": "The expression x² + bx + 18 is equivalent to (x + 3)(x + 6). What is the value of b?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "6", "C": "9", "D": "18"}}], [{"id": "MATH6-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 4(x + 3) − 7 = 2x + 15?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "5", "D": "6"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-002", "prompt": "A line has equation y = mx + 2 and passes through the point (5, 17). What is the value of m?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "5"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-003", "prompt": "A theater sold child tickets and adult tickets. Child tickets cost $6 each, and adult tickets cost $10 each. The theater sold 45 tickets for a total of $350. How many adult tickets were sold?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "20", "C": "25", "D": "30"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-004", "prompt": "The function g is defined by g(x) = x² + 3x − 7. What is g(4)?", "choices": {"A": "9", "B": "15", "C": "21", "D": "35"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-005", "prompt": "A water tank contains 220 liters of water. Water is added at a constant rate of 12 liters per minute. Which equation gives the amount of water A, in liters, in the tank after t minutes?", "choices": {"A": "A = 12t − 220", "B": "A = 220t + 12", "C": "A = 220 + 12t", "D": "A = 232t"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the total cost of buying different numbers of identical notebooks.\nNumber of notebooks | 3 | 5 | 7 | 9\nTotal cost, in dollars | 10.50 | 17.50 | 24.50 | 31.50\nWhat is the cost of 12 notebooks?", "choices": {"A": "$35.00", "B": "$38.50", "C": "$42.00", "D": "$45.50"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of a makes 5a − 6 ≤ 19 true?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-008", "prompt": "A rectangular picture frame has an outside length of 18 inches and an outside width of 12 inches. The picture inside the frame has an area of 120 square inches. What is the area of the frame that surrounds the picture?", "choices": {"A": "96 square inches", "B": "108 square inches", "C": "120 square inches", "D": "216 square inches"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 12x² + 18x can be written as 6x(kx + 3). What is the value of k?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "6", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-010", "prompt": "The data set 7, 10, 12, 16, and 20 has a mean of 13. If the value 20 is replaced with 25, what is the new mean?", "choices": {"A": "13", "B": "14", "C": "15", "D": "18"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = (x − 4)² + 1. What is h(7)?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "8", "C": "10", "D": "15"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-012", "prompt": "A taxi company charges a starting fee plus a cost per mile. A 4-mile ride costs $15, and a 9-mile ride costs $27.50. What is the starting fee?", "choices": {"A": "$2.50", "B": "$5.00", "C": "$7.50", "D": "$10.00"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-013", "prompt": "Which ordered pair is a solution to both equations?\ny = 2x + 1\nx + y = 16", "choices": {"A": "(3, 7)", "B": "(4, 9)", "C": "(5, 11)", "D": "(6, 13)"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-014", "prompt": "A class has 24 students. Of the students, 3/8 are in the school band. How many students in the class are in the school band?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "9", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, one acute angle has a sine of 7/25. Which could be the lengths of the side opposite that angle and the hypotenuse?", "choices": {"A": "opposite: 7, hypotenuse: 24", "B": "opposite: 7, hypotenuse: 25", "C": "opposite: 24, hypotenuse: 25", "D": "opposite: 25, hypotenuse: 7"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (x + 8)(x − 5)?", "choices": {"A": "x² + 3x − 40", "B": "x² − 3x − 40", "C": "x² + 13x − 40", "D": "x² + 3x + 40"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-017", "prompt": "A line has equation y = −4x + 18. What is the x-intercept of the line?", "choices": {"A": "4.5", "B": "14", "C": "18", "D": "22.5"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 7x + 2y = 44 is true for the ordered pair (4, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "10"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-019", "prompt": "A cylinder has a radius of 4 inches and a volume of 96π cubic inches. What is the height of the cylinder? Use V = πr²h.", "choices": {"A": "4 inches", "B": "6 inches", "C": "8 inches", "D": "12 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-020", "prompt": "A bag contains 4 green beads, 5 purple beads, and 7 yellow beads. If one bead is selected at random, what is the probability that the bead is not yellow?", "choices": {"A": "7/16", "B": "9/16", "C": "11/16", "D": "12/16"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has infinitely many solutions?", "choices": {"A": "y = 3x − 2 and 2y = 6x − 4", "B": "y = 3x − 2 and y = 3x + 2", "C": "y = 3x − 2 and y = −3x − 2", "D": "y = 3x − 2 and y = x − 2"}}, {"id": "MATH6-M2-022", "prompt": "The expression x² − 49 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x − 7)(x + 7)", "B": "(x − 7)(x − 7)", "C": "(x + 7)(x + 7)", "D": "(x − 49)(x + 1)"}}], [{"id": "MATH7-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 5(x − 1) + 2 = 3x + 13?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "7", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-002", "prompt": "A line passes through the points (−2, 11) and (4, −1). Which equation represents the line?", "choices": {"A": "y = −2x + 7", "B": "y = −2x − 7", "C": "y = 2x + 7", "D": "y = 3x − 5"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-003", "prompt": "A school bought calculators and rulers. Each calculator cost $14, and each ruler cost $3. The school bought 8 more rulers than calculators and spent $151. How many calculators did the school buy?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "7", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-004", "prompt": "The function r is defined by r(x) = 4x² − 5x + 2. What is r(−2)?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "16", "C": "24", "D": "28"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-005", "prompt": "A candle is 18 centimeters tall before it is lit. After it is lit, its height decreases by 0.6 centimeter each hour. Which equation gives the candle’s height h, in centimeters, after t hours?", "choices": {"A": "h = 18 + 0.6t", "B": "h = 18 − 0.6t", "C": "h = 0.6 − 18t", "D": "h = 18t − 0.6"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the total cost of buying different numbers of identical water bottles, including a one-time bag fee.\nNumber of bottles | 2 | 5 | 8 | 11\nTotal cost, in dollars | 8 | 17 | 26 | 35\nWhat is the one-time bag fee?", "choices": {"A": "$1", "B": "$2", "C": "$3", "D": "$6"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of n is NOT a solution to 18 − 3n ≤ 6?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "5", "D": "6"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-008", "prompt": "A right triangle has one leg with length 5 inches and a hypotenuse with length 13 inches. What is the area of the triangle?", "choices": {"A": "30 square inches", "B": "32.5 square inches", "C": "60 square inches", "D": "65 square inches"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 14a² − 21a can be written as 7a(ka − 3). What is the value of k?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "7", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-010", "prompt": "The numbers 18, 22, 24, 28, and 33 have a mean of 25. If 18 is removed from the data set, what is the mean of the remaining numbers?", "choices": {"A": "24", "B": "25", "C": "26", "D": "27"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = 2(x − 1)². Which value of x gives h(x) = 18?", "choices": {"A": "−2", "B": "−1", "C": "2", "D": "4"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-012", "prompt": "A linear model gives the total cost C, in dollars, of buying x identical shirts from a custom-print shop. The cost is $31 for 2 shirts and $76 for 7 shirts. What is the cost of 1 shirt after the fixed fee is paid?", "choices": {"A": "$7", "B": "$9", "C": "$11", "D": "$14"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-013", "prompt": "Which ordered pair is a solution to both equations?\n3x + y = 19\ny = x + 3", "choices": {"A": "(3, 6)", "B": "(4, 7)", "C": "(5, 8)", "D": "(6, 9)"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-014", "prompt": "A garden has 480 plants. Of the plants, 35% are flowers, 25% are vegetables, and the rest are herbs. How many plants are herbs?", "choices": {"A": "120", "B": "168", "C": "192", "D": "288"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, tan A = 5/12. Which could be the lengths of the side opposite angle A and the side adjacent to angle A?", "choices": {"A": "opposite: 5, adjacent: 12", "B": "opposite: 12, adjacent: 5", "C": "opposite: 5, adjacent: 13", "D": "opposite: 12, adjacent: 13"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (2x + 3)(x − 4)?", "choices": {"A": "2x² − 5x − 12", "B": "2x² + 5x − 12", "C": "2x² − 8x + 3", "D": "2x² − 11x − 12"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-017", "prompt": "A scatterplot compares the number of minutes students spent practicing free throws and the number of free throws they made during a test. The points show a positive association. Which conclusion is best supported?", "choices": {"A": "Students who practiced more tended to make more free throws.", "B": "Students who practiced more always made fewer free throws.", "C": "Practice time and free throws made had no relationship in the data.", "D": "Every student made exactly one free throw per minute practiced."}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 8x − 3y = 14 is true for the ordered pair (4, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-019", "prompt": "A cone has a diameter of 12 centimeters and a height of 9 centimeters. What is the volume of the cone? Use V = (1/3)πr²h.", "choices": {"A": "36π cm³", "B": "72π cm³", "C": "108π cm³", "D": "324π cm³"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-020", "prompt": "A code is made by choosing 1 color from 5 colors and 1 shape from 6 shapes. How many different codes are possible?", "choices": {"A": "11", "B": "20", "C": "30", "D": "60"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has no solution?", "choices": {"A": "y = −x + 4 and y = x + 4", "B": "y = 5x − 2 and y = 5x + 6", "C": "y = 2x + 3 and y = −2x + 3", "D": "y = x − 1 and y = 3x + 5"}}, {"id": "MATH7-M2-022", "prompt": "The quadratic expression x² + 2x − 35 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x + 7)(x − 5)", "B": "(x − 7)(x + 5)", "C": "(x + 35)(x − 1)", "D": "(x − 35)(x + 1)"}}], [{"id": "MATH8-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 2(3x − 4) + 5 = 4x + 15?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "7", "C": "8", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-002", "prompt": "A line passes through the points (1, −2) and (5, 10). Which equation represents the line?", "choices": {"A": "y = 2x − 4", "B": "y = 3x − 5", "C": "y = 3x + 5", "D": "y = 4x − 6"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-003", "prompt": "A school store sold pens and folders. Pens cost $2 each, and folders cost $5 each. The store sold 34 items and collected $116. How many folders were sold?", "choices": {"A": "12", "B": "14", "C": "16", "D": "18"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-004", "prompt": "The function g is defined by g(x) = 5x² − 2x − 1. What is g(−1)?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "4", "C": "6", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-005", "prompt": "A hiking trail is 9.6 miles long. A hiker has already walked 3.4 miles and then walks at a constant rate of 1.2 miles per hour. Which equation gives the number of miles m left on the trail after h more hours of walking?", "choices": {"A": "m = 9.6 − 1.2h", "B": "m = 6.2 − 1.2h", "C": "m = 6.2 + 1.2h", "D": "m = 3.4 + 1.2h"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the total charge for printing different numbers of posters, including a one-time design fee.\nNumber of posters | 4 | 8 | 12 | 16\nTotal charge, in dollars | 42 | 74 | 106 | 138\nWhat is the one-time design fee?", "choices": {"A": "$8", "B": "$10", "C": "$12", "D": "$16"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of t makes 7 − 2t > −9 true?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "9", "D": "10"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-008", "prompt": "A right triangle has legs with lengths 7 centimeters and 24 centimeters. What is the area of the triangle?", "choices": {"A": "31 cm²", "B": "84 cm²", "C": "168 cm²", "D": "625 cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 9y² − 15y can be written as 3y(ay − b), where a and b are positive integers. What is the value of a + b?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-010", "prompt": "The data set 11, 12, 15, 18, 19, 21 has a median of 16.5. If 11 is removed from the data set, what is the new median?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "18", "C": "18.5", "D": "19"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = (x + 2)² + 3. What is h(1)?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "10", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-012", "prompt": "A fitness class charges a registration fee and a cost per session. The total cost is $54 for 4 sessions and $90 for 8 sessions. What is the cost per session?", "choices": {"A": "$6", "B": "$9", "C": "$12", "D": "$15"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-013", "prompt": "Which ordered pair is a solution to both equations?\nx − y = 5\n2x + y = 22", "choices": {"A": "(7, 2)", "B": "(8, 3)", "C": "(9, 4)", "D": "(10, 5)"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-014", "prompt": "A jacket is discounted by 30%. The sale price is $49. What was the original price of the jacket?", "choices": {"A": "$63", "B": "$70", "C": "$79", "D": "$84"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, sin B = 9/15. Which ratio is equivalent to sin B in simplest form?", "choices": {"A": "1/3", "B": "2/5", "C": "3/5", "D": "5/3"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (3x − 2)(x + 5)?", "choices": {"A": "3x² + 13x − 10", "B": "3x² − 13x − 10", "C": "3x² + 15x − 2", "D": "3x² + 17x + 10"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-017", "prompt": "A scatterplot compares the outside temperature and the number of hot drinks sold at a café. The points generally fall from left to right. Which statement is best supported by the scatterplot?", "choices": {"A": "Higher temperatures tend to be associated with fewer hot drinks sold.", "B": "Higher temperatures tend to be associated with more hot drinks sold.", "C": "The number of hot drinks sold is always equal to the temperature.", "D": "Temperature has no possible association with hot drink sales."}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 6x + 5y = 47 is true for the ordered pair (2, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "9", "D": "11"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-019", "prompt": "A rectangular prism has a volume of 180 cubic centimeters. Its length is 9 centimeters, and its width is 5 centimeters. What is its height?", "choices": {"A": "4 centimeters", "B": "6 centimeters", "C": "9 centimeters", "D": "20 centimeters"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-020", "prompt": "A box contains 6 plain pencils, 5 striped pencils, and 9 dotted pencils. If one pencil is selected at random, what is the probability that the pencil is either plain or striped?", "choices": {"A": "5/20", "B": "6/20", "C": "11/20", "D": "15/20"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has infinitely many solutions?", "choices": {"A": "y = 2x + 6 and y = 2x − 6", "B": "y = −x + 4 and 2y = −2x + 8", "C": "y = 3x − 1 and y = −3x + 1", "D": "y = x + 5 and y = 5x + 1"}}, {"id": "MATH8-M2-022", "prompt": "The expression x² − 4x − 21 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x − 7)(x + 3)", "B": "(x + 7)(x − 3)", "C": "(x − 21)(x + 1)", "D": "(x + 21)(x − 1)"}}], [{"id": "MATH9-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 8 + 3(x − 2) = 2x + 19?", "choices": {"A": "11", "B": "13", "C": "15", "D": "17"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-002", "prompt": "A line contains the point (−1, 8) and has a slope of −4. Which equation represents the line?", "choices": {"A": "y = −4x + 4", "B": "y = −4x − 4", "C": "y = 4x + 12", "D": "y = 4x − 12"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-003", "prompt": "A theater has 96 seats in two sections. The front section has 12 fewer seats than the back section. How many seats are in the front section?", "choices": {"A": "36", "B": "42", "C": "48", "D": "54"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-004", "prompt": "The function q is defined by q(x) = 3x² + 2x − 4. What is q(−2)?", "choices": {"A": "0", "B": "4", "C": "8", "D": "12"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-005", "prompt": "A pool contains 1,250 gallons of water. Water is drained at a constant rate of 75 gallons per hour. Which equation gives the amount of water W, in gallons, remaining after t hours?", "choices": {"A": "W = 1,250 − 75t", "B": "W = 1,250 + 75t", "C": "W = 75 − 1,250t", "D": "W = 75t − 1,250"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the total cost of buying different numbers of identical notebooks, including a fixed delivery fee.\nNumber of notebooks | 4 | 7 | 10 | 13\nTotal cost, in dollars | 19 | 28 | 37 | 46\nWhat is the fixed delivery fee?", "choices": {"A": "$3", "B": "$5", "C": "$7", "D": "$9"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of r is NOT a solution to 4r + 7 ≥ 31?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "7", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-008", "prompt": "A rectangle has a length of 16 centimeters. Its width is 5 centimeters less than its length. What is the area of the rectangle?", "choices": {"A": "42 cm²", "B": "88 cm²", "C": "176 cm²", "D": "336 cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 18n² − 12n can be written as 6n(an − b), where a and b are positive integers. What is the value of a − b?", "choices": {"A": "1", "B": "2", "C": "3", "D": "5"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-010", "prompt": "The data set 9, 14, 16, 20, and 26 has a mean of 17. If 9 and 26 are both removed, what is the mean of the remaining numbers?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "16", "C": "50/3", "D": "17"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = (x − 3)² − 2. What is h(8)?", "choices": {"A": "21", "B": "23", "C": "25", "D": "27"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-012", "prompt": "A tutoring service charges a signup fee and an hourly fee. The total cost is $52 for 3 hours and $88 for 7 hours. What is the signup fee?", "choices": {"A": "$9", "B": "$16", "C": "$25", "D": "$36"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-013", "prompt": "Which ordered pair is a solution to both equations?\n2x − y = 1\nx + 2y = 18", "choices": {"A": "(2, 3)", "B": "(3, 5)", "C": "(4, 7)", "D": "(5, 9)"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-014", "prompt": "A tablet is discounted by 15%. If the original price is $240, what is the sale price?", "choices": {"A": "$36", "B": "$204", "C": "$225", "D": "$276"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, cos A = 12/13. Which ratio is the value of sin A if the side lengths form a 5-12-13 triangle?", "choices": {"A": "5/13", "B": "12/13", "C": "5/12", "D": "13/12"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (2x − 5)(x + 6)?", "choices": {"A": "2x² + 7x − 30", "B": "2x² − 7x − 30", "C": "2x² + 12x − 5", "D": "2x² − 17x − 30"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-017", "prompt": "A scatterplot compares the number of hours a plant receives sunlight each day and the plant’s weekly growth. The points generally rise from left to right, but they are not exactly on a line. Which statement is best supported?", "choices": {"A": "More sunlight tends to be associated with more weekly growth.", "B": "More sunlight tends to be associated with less weekly growth.", "C": "Every plant grows the same amount each week.", "D": "Sunlight and weekly growth have no association in the data."}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 3x + 4y = 38 is true for the ordered pair (6, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "5", "C": "6", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-019", "prompt": "A cone has a volume of 48π cubic inches and a radius of 4 inches. What is the height of the cone? Use V = (1/3)πr²h.", "choices": {"A": "6 inches", "B": "9 inches", "C": "12 inches", "D": "16 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-020", "prompt": "A teacher has 5 fiction books, 6 nonfiction books, and 4 poetry books on a shelf. If one book is chosen at random, what is the probability that the book is not poetry?", "choices": {"A": "4/15", "B": "5/15", "C": "9/15", "D": "11/15"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has exactly one solution?", "choices": {"A": "y = −2x + 8 and y = −2x − 3", "B": "y = 4x + 1 and 2y = 8x + 2", "C": "y = x − 5 and y = x + 5", "D": "y = 3x − 7 and y = −x + 9"}}, {"id": "MATH9-M2-022", "prompt": "The expression x² + 6x + 8 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x + 2)(x + 4)", "B": "(x − 2)(x − 4)", "C": "(x + 1)(x + 8)", "D": "(x − 1)(x − 8)"}}], [{"id": "MATH10-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 5 − 2(3x − 4) = 31?", "choices": {"A": "−5", "B": "−3", "C": "3", "D": "5"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-002", "prompt": "A line has equation y = mx − 6 and passes through the point (4, 10). What is the value of m?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "3", "C": "4", "D": "6"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-003", "prompt": "A bookstore sold paperback books and hardcover books. Paperback books cost $7 each, and hardcover books cost $13 each. The bookstore sold 28 books for a total of $280. How many hardcover books were sold?", "choices": {"A": "10", "B": "12", "C": "14", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-004", "prompt": "The function g is defined by g(x) = 2x² − 5x + 3. What is g(5)?", "choices": {"A": "18", "B": "23", "C": "28", "D": "33"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-005", "prompt": "A tank contains 540 liters of water. A pump removes water at a constant rate of 18 liters per minute. Which equation gives the number of liters L left in the tank after t minutes?", "choices": {"A": "L = 18t + 540", "B": "L = 540 − 18t", "C": "L = 540t − 18", "D": "L = 18 − 540t"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the total cost of buying different numbers of identical keychains, including a fixed packaging fee.\nNumber of keychains | 3 | 6 | 9 | 12\nTotal cost, in dollars | 15 | 24 | 33 | 42\nWhat is the fixed packaging fee?", "choices": {"A": "$3", "B": "$6", "C": "$9", "D": "$12"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of b is NOT a solution to 3b − 11 < 10?", "choices": {"A": "2", "B": "4", "C": "6", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-008", "prompt": "A rectangle has a width of 14 meters. Its length is 6 meters more than half its width. What is the perimeter of the rectangle?", "choices": {"A": "27 meters", "B": "40 meters", "C": "54 meters", "D": "70 meters"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 16r² − 24r can be written as 8r(ar − b), where a and b are positive integers. What is the value of a + b?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "8", "D": "10"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-010", "prompt": "The data set 12, 15, 18, 19, 21, and 23 has a median of 18.5. If 12 and 23 are removed, what is the median of the remaining data set?", "choices": {"A": "17", "B": "18.5", "C": "19", "D": "20"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = (x + 4)² − 9. What is h(−1)?", "choices": {"A": "0", "B": "3", "C": "8", "D": "16"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-012", "prompt": "A music school charges a registration fee and a weekly lesson fee. The total cost is $115 for 5 weeks of lessons and $175 for 9 weeks of lessons. What is the registration fee?", "choices": {"A": "$15", "B": "$25", "C": "$40", "D": "$60"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-013", "prompt": "Which ordered pair is a solution to both equations?\n4x − y = 11\nx + y = 19", "choices": {"A": "(5, 14)", "B": "(6, 13)", "C": "(7, 12)", "D": "(8, 11)"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-014", "prompt": "A tablet’s battery level is 84%. After one hour of use, the battery level is 63%. By what percent of the original battery level did the battery level decrease?", "choices": {"A": "21%", "B": "25%", "C": "33%", "D": "63%"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, sin A = 8/17. Which could be the lengths of the side opposite angle A and the hypotenuse?", "choices": {"A": "opposite: 8, hypotenuse: 15", "B": "opposite: 8, hypotenuse: 17", "C": "opposite: 15, hypotenuse: 17", "D": "opposite: 17, hypotenuse: 8"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (4x + 1)(x − 3)?", "choices": {"A": "4x² − 11x − 3", "B": "4x² + 11x − 3", "C": "4x² − 12x + 1", "D": "4x² − 13x − 3"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-017", "prompt": "A scatterplot compares the number of practice problems students completed and the number of errors they made on a review quiz. The points generally fall from left to right. Which conclusion is best supported?", "choices": {"A": "Students who completed more practice problems tended to make fewer errors.", "B": "Students who completed more practice problems tended to make more errors.", "C": "Every student made the same number of errors.", "D": "The number of practice problems completed had no association with errors."}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 9x + 5y = 62 is true for the ordered pair (3, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "7", "C": "9", "D": "11"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-019", "prompt": "A rectangular prism has a length of 12 inches, a width of 5 inches, and a surface area of 340 square inches. What is the height of the prism? Use S = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh.", "choices": {"A": "4 inches", "B": "5 inches", "C": "6 inches", "D": "7 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-020", "prompt": "A bag contains 6 red clips, 8 silver clips, and 10 gold clips. If one clip is selected at random, what is the probability that the clip is silver or gold?", "choices": {"A": "1/3", "B": "3/4", "C": "7/12", "D": "18/24"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has no solution?", "choices": {"A": "y = −4x + 1 and y = −4x + 6", "B": "y = 2x − 5 and y = −2x − 5", "C": "y = x + 8 and y = 3x − 2", "D": "y = −x + 4 and y = −x + 4"}}, {"id": "MATH10-M2-022", "prompt": "The expression x² − 10x + 21 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x − 3)(x − 7)", "B": "(x + 3)(x + 7)", "C": "(x − 1)(x − 21)", "D": "(x + 1)(x + 21)"}}], [{"id": "MATH11-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 4(2x − 3) − 5 = 3x + 18?", "choices": {"A": "5", "B": "6", "C": "7", "D": "8"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-002", "prompt": "A line is represented by the equation 3x + y = 14. Which equation represents the same line in slope-intercept form?", "choices": {"A": "y = 3x + 14", "B": "y = −3x + 14", "C": "y = (1/3)x + 14", "D": "y = −(1/3)x + 14"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-003", "prompt": "A museum sold child tickets and adult tickets. Child tickets cost $4 each, and adult tickets cost $9 each. A total of 52 tickets were sold for $328. How many adult tickets were sold?", "choices": {"A": "20", "B": "24", "C": "28", "D": "32"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-004", "prompt": "The function p is defined by p(x) = −x² + 6x + 4. What is p(2)?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "10", "C": "12", "D": "14"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-005", "prompt": "A phone has 96 gigabytes of storage available before any videos are downloaded. Each video uses 1.8 gigabytes of storage. Which equation gives the amount of storage S, in gigabytes, available after v videos are downloaded?", "choices": {"A": "S = 96 + 1.8v", "B": "S = 96 − 1.8v", "C": "S = 1.8 − 96v", "D": "S = 1.8v − 96"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the total charge for renting different numbers of chairs, including a fixed setup fee.\nNumber of chairs | 10 | 20 | 30 | 40\nTotal charge, in dollars | 45 | 75 | 105 | 135\nWhat is the fixed setup fee?", "choices": {"A": "$10", "B": "$12", "C": "$15", "D": "$30"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of n is a solution to 5 − 4n ≥ −23?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "7", "C": "8", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-008", "prompt": "A square and an equilateral triangle have the same perimeter. The square has side length 9 inches. What is the side length of the equilateral triangle?", "choices": {"A": "9 inches", "B": "10 inches", "C": "12 inches", "D": "18 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 20x² + 35x can be written as 5x(ax + b), where a and b are positive integers. What is the value of b − a?", "choices": {"A": "1", "B": "2", "C": "3", "D": "4"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-010", "prompt": "The data set 6, 8, 10, 12, and 14 has a mean of 10. If a number is added and the new mean is 11, what number was added?", "choices": {"A": "14", "B": "15", "C": "16", "D": "17"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = (x − 5)². Which value of x gives h(x) = 16?", "choices": {"A": "−1", "B": "1", "C": "4", "D": "9"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-012", "prompt": "A club pays a one-time room fee and a cost for each person attending a meeting. The total cost is $70 for 10 people and $106 for 16 people. What is the one-time room fee?", "choices": {"A": "$6", "B": "$10", "C": "$16", "D": "$20"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-013", "prompt": "Which ordered pair is a solution to both equations?\ny = 3x − 2\n2x + y = 23", "choices": {"A": "(4, 10)", "B": "(5, 13)", "C": "(6, 16)", "D": "(7, 19)"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-014", "prompt": "A bicycle helmet is discounted by 25%. The sale price is $33. What was the original price of the helmet?", "choices": {"A": "$39", "B": "$42", "C": "$44", "D": "$48"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, tan A = 8/15. Which could be the lengths of the side opposite angle A and the side adjacent to angle A?", "choices": {"A": "opposite: 8, adjacent: 15", "B": "opposite: 15, adjacent: 8", "C": "opposite: 8, adjacent: 17", "D": "opposite: 15, adjacent: 17"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (3x + 4)(2x − 1)?", "choices": {"A": "6x² + 5x − 4", "B": "6x² − 5x − 4", "C": "6x² + 8x − 1", "D": "6x² + 11x − 4"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-017", "prompt": "A scatterplot compares the number of hours a battery has been used and the percent of battery charge remaining. The points generally fall from left to right. Which statement is best supported?", "choices": {"A": "Batteries used longer tended to have less charge remaining.", "B": "Batteries used longer tended to have more charge remaining.", "C": "Battery charge was always equal to hours used.", "D": "There was no association between hours used and charge remaining."}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 11x − 2y = 37 is true for the ordered pair (5, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "6", "B": "8", "C": "9", "D": "11"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-019", "prompt": "A cylinder has a height of 9 inches and a volume of 225π cubic inches. What is the radius of the cylinder? Use V = πr²h.", "choices": {"A": "4 inches", "B": "5 inches", "C": "6 inches", "D": "15 inches"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-020", "prompt": "A box contains 12 markers. Of the markers, 5 are blue, 3 are red, and the rest are black. If one marker is selected at random, what is the probability that it is black?", "choices": {"A": "1/4", "B": "1/3", "C": "5/12", "D": "2/3"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has infinitely many solutions?", "choices": {"A": "y = 5x − 4 and y = 5x + 4", "B": "y = −2x + 9 and 3y = −6x + 27", "C": "y = x − 1 and y = −x + 1", "D": "y = 4x + 2 and y = 2x + 4"}}, {"id": "MATH11-M2-022", "prompt": "The expression x² − x − 42 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x − 7)(x + 6)", "B": "(x + 7)(x − 6)", "C": "(x − 42)(x + 1)", "D": "(x + 42)(x − 1)"}}], [{"id": "MATH12-M2-001", "prompt": "What is the solution to 3(2x + 5) − 4 = 5x + 19?", "choices": {"A": "4", "B": "6", "C": "8", "D": "10"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-002", "prompt": "A line is represented by the equation 2x − 5y = 20. Which equation represents the same line in slope-intercept form?", "choices": {"A": "y = (2/5)x − 4", "B": "y = −(2/5)x + 4", "C": "y = 2x − 4", "D": "y = −2x + 20"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-003", "prompt": "A camp bought tents and sleeping bags. Each tent cost $24, and each sleeping bag cost $15. The camp bought 18 items for a total of $351. How many tents did the camp buy?", "choices": {"A": "8", "B": "9", "C": "10", "D": "11"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-004", "prompt": "The function g is defined by g(x) = −2x² + 5x + 7. What is g(−3)?", "choices": {"A": "−26", "B": "−20", "C": "−8", "D": "10"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-005", "prompt": "A 64-ounce container of soap is full. A dispenser uses 2.5 ounces of soap each time it is pressed. Which equation gives the number of ounces s of soap left after p presses?", "choices": {"A": "s = 64 + 2.5p", "B": "s = 64 − 2.5p", "C": "s = 2.5 − 64p", "D": "s = 2.5p − 64"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-006", "prompt": "The table shows the total charge for buying different numbers of identical candles, including a fixed gift-box fee.\nNumber of candles | 2 | 5 | 8 | 11\nTotal charge, in dollars | 19 | 37 | 55 | 73\nWhat is the fixed gift-box fee?", "choices": {"A": "$5", "B": "$6", "C": "$7", "D": "$9"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-007", "prompt": "Which value of k is NOT a solution to 28 − 3k > 10?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "5", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-008", "prompt": "A rectangle has a length of 20 inches. Its width is 40% of its length. What is the area of the rectangle?", "choices": {"A": "80 in²", "B": "120 in²", "C": "160 in²", "D": "200 in²"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-009", "prompt": "The expression 21m² + 14m can be written as 7m(am + b), where a and b are positive integers. What is the value of a + b?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "5", "D": "7"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-010", "prompt": "The data set 8, 12, 15, 17, and 23 has a mean of 15. If 8 is replaced by 18, what is the new mean?", "choices": {"A": "15", "B": "16", "C": "17", "D": "18"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-011", "prompt": "The function h is defined by h(x) = (x + 6)² − 5. What is h(−2)?", "choices": {"A": "7", "B": "11", "C": "16", "D": "21"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-012", "prompt": "A repair company charges a fixed service fee and an hourly labor charge. A 2-hour repair costs $82, and a 5-hour repair costs $190. What is the fixed service fee?", "choices": {"A": "$10", "B": "$18", "C": "$24", "D": "$36"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-013", "prompt": "Which ordered pair is a solution to both equations?\n2x + y = 20\ny = 14 − x", "choices": {"A": "(4, 10)", "B": "(5, 9)", "C": "(6, 8)", "D": "(7, 7)"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-014", "prompt": "A pair of headphones has an original price of $80. The sale price is $68. What is the percent decrease from the original price?", "choices": {"A": "12%", "B": "15%", "C": "20%", "D": "85%"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-015", "prompt": "In a right triangle, cos A = 9/15. Which ratio is equivalent to cos A in simplest form?", "choices": {"A": "2/3", "B": "3/5", "C": "5/3", "D": "9/6"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-016", "prompt": "Which expression is equivalent to (5x − 2)(x + 4)?", "choices": {"A": "5x² + 18x − 8", "B": "5x² − 18x − 8", "C": "5x² + 20x − 2", "D": "5x² + 22x + 8"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-017", "prompt": "A scatterplot compares the number of days after a new app was released and the total number of downloads. The points generally rise from left to right. Which statement is best supported?", "choices": {"A": "As more days passed, the total number of downloads tended to increase.", "B": "As more days passed, the total number of downloads tended to decrease.", "C": "The total number of downloads was always the same.", "D": "The number of days caused each download to take exactly one minute."}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-018", "prompt": "The equation 5x + 6y = 59 is true for the ordered pair (7, y). What is the value of y?", "choices": {"A": "3", "B": "4", "C": "5", "D": "6"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-019", "prompt": "A cylinder has a radius of 6 centimeters and a height of 5 centimeters. What is the surface area of the cylinder? Use S = 2πr² + 2πrh.", "choices": {"A": "66π cm²", "B": "96π cm²", "C": "132π cm²", "D": "180π cm²"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-020", "prompt": "A box contains 9 red cards, 6 black cards, and 5 white cards. If one card is selected at random, what is the probability that it is not red?", "choices": {"A": "9/20", "B": "11/20", "C": "14/20", "D": "15/20"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-021", "prompt": "Which system of equations has exactly one solution?", "choices": {"A": "y = 6x − 5 and y = 6x + 1", "B": "y = −x + 8 and 3y = −3x + 24", "C": "y = 2x + 4 and y = −3x + 9", "D": "y = 4x − 7 and 2y = 8x − 14"}}, {"id": "MATH12-M2-022", "prompt": "The expression x² + 5x − 24 is equivalent to which expression?", "choices": {"A": "(x + 8)(x − 3)", "B": "(x − 8)(x + 3)", "C": "(x + 6)(x − 4)", "D": "(x − 6)(x + 4)"}}]]}];
